File: org-agents.info,  Node: Summary,  Next: What an agent is,  Up: Introduction

1.1 Summary
===========

org-agents provides _agents_: ordinary Org entries that carry a query in
an ‘:AGENT_QUERY:’ property and, when you update them, populate
themselves with links back to every entry in your corpus that the query
matches.  An agent renders its matches as child headings, as a plain
list, or as a table.  Nothing else about the entry is special.  It is a
heading like any other, in a file like any other, and you fold it,
annotate it, link to it, export it and commit it exactly as you would
anything else you wrote.

   The idea, and the word, come from Eastgate Systems' Tinderbox, where
agents have been in use since 2002.  So do prototypes, action code and
the typed attribute model that the rest of this package implements.  See
*note Agents in the Tinderbox tradition:: for what those ideas are and
what became of each one here, and *note History and Acknowledgments::
for the attribution they are owed.

   Beyond agents themselves, org-agents implements the remainder of that
model over Org outlines:

a corpus-wide attribute registry
     One optional file declares your own properties, giving each a type,
     a vocabulary, a default and documentation, and a command lints the
     corpus against those declarations.  See *note The attribute
     registry::.
prototypes
     Any entry may read its properties through a _master_ entry it
     names, resolved when a value is read rather than copied into the
     follower.  Change the master and every follower changes with it.
     See *note Prototypes::.
appearance from attribute values
     A declared attribute may map its values to Emacs faces, and a minor
     mode draws each headline in the face its resolved value names.  It
     writes nothing: no text, no property, no overlay.  See *note
     Appearance::.
action code
     An agent may carry not only a query but a small declarative
     sentence saying what to _do_ to the entries the query matched --
     applied only by an explicit command, and only after a dry run you
     have read.  See *note Action code::.

   The whole package is one file, ‘org-agents.el’.  It establishes no
key bindings; every command below is reached with ‘M-x’ until you bind
it yourself.


