1.2 What an agent is
====================

Suppose your corpus records, on the entries that need it, a
‘NEXT_REVIEW’ property saying when you next mean to look at something.
The entries carrying it are scattered across projects, errands and
reading lists, and the set changes weekly.  You can already find them:
Org's agenda will match them, and ‘org-ql-search’ will match them more
precisely.  What you cannot do is _keep_ the answer.  A search result
lives in a transient buffer.  You cannot annotate one of its rows, or
link to it, or fold it away beside the project it belongs to, or read it
with ‘grep’, or commit it and see in next month's diff what changed.

   An agent keeps the answer.  Write a heading whose drawer holds a
query:

     * Up for review
       :PROPERTIES:
       :AGENT_QUERY: (and (todo "TODO") (property "NEXT_REVIEW"))
       :END:

   Then, with point anywhere inside that entry, type ‘M-x
org-agents-update’.  The echo area says ‘org-agents: 3 matches’, and the
entry now reads:

     * Up for review
       :PROPERTIES:
       :AGENT_QUERY: (and (todo "TODO") (property "NEXT_REVIEW"))
       :AGENT_MATCHED: 3 [2026-08-20 Thu 17:47]
       :END:
     ** [[id:rewrite-exporter][Rewrite the exporter]]
     :PROPERTIES:
     :AGENT_MATCH: t
     :END:
     ** [[id:audit-prefilter][Audit the prefilter]]
     :PROPERTIES:
     :AGENT_MATCH: t
     :END:
     ** [[id:renew-passport][Renew the passport]]
     :PROPERTIES:
     :AGENT_MATCH: t
     :END:

   Each match has become a child heading holding a link to it -- an
_alias_, in Tinderbox's word -- marked ‘:AGENT_MATCH: t’ so that the
next update knows it wrote it.  The agent also records what it found and
when, in ‘:AGENT_MATCHED:’.  From here the answer is Org text: you can
fold the agent shut, follow a link, write a paragraph under one of the
aliases explaining why it matters, and export or commit the lot.
Writing under an alias also changes what the next update may do to it,
which is worth knowing before you rely on it; see *note The alias
contract::.

   The same three matches can be rendered as a list or as a table inside
an ‘org-agents’ dynamic block instead.  See *note Views::.

   One more fact belongs here rather than four chapters later, because a
reader who misunderstands it will misunderstand everything downstream.

   *Evaluation is always performed by org-ql, against live Org buffers.*
There is exactly one evaluation engine and therefore exactly one answer.
A query is an org-ql S-expression, extended with a compact ‘$PROP’
reference layer that this package expands before org-ql sees it (see
*note The query language::).  Whatever the scope, whatever the view, the
entries an agent reports are the entries org-ql matched in a real
buffer.

   ripgrep appears nowhere in that sentence.  It is an _optional
candidate-FILE prefilter_: for a scope that names a directory rather
than a list of files, org-agents may run ripgrep first to work out which
files are worth opening at all, and then org-ql opens only those.  A
prefilter can therefore only narrow the set of files org-ql reads.  It
can never change what matches, and it can never make something match
that org-ql would not have matched.  Narrowing too little is merely
slow; narrowing too much would be a wrong answer with no error, so only
conditions whose ripgrep answer is provably a superset of org-ql's are
ever pushed into it.  Where ripgrep is absent, or where a query offers
nothing to push, the scope is read live instead -- slower, and identical
in what it finds.  See *note Scope and the ripgrep prefilter::.


