1.3 What you need
=================

Emacs 29.1 or later
     Declared in the package header, and the floor the code is written
     to.
org-ql 0.8 or later
   The query engine.  ‘org-ql’ is required at load time; ‘org-ql-search’
is required by ‘org-agents-preview’, the one command that uses it.  Both
ship in the same package.
Org
     Bundled with Emacs.  The package was developed against Org 9.8.7.
     No lower bound has been established by testing and none is
     declared.
‘org-ql-ext’ and ‘org-ext’
   Two single-file modules from the author's Emacs configuration.
‘org-agents’ requires ‘org-ql-ext’, which requires ‘org-ext’; the
‘property-ts’ predicate that several examples in this manual lean on
comes from the first of them.  Neither is part of the org-agents
repository, and neither is on a package archive.
ripgrep 13 or later, optional
     Used only by the prefilter.  Without it, an ‘agenda’ scope and an
     explicit file list behave exactly as they do with it, and an
     unbounded scope is read live with one message saying so.  The
     argument vector was verified against ripgrep 15.2.0 and no other
     version; 13 is named as a round, safely old floor, because the
     prefilter passes ‘--crlf’.  See *note Bounding a run::.

   Because ‘org-ql-ext’ and ‘org-ext’ are not on any package archive,
they cannot be declared in ‘Package-Requires’, and org-agents cannot be
installed as a self-contained package until that is resolved.  What has
to be true is only that both files are on ‘load-path’ before org-agents
is required.


File: org-agents.info,  Node: Installation,  Next: Activation,  Prev: What you need,  Up: Introduction

1.4 Installation
================

There is no package-archive recipe.  Clone the repository and put its
directory, and the directory holding the two modules of the previous
section, on ‘load-path’:

     (add-to-list 'load-path "~/src/org-agents")
     (add-to-list 'load-path "~/src/dot-emacs/lisp")   ; org-ql-ext, org-ext
     (require 'org-agents)

   There is no generated autoloads file either, which matters for
exactly one line of the deferred configuration below.  In the style of
the surrounding Org extensions in the author's own init:

     (use-package org-agents
       :after (org-ql-ext)
       :commands (org-agents-update
                  org-agents-update-buffer
                  org-agents-update-all
                  org-agents-preview
                  org-agents-insert-dblock
                  org-agents-list-approvals
                  org-agents-check-attributes
                  org-agents-attribute-columns
                  org-agents-apply-actions
                  org-agents-mode
                  global-org-agents-mode
                  org-agents-faces-mode
                  global-org-agents-faces-mode)
       ;; Org calls the dynamic-block writer by name, and Org calls
       ;; `org-agents-allowed-values' by name off one of its own hooks from any
       ;; Org buffer, so both have to be reachable before the package loads.
       :autoload (org-dblock-write:org-agents
                  org-agents-allowed-values)
       :init
       ;; Completion of declared attribute values.  A library has no business
       ;; adding itself to a user's hook at load time, so this one is yours.
       (add-hook 'org-property-allowed-value-functions
                 #'org-agents-allowed-values)
       :bind (("M-s a" . org-agents-preview)
              :map org-mode-map
              ("C-c C-x Q" . org-agents-update))
       :custom
       (org-agents-files '("~/org/agents.org")))

   The ‘:autoload’ line beside the ‘add-hook’ is not decoration.
Because the package has no autoloads file, a deferred ‘use-package’ form
that puts ‘org-agents-allowed-values’ on Org's hook without autoloading
that symbol breaks ‘org-set-property’ in *every* Org buffer, with
‘void-function org-agents-allowed-values’.  Adding the hook outside the
form, from an init that never loads org-agents eagerly, has the same
effect.  Keep the two together.


File: org-agents.info,  Node: Activation,  Next: Conventions used in this manual,  Prev: Installation,  Up: Introduction

1.5 Activation
==============

Nothing has to be configured for an agent to work.  Write an
‘:AGENT_QUERY:’ in a drawer and update it; the default scope is your
agenda files, and the default view writes child aliases into the entry
you are looking at.  The remaining settings buy the features you choose
to use.

‘org-agents-files’
     Which files ‘org-agents-update-all’ opens *and writes*.  Defaults
     to ‘("~/org/agents.org")’.  Set it if you keep agents in particular
     files and want one command to refresh them all; you do not need it
     to update the agent in front of you.  See *note By hand::.
‘org-agents-attributes-file’
     The attribute registry.  Defaults to ‘"~/org/attributes.org"’.
     Wholly optional: a missing or unreadable file declares nothing and
     says nothing about it.  Create it when you want typed attributes,
     prototypes or faces.  See *note The registry file::.
‘org-agents-prefilter’
     Defaults to ‘auto’, which uses ripgrep where the executable is
     found and reads the scope live where it is not.  Leave it alone
     until you have a corpus-scope agent that is too slow.  See *note
     Bounding a run::.

   One hook is deliberately *not* added for you.  Putting
‘org-agents-allowed-values’ on ‘org-property-allowed-value-functions’,
as the ‘use-package’ form of the previous section does, is what makes
‘org-set-property’ complete the values your registry declares -- in
every Org buffer, not only in the corpus.  A library has no business
mutating a user's hook, so the ‘add-hook’ is yours to write.  See *note
Completing declared values::.

   Two minor modes are also off until you turn them on:
‘org-agents-mode’, which refreshes a buffer's agents before each save
(see *note org-agents-mode::), and ‘org-agents-faces-mode’, which
colours headlines from attribute values (see *note Faces from attribute
values::).  Each has a globalized counterpart, ‘global-org-agents-mode’
and ‘global-org-agents-faces-mode’; the two choose which buffers to act
in by different rules, and for good reason (see *note
global-org-agents-mode::).


File: org-agents.info,  Node: Conventions used in this manual,  Prev: Activation,  Up: Introduction

1.6 Conventions used in this manual
===================================

Commands, functions, user options, properties and action verbs are given
their own definitions in *note Reference:: and are shown there like
this:

     -- Command: org-agents-update
     -- User Option: org-agents-prefilter
     -- Agent Property: AGENT_QUERY
     -- Action Verb: set-property! name value

   In running prose, a Lisp symbol is set as ‘org-agents-update’ and a
property name, a keyword or a literal value as ‘AGENT_QUERY’.  A
property is written with its surrounding colons, ‘:AGENT_QUERY:’, where
the point is the line you type into a drawer, and bare where the point
is the name.

   *This package binds no keys.*  It defines exactly one keymap, and
that one is local to the buffer ‘org-agents-list-approvals’ pops up (see
*note Reviewing what you approved::).  Every command in this manual is
therefore shown as ‘M-x org-agents-update’ and the like.  Three bindings
that do appear are Org's own, and work on an ‘org-agents’ dynamic block
because it is a dynamic block: ‘C-c C-c’ inside one writes it, ‘C-c C-x
C-u’ runs ‘org-dblock-update’, and ‘C-c C-x x’ offers ‘org-agents’ among
the block types it can insert.

   Examples are shown as Org source, and every one in this manual was
run.  Where the output of a run is shown, it is the text the buffer
actually held afterwards, and where an echo-area message is quoted it is
the message the package actually printed.  An absolute path that would
otherwise be a page wide is elided to ‘…’.  Timestamps and ID values are
of course whatever your own corpus produces; the tutorial uses short,
readable ‘:ID:’ values so that the rendered links fit on a line.

   The manual carries four indices.  *note Concept Index:: is the
subject-matter route in and is the one to try first.  *note Key Index::
lists key sequences.  *note Command Function and Name Index:: lists
commands, functions, and the property and verb names, all by name.
*note Variable Index:: lists user options and variables.


