2 Agents in the Tinderbox tradition
***********************************

Nothing in the model this package implements is its own invention.
Agents, prototypes, action code and user-defined attributes are
Tinderbox's: a Macintosh application for notes, published by Eastgate
Systems, Inc., and designed by Mark Bernstein, where those ideas have
been in continuous use since 2002.  org-agents is an independent Emacs
implementation of five of them over Org outlines.  It is not affiliated
with, endorsed by, or connected to Eastgate Systems in any way, and
nothing in it derives from Tinderbox's code.

   This chapter is here rather than in an appendix because the ideas are
easier to learn in the order Tinderbox arrived at them than in the order
an Org property drawer presents them.  Each section says what Tinderbox
does, quoting its own manual, and then what org-agents made of it and
where the rest of this manual describes that.  *note History and
Acknowledgments:: states the attribution proper and catalogues, with
reasons, what was deliberately left out.

   All quotations are from the Tinderbox v9.2 manual and are cited by
its line number in the plain-text edition, as ‘tbxman920:LINE’.  Later
versions of Tinderbox may differ.

* Menu:

* The five ideas::               Attributes, prototypes, agents, action code, appearance.
* Attributes in Tinderbox::      Typed values, declared once, with a vocabulary and a default.
