2.1 The five ideas
==================

Tinderbox is built on five ideas that reinforce one another.  Stated as
briefly as they can be:

  1. *Attributes.*  Every note has a large space of named, *typed*
     values.  Some are the application's; the rest are yours to define,
     with a type, a default, a suggested vocabulary and a description.
  2. *Prototypes.*  A note may be described as another note plus its
     differences.  Where it has no value of its own for an attribute,
     the value comes from its prototype -- and this has nothing to do
     with where either note sits in the outline.
  3. *Agents.*  A query can itself be a note.  An agent searches the
     document for notes that satisfy its criteria and gathers an alias
     of each one inside itself.
  4. *Action code.*  A document can act on itself: an action is a
     sentence that changes one or more notes, and an agent can carry
     one.
  5. *Appearance follows from values.*  How a note looks is itself read
     out of its attributes, so making a set of notes conspicuous is a
     matter of what they mean rather than of what colour you painted
     them.

   Put together, those five say something a search command does not:
that the answer to a question is a part of the document, that describing
a thing and describing where you filed it are different acts, and that a
corpus can be asked to maintain itself.  That is the part worth
borrowing, and it is what the rest of this manual is about.

   The five ideas are not borrowed whole.  Tinderbox is one binary
document that one application owns while it is open; an Org corpus is
thousands of plain-text files under version control that other tools
also write.  Two of Tinderbox's mechanisms depend on the first
arrangement and are deliberately absent here: continuous, timer-driven
evaluation, and inheritable action code.  Both absences are argued in
*note What was deliberately not ported and why::.


File: org-agents.info,  Node: Attributes in Tinderbox,  Next: Prototypes in Tinderbox,  Prev: The five ideas,  Up: Agents in the Tinderbox tradition

2.2 Attributes in Tinderbox
===========================

Tinderbox divides the attribute space in two:

     System attributes: information built into all Tinderbox documents
     such as the color of the note, or its width and height.  Tinderbox
     keeps this information about every note, and you may view, use, and
     change it.

     User attributes: you may add your own attributes that every note
     will have.  For instance, you could add the attribute "Priority,"
     and assign every note a priority level from one to five.  Or in a
     bibliography, create the attribute "Type" and tag the note for each
     source as either "primary" or "secondary."

     -- _Attributes_, ‘tbxman920:1338--1342’

   A user attribute is *declared* before it is used, and the declaration
carries more than a name:

     The inspector lets you choose to type of attribute -- some of the
     choice included number, string, URL, or list -- and set the default
     value that the attribute should use.  A text field labeled
     "Description" invites you to write a brief explanation of the way
     you intend to use the new attribute.

     -- _User Attributes_, ‘tbxman920:1534--1536’

   and a vocabulary may be suggested for it:

     Suggested values can be useful when an attribute's vocabulary
     should be limited -- for example, the anticipated values of $Status
     might be "planned;in progress;overdue;completed".  Suggested values
     are entered as semi-colon delimited lists.

     -- _Suggested Values_, ‘tbxman920:1472--1475’

   *What org-agents made of it.*  Org's own drawer properties are the
system attributes: they exist on every entry, Org gives several of them
meaning, and you may read and write any of them.  What Org has no notion
of is the other half.  A property drawer is untyped and undeclared:
‘:REVIEWS: lots’ is as valid to Org as ‘:REVIEWS: 5’, nothing records
that the property was ever meant to be a number, and nothing can tell
you where your corpus and your intentions have drifted apart.

   The registry file named by ‘org-agents-attributes-file’ is the
missing half.  One top-level entry declares one attribute: the heading
is its name, ‘:ATTR_TYPE:’ its type, ‘:ATTR_VALUES:’ its suggested
values, ‘:ATTR_DEFAULT:’ its default, and the entry's body is its
description.  ‘org-agents-check-attributes’ is then a lint that reports
where the corpus disagrees with the declarations -- a report Tinderbox
has no need of, because there its attributes cannot be used before they
are declared.  See *note The attribute registry::.

   org-agents implements six of Tinderbox's dozen-odd types, keeps
Tinderbox's ‘set’ and ‘list’ semantics exactly, changes their separator
from semicolons to whitespace so as not to fight Org, and declines to
port the type whose values are evaluated.  Each of those decisions is
argued in *note What it changed:: and *note What was deliberately not
ported and why::.


File: org-agents.info,  Node: Prototypes in Tinderbox,  Next: Agents in Tinderbox,  Prev: Attributes in Tinderbox,  Up: Agents in the Tinderbox tradition

2.3 Prototypes in Tinderbox
===========================

     Often, the easiest way to describe a note is to explain how it
     differs from another note.  We say that the first note serves as
     the prototype of the second: it shares all the properties of the
     prototype, except where we specify otherwise.

     -- _Prototypes_, ‘tbxman920:1682--1683’

   The lookup rule is a short checklist:

     Whenever Tinderbox looks up the value of an attribute, it reviews
     the following checklist: If the note has a value for that
     attribute, that's the value.  Otherwise, If the note has a
     prototype, and if the prototype has a value, then we inherit the
     prototype's value.  Otherwise, If the prototype itself has a
     prototype, we inherit that value.  Otherwise, We use the default
     value for that attribute.

     -- _Inheritance_, ‘tbxman920:1739--1746’

   and the sentence that makes the idea worth having at all:

     Note that inheritance has nothing to do with the document
     hierarchy; a note's prototype is not necessarily its container.

     -- _Inheritance_, ‘tbxman920:1751’

   *What org-agents made of it.*  ‘:PROTOTYPE:’ on any entry names a
master, either by name out of a reserved ‘Prototypes’ section of the
registry file -- the same name Tinderbox gives the same container -- or
as ‘id:UUID’ anywhere in the corpus.  The resolution order is
Tinderbox's checklist item for item: the entry's own drawer, then the
chain nearest hop first, then the registry's ‘:ATTR_DEFAULT:’, then
nothing.  Reads are virtual, so a master may be changed once and every
follower changes with it.  See *note Prototypes:: and *note The
resolution order::.

   Org does have an inheritance of its own --
‘org-use-property-inheritance’ makes a property visible to an entry's
descendants -- and it is a different relation.  Tinderbox's sentence
above is exactly org-agents' position, and it is why the query layer
spells the two axes differently: ‘$NAME*’ asks the outline and ‘$NAME^’
asks the prototype chain.  Containment is not inheritance; a task filed
under a project is not a kind of project, and a query has to say which
it means.  See *note The two axes::.


File: org-agents.info,  Node: Agents in Tinderbox,  Next: Action code in Tinderbox,  Prev: Prototypes in Tinderbox,  Up: Agents in the Tinderbox tradition

2.4 Agents in Tinderbox
=======================

     Agents are notes that search your Tinderbox document continuously
     for notes that meet the agent's criteria.  If an agent finds a note
     that satisfies its criteria, it automatically places an alias of
     that note inside the agent.  The agent can also apply an action to
     notes it finds.  For example, one agent might find notes that
     represent overdue library books and automatically set their color
     to red.

     -- _Notes and Containers_, ‘tbxman920:1015--1017’

   The property that makes a note an agent, and the one that gives it
something to do:

     AgentQuery -- the criteria for which the agent is looking.  If a
     note satisfies the query, the agent will contain an alias of that
     note.  If a note no longer satisfies that query, the agent will
     remove the note's alias from its children.

     AgentAction -- an optional action to be performed on the
     newly-created alias of any note that matches the agent's query.

     -- _Agents_, ‘tbxman920:2538--2545’

   *What org-agents made of it.*  An ordinary Org entry carrying
‘:AGENT_QUERY:’; the property is what makes the entry an agent, exactly
as ‘$AgentQuery’ is what makes a Tinderbox note one.  ‘:AGENT_ACTION:’
is ‘$AgentAction’.  In the default view each match becomes a child alias
heading, and a match that no longer satisfies the query loses its alias
-- with one deliberate exception, for an alias you have written under,
which is marked stale rather than removed.  See *note Views:: and *note
The alias contract::.

   Two differences are worth naming here, because a Tinderbox reader
will look for them.  Tinderbox agents run *continuously*, on a
priority-driven timer; org-agents' run when you say so.  And a Tinderbox
alias is a live proxy of its original, where an org-agents alias is a
heading holding a link and nothing more.  Both are argued in *note What
it changed:: and *note What was deliberately not ported and why::.


File: org-agents.info,  Node: Action code in Tinderbox,  Next: Appearance in Tinderbox,  Prev: Agents in Tinderbox,  Up: Agents in the Tinderbox tradition

2.5 Action code in Tinderbox
============================

     An action describes a change made on one or more notes.  For
     example, the action $Color(California)="red" sets the color of the
     note named "California" to "red".

     -- _Queries and Actions_, ‘tbxman920:2795--2796’

   Tinderbox runs an action at several different times, and the times
are as much a part of the idea as the syntax:

     An OnAdd action is performed when a note is added to a container,
     is discovered by an agent, or is placed atop an adornment, and
     affects the note being added.  A Rule is performed at frequent
     intervals, and affects the note that possesses the rule.  An Edict
     is performed after a document is opened, and at infrequent
     intervals while the document remains open.

     -- _Queries and Actions_, ‘tbxman920:2798--2801’

   It is a full language, and it reaches outside the document:

     If an action is simply an expression, the expression is evaluated
     and the result discarded.  For example, the action runCommand("open
     /Applications/iTunes.app") will ask your computer to open iTunes.

     -- _Side Effects_, ‘tbxman920:2880--2882’

   *What org-agents made of it.*  ‘:AGENT_ACTION:’ holds a declarative
sentence over a *fixed vocabulary of nine verbs* -- ‘set-property!’,
‘delete-property!’, ‘tag!’, ‘todo!’, ‘priority!’, ‘scheduled!’,
‘deadline!’, ‘effort!’, ‘archive!’ -- applied to the entries the query
matched.  It is a vocabulary rather than a language: the parser is a
regexp lexer, a token becomes a function by name construction plus
‘fboundp’, arguments reach a verb as strings, and *nothing in the
property is ever evaluated*.  The worst thing expressible is a bounded
Org edit that ‘grep’ can find afterwards.  See *note Action code:: and
*note The nine verbs::.

   The three times at which Tinderbox runs an action are all absent.
‘:AGENT_ACTION:’ is run by one command, ‘org-agents-apply-actions’,
typed, and that command prints a dry run and writes nothing until you
have agreed to it.  Neither minor mode runs an action, no timer does,
and saving a file does not.  The reason is stated in *note What was
deliberately not ported and why::; the mechanism, and why it is
structural rather than merely disciplined, is in *note The three
refusals::.


File: org-agents.info,  Node: Appearance in Tinderbox,  Next: What Org already had,  Prev: Action code in Tinderbox,  Up: Agents in the Tinderbox tradition

2.6 Appearance in Tinderbox
===========================

     The Inspector provides direct access to many of the
     most-commonly-used attributes.  For example, the Appearance
     inspector lets you change $Color -- the note's Color --
     $BorderColor -- the color of the note's border -- and many other
     attributes related to the note's appearance.

     -- _Attributes_, ‘tbxman920:1353--1354’

   and, joining appearance to agents:

     An agent that contains "urgent tasks" can automatically change
     their color in order to draw your attention.

     -- _Actions, Expressions, and Rules_, ‘tbxman920:2792’

   *What org-agents made of it.*  ‘:ATTR_FACES:’ in the registry maps an
attribute's values to Emacs faces, and ‘org-agents-faces-mode’ draws a
headline in the face its *resolved* value names.  Resolved is the whole
point: an entry that spells only ‘:PROTOTYPE:’, or nothing at all and so
takes ‘:ATTR_DEFAULT:’, is coloured exactly as one that spells the value
in its own drawer.  See *note Appearance::.

   The mechanism, though, is not Tinderbox's.  There, colour follows
from attribute values *by running action code*: a rule or an agent
action assigns ‘$Color’, which is a write to a note.  Here it is one
font-lock keyword -- no text inserted, no property written, no overlay
created, and turning the mode off puts the buffer back exactly as it
was.  That difference is what makes it safe to leave on in a file under
version control.


File: org-agents.info,  Node: What Org already had,  Next: The vocabulary map,  Prev: Appearance in Tinderbox,  Up: Agents in the Tinderbox tradition

2.7 What Org already had
========================

Much of Tinderbox's model has an Org counterpart already, and org-agents
neither replaces nor reimplements any of it.  Knowing which parts are
Org's makes it clear how little of this package is actually new.

the property drawer
     The attribute space itself, minus the types.
‘:NAME_ALL:’, and ‘org-property-allowed-value-functions’
     A per-file vocabulary, and the mechanism that completes it.
‘org-use-property-inheritance’
     Inheritance along the outline -- a different axis.
the agenda, and org-ql
     The query engine, and interactive searching.
column view, and ‘org-agenda-columns’
     A table of attribute values, editable in place.
dynamic blocks
     A region of a file that a function rewrites.
tags, TODO keywords, priorities, planning lines
     Most of what an action needs to be able to set.

   What org-agents adds on top is correspondingly narrow: a declaration
for the properties you invented, so they can have a type and a
vocabulary and be linted; a second inheritance axis that relates two
entries instead of two positions; a way for a query's answer to become
part of the document rather than a transient buffer; a way for
appearance to follow from a value without anything being written; a
bounded action vocabulary with a dry run in front of it; and a
prefilter, so that asking a question of a whole corpus is affordable.

   In particular, if what you want is to _look_ at the answer to a query
once, Org and org-ql already do that better than an agent does, and
‘org-agents-preview’ is the door into it (see *note Looking before you
write::).  An agent is for a question you want to keep asking, whose
answer you want to keep.


File: org-agents.info,  Node: The vocabulary map,  Prev: What Org already had,  Up: Agents in the Tinderbox tradition

2.8 The vocabulary map
======================

One list, for a reader who knows Tinderbox and wants the spelling.  ‘Not
ported’ means the idea was deliberately left out; see *note What was
deliberately not ported and why:: for the reason in each case.

agent
     An entry with ‘:AGENT_QUERY:’.  See *note Views:: and *note
     Updating::.
‘$AgentQuery’
     ‘:AGENT_QUERY:’.  See *note The query language::.
alias inside an agent
     A child heading with ‘:AGENT_MATCH: t’.  See *note The alias
     contract::.
‘$AgentAction’
     ‘:AGENT_ACTION:’.  See *note Action code::.
‘$AgentPriority’
     Not ported: it exists to schedule a timer, and there is no timer.
     See *note Updating::.
system attribute
     An Org property.  See *note The attribute registry::.
user attribute
     A registry entry with ‘:ATTR_TYPE:’.  See *note The registry
     file::.
suggested values
     ‘:ATTR_VALUES:’.  See *note The declaration::.
default value
     ‘:ATTR_DEFAULT:’.  See *note The declaration::.
Description
     The registry entry's body text.  See *note The registry file::.
pull-down of suggested values
     ‘org-agents-allowed-values’ on Org's own hook.  See *note
     Completing declared values::.
Displayed Attributes
     ‘org-agents-attribute-columns’.  See *note A corpus-wide column
     view::.
‘$Prototype’
     ‘:PROTOTYPE:’.  See *note Prototypes::.
the ‘Prototypes’ container
     The registry's ‘Prototypes’ section.  See *note Declaring
     masters::.
the inheritance checklist
     The resolution order.  See *note The resolution order::.
‘$Color’
     ‘:ATTR_FACES:’, and ‘org-agents-faces-mode’.  See *note
     Appearance::.
action code
     The nine verbs of ‘:AGENT_ACTION:’.  See *note The nine verbs::.
stamps
     ‘org-agents-apply-actions’ over a selection.  See *note The same
     command over a selection::.
‘$Rule’, ‘$Edict’, ‘$OnAdd’
     Not ported: nothing here edits a corpus while you are not looking.
     See *note Action code::.
‘$Width’, ‘$Height’, ‘$Xpos’, ‘$Ypos’
     Not ported: Org has no map for them to describe.  See *note
     Appearance::.
the ‘original’ designator
     Not ported: an org-agents alias is a heading holding a link, not a
     proxy.  See *note The alias contract::.
‘$PrototypeBequeathsChildren’
     Not ported: a copy made at assignment time stops tracking its
     source.  See *note Prototypes::.


