3.1 A corpus to work with
=========================

Make a directory, put these two files in it, add both to
‘org-agenda-files’, and point ‘org-directory’ at the directory -- the
last of those matters only for the scope step near the end.  The first
file is a corpus in miniature: two projects, two errands, and a
‘NEXT_REVIEW’ property on some of them but not all.

   The ‘:ID:’ values are short and readable only so that the links this
tutorial writes fit on a printed line.  In your own corpus they will be
the UUIDs ‘org-id’ generates, and everything below works the same.

   ‘work.org’:

     #+title: Work

     * Projects
       :PROPERTIES:
       :OWNER: me
       :END:
     ** TODO Rewrite the exporter
        :PROPERTIES:
        :ID:          rewrite-exporter
        :NEXT_REVIEW: 2026-09-01
        :END:
     ** TODO Audit the prefilter
        :PROPERTIES:
        :ID:          audit-prefilter
        :NEXT_REVIEW: 2026-08-25
        :END:
     ** DONE Ship 0.1
        :PROPERTIES:
        :ID: ship-0-1
        :END:
     * Errands
     ** TODO Renew the passport
        :PROPERTIES:
        :ID:          renew-passport
        :NEXT_REVIEW: 2026-08-21
        :END:
     ** TODO Collect the print
        :PROPERTIES:
        :ID: collect-print
        :END:

   ‘agents.org’ is where the agent will live.  It starts empty apart
from its title:

     #+title: Agents

   Three of the five entries in ‘work.org’ have both a ‘TODO’ keyword
and a ‘NEXT_REVIEW’ property.  Those three are the answer the agent
should find, and it is worth knowing it in advance: ‘Ship 0.1’ is
‘DONE’, and ‘Collect the print’ has no review date.


File: org-agents.info,  Node: Your first agent,  Next: What the update wrote,  Prev: A corpus to work with,  Up: Tutorial

3.2 Your first agent
====================

In ‘agents.org’, write a heading and give it a query:

     * Up for review
       :PROPERTIES:
       :AGENT_QUERY: (and (todo "TODO") (property "NEXT_REVIEW"))
       :END:

   That is the whole of it.  ‘:AGENT_QUERY:’ is what makes the entry an
agent; every other agent property has a default, and the defaults are
the ones you want here.  The query is a plain org-ql S-expression --
‘and’, ‘todo’ and ‘property’ are all org-ql's -- so nothing about it
needs your approval before it runs.  A query built out of anything else
does; see *note What is evaluated and what is refused::.

   Now put point anywhere inside that entry -- on the heading, in the
drawer, or in the body -- and type ‘M-x org-agents-update’.  The echo
area says:

     org-agents: 3 matches

   and the buffer now reads:

     #+title: Agents

     * Up for review
       :PROPERTIES:
       :AGENT_QUERY: (and (todo "TODO") (property "NEXT_REVIEW"))
       :AGENT_MATCHED: 3 [2026-08-20 Thu 17:47]
       :END:
     ** [[id:rewrite-exporter][Rewrite the exporter]]
     :PROPERTIES:
     :AGENT_MATCH: t
     :END:
     ** [[id:audit-prefilter][Audit the prefilter]]
     :PROPERTIES:
     :AGENT_MATCH: t
     :END:
     ** [[id:renew-passport][Renew the passport]]
     :PROPERTIES:
     :AGENT_MATCH: t
     :END:

   The three matches are the three you expected.  Follow one of the
links with ‘C-c C-o’ and you land on the entry in ‘work.org’.

   Two other commands do the same work over more ground:
‘org-agents-update-buffer’ updates every agent in the current buffer,
and ‘org-agents-update-all’ updates every agent in the files
‘org-agents-files’ names.  See *note By hand::.


File: org-agents.info,  Node: What the update wrote,  Next: Showing properties beside the link,  Prev: Your first agent,  Up: Tutorial

3.3 What the update wrote
=========================

Read what the update put in the file, because each part of it is a
promise about the next update.

   Each match became a child heading whose title is a link to the match
and whose drawer holds ‘:AGENT_MATCH: t’.  That property is written by
the package and never by you: it is how the next update recognizes the
headings it wrote, so that it can replace them.  A child that has never
carried it is not the package's to touch at all.  A heading of this kind
is an _alias_, which is Tinderbox's word for it.

   The agent itself gained ‘:AGENT_MATCHED:’, holding a count and an
inactive timestamp.  It records that the answer was computed, and when.
An agent whose stamp is three weeks old is telling you something an
agent that quietly re-answered on a timer could not.

   Notice what the update did *not* do: it did not save the file.  An
update leaves its buffers modified and unsaved on purpose, so that you
can read the result over -- and undo it with ‘C-/’ -- before you keep
it.  That is true of ‘org-agents-update-all’ across many files as well.

   Two consequences of the ‘:AGENT_MATCHED:’ stamp are worth knowing now
rather than discovering later.  Its timestamp is inactive, and inactive
timestamps are visible to queries, so a ‘(ts-inactive :from ...)’ query
over a corpus that holds agents will match the agents themselves.  And
because the stamp changes, a version-control diff of a saved update
shows movement even where the rendered aliases did not move.  *note What
writing AGENT_MATCHED costs:: sets out the whole cost of recording the
result on the agent.

   Now write a line of your own under one of the aliases:

     ** [[id:audit-prefilter][Audit the prefilter]]
     :PROPERTIES:
     :AGENT_MATCH: t
     :END:
     Why this one matters: it is the slow one.

   You have just changed who owns that heading.  An alias holding
nothing but its drawer is _pristine_, and every update deletes it and
writes it again.  The moment there is anything under it, no update will
delete it; if its match stops matching, the heading is marked ‘(stale)’
and left where it is, with your note.  This is the single most important
thing to understand about the ‘children’ view, and *note The alias
contract:: sets out its four sharp edges.


File: org-agents.info,  Node: Showing properties beside the link,  Next: The same matches as a table,  Prev: What the update wrote,  Up: Tutorial

3.4 Showing properties beside the link
======================================

A list of titles is often not enough: for a review queue you want the
date beside each one.  Add ‘:AGENT_FORMAT:’, naming properties to show
after the link, and update again:

     * Up for review
       :PROPERTIES:
       :AGENT_QUERY:  (and (todo "TODO") (property "NEXT_REVIEW"))
       :AGENT_FORMAT: NEXT_REVIEW
       :AGENT_MATCHED: 3 [2026-08-20 Thu 17:47]
       :END:
     ** [[id:rewrite-exporter][Rewrite the exporter]] 2026-09-01
     :PROPERTIES:
     :AGENT_MATCH: t
     :END:
     ** [[id:audit-prefilter][Audit the prefilter]] 2026-08-25
     :PROPERTIES:
     :AGENT_MATCH: t
     :END:
     ** [[id:renew-passport][Renew the passport]] 2026-08-21
     :PROPERTIES:
     :AGENT_MATCH: t
     :END:

   ‘:AGENT_FORMAT:’ takes whitespace-separated property names, so
‘NEXT_REVIEW OWNER’ would show both.  Be aware of two things before you
do that.  The values are read from the match's own drawer with *no*
inheritance, so naming ‘OWNER’ here shows nothing for the two matches
under ‘Projects’, even though ‘Projects’ sets it -- the outline axis has
a spelling of its own, and it is ‘$OWNER*’ (see *note The two axes::).
And a named property that a match does not have contributes an empty
string, which leaves trailing whitespace on the heading.  See *note
Formats::.


File: org-agents.info,  Node: The same matches as a table,  Next: Narrowing what the agent reads,  Prev: Showing properties beside the link,  Up: Tutorial

3.5 The same matches as a table
===============================

Child headings are the right rendering for something you want to fold
into the outline and write under.  For a queue you mean to scan and
sort, a table is better.  Set ‘:AGENT_VIEW: table’ and name the columns:

     * Up for review
       :PROPERTIES:
       :AGENT_QUERY:   (and (todo "TODO") (property "NEXT_REVIEW"))
       :AGENT_VIEW:    table
       :AGENT_COLUMNS: ITEM_BY_ID NEXT_REVIEW
       :END:

   Update it, and the aliases are gone; in their place is an
‘org-agents’ dynamic block, which the update opened because the entry
had none:

     #+BEGIN: org-agents
     | ITEM_BY_ID           | NEXT_REVIEW |
     |----------------------+-------------|
     | [[id:rewrite-exporter][Rewrite the exporter]] |  2026-09-01 |
     | [[id:audit-prefilter][Audit the prefilter]]  |  2026-08-25 |
     | [[id:renew-passport][Renew the passport]]   |  2026-08-21 |
     #+END:

   ‘ITEM_BY_ID’ is the link to the match, wherever in the column list
you put it.  Every other name is read as a property at the match, and a
property the match does not have makes an empty cell rather than an
error.  The cells are not padded: the writer leaves that to Org, and
‘C-c C-c’ on the table lines it up.

   A review queue in file order is not much of a queue.  Add a sort:

     :AGENT_SORT:    (ts-column 2)

   and the rows come out in date order:

     #+BEGIN: org-agents
     | ITEM_BY_ID           | NEXT_REVIEW |
     |----------------------+-------------|
     | [[id:renew-passport][Renew the passport]]   |  2026-08-21 |
     | [[id:audit-prefilter][Audit the prefilter]]  |  2026-08-25 |
     | [[id:rewrite-exporter][Rewrite the exporter]] |  2026-09-01 |
     #+END:

   ‘(ts-column 2)’ sorts the table's rows by reading column 2 as a
timestamp; the count is one-based over ‘:AGENT_COLUMNS:’.  It is a row
sort, so it applies to a table and is refused in any other view.  A
‘list’ view is available too, and a block can be given parameters of its
own so that one agent carries two views at once.  See *note Views:: and
*note Dynamic blocks::.


