3.6 Narrowing what the agent reads
==================================

Everything so far has used the default scope, ‘agenda’, which reads the
files ‘org-agenda-files’ names.  ‘:AGENT_SCOPE:’ changes that.  Its
value may be ‘agenda’, ‘active’, ‘all’, a directory relative to
‘org-directory’, or a list of file names.  Adding a limit as well:

     * The two nearest reviews
       :PROPERTIES:
       :AGENT_QUERY:   (and (todo "TODO") (property "NEXT_REVIEW"))
       :AGENT_SCOPE:   ("work.org")
       :AGENT_VIEW:    table
       :AGENT_COLUMNS: ITEM_BY_ID NEXT_REVIEW
       :AGENT_SORT:    (ts-column 2)
       :AGENT_LIMIT:   2
       :END:
     #+BEGIN: org-agents
     | ITEM_BY_ID          | NEXT_REVIEW |
     |---------------------+-------------|
     | [[id:renew-passport][Renew the passport]]  |  2026-08-21 |
     | [[id:audit-prefilter][Audit the prefilter]] |  2026-08-25 |
     #+END:

   The echo area says ‘org-agents: 2 matches’, and ‘:AGENT_MATCHED:’
records 2 -- the rows written, not the rows found.  The limit is applied
*after* the sort, so these really are the two nearest reviews and not
the first two org-ql happened to come across.

   Now try a scope that names a directory instead of a file.  Set
‘:AGENT_SCOPE: .’ -- the directory holding these two files, since
‘org-directory’ is where you put them -- and update.  This time there is
a second message:

     org-agents: scope `.' not narrowed (prefiltering off); scanning 2 files live
     org-agents: 3 matches

   That first line is your introduction to the prefilter.  ‘agenda’ and
an explicit file list *name* the files they will read, so there is
nothing for a prefilter to work out and none is run.  ‘active’, ‘all’
and a directory name nothing: naming a directory says nothing about how
much it holds, and reading one live means opening however many files it
turns out to contain.  Those three scopes are therefore narrowed with
ripgrep where it can be done, and read live with a message like the one
above where it cannot -- two files here, and possibly several thousand
in a real corpus.  Whichever happens, the answer is the same three
matches, because a prefilter chooses files and never matches.  See *note
Scope and the ripgrep prefilter::.


File: org-agents.info,  Node: Refreshing on save,  Next: Where to go next,  Prev: Narrowing what the agent reads,  Up: Tutorial

3.7 Refreshing on save
======================

Typing a command to refresh an agent gets old.  Put the buffer back to
the simple version:

     * Up for review
       :PROPERTIES:
       :AGENT_QUERY: (and (todo "TODO") (property "NEXT_REVIEW"))
       :END:

   turn on ‘M-x org-agents-mode’, and save with ‘C-x C-s’.  The echo
area says ‘org-agents: updated 1 agent’, and the file that reached disk
is the fully rendered one -- the three aliases and the ‘:AGENT_MATCHED:’
stamp included.

   Save again with nothing changed and the file on disk is
*byte-for-byte identical*, old stamp and all.  That is not an accident:
an update that renders what was already there puts the buffer back
exactly as it was, and it is the one property that makes writing
‘:AGENT_MATCHED:’ on every save affordable.  Without it, every save of
every file holding an agent would show up in ‘git diff’.

   Three deliberate refusals are what keep a save cheap, and the
paragraph above is the first of them.  The second is that a save never
spawns a prefilter, whatever ‘org-agents-prefilter’ says.  The third
follows from it: an agent whose scope *needs* a prefilter is not updated
on save at all.  It is named once and left for ‘org-agents-update’.  Add
‘:AGENT_SCOPE: all’ to the agent above, save, and you get:

     org-agents: not updated on save, the scope needs a prefilter: Up for review (…/agents.org:3)

   with the render left exactly as it was.  The reasoning behind all
three refusals is in *note The three refusals on the save path::.

   ‘org-agents-mode’ is buffer-local.  ‘M-x global-org-agents-mode’
turns it on in every Org buffer whose text mentions ‘:AGENT_QUERY:’, and
leaves the others alone -- in this tutorial, on in ‘agents.org’ and off
in ‘work.org’.  See *note global-org-agents-mode::.

   One surprise here is Emacs' rather than this package's, and it is the
first thing to check when a save appears not to have rendered: a file
you have visited and not touched does not render on ‘C-x C-s’, because
there was no save.  See *note org-agents-mode::.


File: org-agents.info,  Node: Where to go next,  Prev: Refreshing on save,  Up: Tutorial

3.8 Where to go next
====================

You now have a working agent and have met every part of one that has a
default.  Four routes lead out of here, and they are independent of each
other:

to ask better questions
     *note The query language:: covers the ‘$PROP’ layer that shortens
     ‘(property "URL")’ to ‘$URL’, the four positions a reference can
     occupy, and the two inheritance axes.  *note What is evaluated and
     what is refused:: explains why a query built out of ordinary org-ql
     ran without comment above, and what happens when one is not.  *note
     Scope and the ripgrep prefilter:: is what to read before pointing
     an agent at a whole corpus.
to render them differently
     *note Views:: documents all three renderings and their properties
     in full, including the alias contract sketched above.  *note
     Dynamic blocks:: covers giving one agent several views.  *note
     Updating:: covers the commands and both minor modes.
to give your corpus a vocabulary
     *note The attribute registry:: is how a property you invented
     acquires a type, a vocabulary, a default and documentation, and how
     to find out where your corpus disagrees with what you declared.
     *note Prototypes:: adds an inheritance that relates two entries
     rather than two positions.  *note Appearance:: colours a headline
     from a resolved value without writing anything.
to make agents act
     *note Action code:: is ‘:AGENT_ACTION:’: nine verbs, a dry run, and
     a trust model worth reading in full before you use any of it.

   *note Reference:: lists every command, option, property and verb in
one place, for when you know what you want and need the spelling.


