4 The query language
********************

An agent is an Org entry carrying a query, and the query is the whole of
what the agent knows about your corpus.  This chapter is about writing
one: the language it is written in, the shorthand this package adds to
that language, and the two axes along which a property may be read.

   Nothing here says anything about what an agent _does_ with its
matches.  For that see *note Views:: and *note Updating::.

* Menu:

* org-ql is the base language::  What org-agents adds, and what it leaves alone.
* Property references::          The $PROP layer, and why it exists.
* The four positions::           Boolean, value, numeric and name position, with an example of each.
* The two axes::                 $NAME* is the outline, $NAME^ is the prototype chain.
* Entry specials::               The seven references that name the entry rather than a property.
* The property-resolved predicate:: The prototype-aware property, and when to reach for it.
* Putting a query in a property drawer:: One line, and what Org does to it on the way in.
* Limitations of the query layer:: What the sugar cannot express.


