4.1 org-ql is the base language
===============================

An agent's query is an org-ql sexp query.  This package defines no query
language of its own, and does not wrap or restrict org-ql's: anything
you can write in an org-ql sexp query you can write in ‘:AGENT_QUERY:’.
That includes the predicates -- ‘todo’, ‘tags’, ‘heading’, ‘level’,
‘priority’, ‘category’, ‘ts’, ‘scheduled’, ‘deadline’, ‘closed’, ‘src’
and the rest -- the combinators ‘and’, ‘or’, ‘not’, ‘when’ and ‘unless’,
the relational predicates ‘parent’, ‘ancestors’, ‘children’ and
‘descendants’, and arbitrary Lisp.

   The predicate catalogue is org-ql's own and is not reproduced here.
See *note Queries: (org-ql)Queries. in the org-ql manual for the list,
the argument shapes, and the aliases.  Two of org-ql's conventions are
worth recalling because they surprise people who meet them through an
agent: a bare string is read as a ‘regexp’ predicate over the entry's
text, so ‘:AGENT_QUERY: "Tinderbox"’ is a full-text search rather than a
heading search; and the comparator symbols ‘<’, ‘<=’, ‘>’, ‘>=’ and ‘=’
need no quoting where a predicate accepts one.

   What this package adds to that language is small and is the subject
of the rest of this chapter:

   • the ‘$PROP’ reference layer, which is rewritten into plain org-ql
     before anything else happens (*note Property references::);
   • one predicate, ‘property-resolved’, which org-ql has no equivalent
     of (*note The property-resolved predicate::);
   • a gate, because a query read out of a file is code read out of a
     file (*note What is evaluated and what is refused::);
   • the conjunct in ‘org-agents-exclude’, which is conjoined into every
     query so that an agent does not match the aliases it wrote (*note
     The structural gate::);
   • an optional ripgrep prefilter, which chooses which files org-ql
     opens and can never change what matches (*note Scope and the
     ripgrep prefilter::).

   One habit is worth forming early.  Where org-ql has a predicate for
what you mean, write the predicate rather than the equivalent Lisp.
Both answer the same question, but a predicate call stays inside what
the gate admits without asking, and it may be pushed into the prefilter;
a piece of Lisp does neither.  So prefer ‘(level < 3)’ to ‘(< $LEVEL
3)’, and ‘(todo "TODO")’ to ‘(equal $TODO "TODO")’, and keep the Lisp
for the questions org-ql has no predicate for.


