4.2 Property references
=======================

A property reference is a ‘$’ followed by a property name, optionally
followed by one suffix:

     $URL        the entry's own :URL: value
     $URL*       the outline axis:   an ancestor's value counts
     $URL^       the prototype axis: a master's value counts

   The layer exists for two reasons.  The first is brevity: over a
corpus with a dozen user attributes ‘property’ appears in almost every
query, and ‘$NEXT_REVIEW’ says what ‘(property "NEXT_REVIEW")’ says in a
third of the space.  The second reason matters more.  "The entry's
‘:OWNER:’" is ambiguous in an outline, and dangerously so -- it may mean
the value in this entry's own drawer, or the one an ancestor wrote, or
the one a prototype lends.  Writing ‘$OWNER’, ‘$OWNER*’ and ‘$OWNER^’
forces the question to be answered in the query, where a reader can see
it, rather than in the reader's head.  See *note The two axes::.

   Expansion is a source-to-source rewrite of the form, performed before
the gate is consulted and before org-ql sees anything.  The expander
reads no buffer and evaluates nothing, so what a reference means never
depends on where point happens to be.  That also means you can work out
what a query will do by expanding it in your head, and that the form the
gate shows you is the expanded one -- so a prompt about ‘(> $REVIEWS 3)’
names ‘string-to-number’ and ‘org-entry-get’, which is what will
actually run.

   Write the name as the drawer spells it.  Org compares property keys
case-insensitively, so a drawer key ‘:next_review:’ answers a query for
‘NEXT_REVIEW’, but a reader of your query has only what you wrote.


File: org-agents.info,  Node: The four positions,  Next: The two axes,  Prev: Property references,  Up: The query language

4.3 The four positions
======================

A reference expands according to the position it appears in.  There are
four positions, and the whole of the layer is in this list -- what you
write, and what org-ql is handed:

‘$URL’
     ‘(property "URL")’
‘$URL*’
     ‘(org-entry-get nil "URL" t)’
‘$URL^’
     ‘(property-resolved "URL")’
‘(and (todo) $URL)’
     ‘(and (todo) (property "URL"))’
‘(string-match "gh" $URL)’
     ‘(string-match "gh" (or (org-entry-get nil "URL") ""))’
‘(equal $OWNER* "johnw")’
     ‘(equal (or (org-entry-get nil "OWNER" t) "") "johnw")’
‘(equal $OWNER^ "johnw")’
     ‘(equal (or (org-agents-resolve-property-quietly "OWNER") "")
     "johnw")’
‘(> $REVIEWS 3)’
     ‘(> (string-to-number (or (org-entry-get nil "REVIEWS") "0")) 3)’
‘(> $REVIEWS* 3)’
     ‘(> (string-to-number (or (org-entry-get nil "REVIEWS" t) "0")) 3)’
‘(> $REVIEWS^ 3)’
     ‘(> (string-to-number (or (org-agents-resolve-property-quietly
     "REVIEWS") "0")) 3)’
‘(property $OWNER "johnw")’
     ‘(property "OWNER" "johnw")’
‘(property-ts $NEXT_REVIEW :from -7)’
     ‘(property-ts "NEXT_REVIEW" :from -7)’
‘(property-resolved $STATUS "blocked")’
     ‘(property-resolved "STATUS" "blocked")’
‘(children $URL)’
     ‘(children (property "URL"))’
‘$ITEM’
     ‘(org-get-heading t t t t)’

   The four rules behind it are these.

Boolean position
----------------

A reference stands where a query is expected: as the whole query, as an
argument of ‘and’, ‘or’, ‘not’, ‘when’ or ‘unless’, or as the query
argument of ‘parent’, ‘ancestors’, ‘children’ or ‘descendants’.  There
it becomes a predicate: ‘(property NAME)’ for a bare reference,
‘(property-resolved NAME)’ for the prototype axis, and the inheriting
‘org-entry-get’ call for the outline axis.

   Motivation: anything actionable that has a link.

     * Anything with a link
       :PROPERTIES:
       :AGENT_QUERY:  (and (todo) $URL)
       :AGENT_VIEW:   list
       :END:

   renders

     - [[id:e100…001][Rewrite the exporter]]
     - [[id:e100…004][Renew the passport]]

   ‘Tinderbox notes’ has a ‘:URL:’ but no TODO keyword, so it does not
match.  Nothing was asked about this query: ‘$URL’ became a plain org-ql
predicate, and the gate admits it unremarked.

Value position
--------------

A reference in residual Lisp -- Lisp that is not a predicate call --
becomes ‘(or ACCESSOR "")’.  It is never nil, so ‘string-match’ and its
neighbours cannot signal at an entry that has no such property.

   Motivation: which of these live on GitHub?

     * Hosted on GitHub
       :PROPERTIES:
       :AGENT_QUERY:  (string-match "github" $URL)
       :AGENT_VIEW:   list
       :AGENT_FORMAT: URL
       :END:

   renders

     - [[id:e100…001][Rewrite the exporter]] https://github.com/example/exporter

   This one is residual Lisp, so it reaches the gate: the first update
asks once, and remembers the answer.  See *note Remembered approvals::.

Numeric position
----------------

Any argument of ‘<’, ‘>’, ‘<=’, ‘>=’, ‘=’, ‘+’, ‘-’, ‘*’ or ‘/’ is
numeric, and the property is coerced with ‘(string-to-number (or
ACCESSOR "0"))’.  The coercion propagates down, so a reference nested
inside an arithmetic expression is coerced too.  A property nothing set
therefore reads as zero rather than as an error.

   Motivation: what have I been over more than three times?

     * Reviewed more than three times
       :PROPERTIES:
       :AGENT_QUERY:  (> $REVIEWS 3)
       :AGENT_VIEW:   list
       :AGENT_FORMAT: REVIEWS
       :END:

   renders

     - [[id:e100…001][Rewrite the exporter]] 5
     - [[id:e100…003][Ship 0.1]] 9

Name position
-------------

The first argument of ‘property’, ‘property-ts’ and ‘property-resolved’
is a property _name_, not a value, and a reference there denotes the
name string.  This is what lets you write the name once, in the same
spelling you use everywhere else, and still reach a predicate's keyword
arguments.

   Motivation: everything whose next review falls in the next month.

     * Reviews due within the month
       :PROPERTIES:
       :AGENT_QUERY:  (property-ts $NEXT_REVIEW :from -7 :to 30)
       :AGENT_VIEW:   list
       :AGENT_FORMAT: NEXT_REVIEW
       :END:

   renders

     - [[id:e100…001][Rewrite the exporter]] 2026-09-01
     - [[id:e100…002][Audit the prefilter]] 2026-08-25
     - [[id:e100…004][Renew the passport]] 2026-08-21

   Only the _first_ argument of those three predicates is a name
position.  ‘(property $OWNER "johnw")’ is ‘(property "OWNER" "johnw")’,
but ‘(property "STATUS" $STATUS)’ is refused: the value argument is not
a position the expander has a reading for.  See *note Limitations of the
query layer::.

   A known org-ql predicate holding no reference in one of these
positions is passed through untouched, whatever its arguments are.  That
is what keeps this layer from being able to break a query that does not
use it.


File: org-agents.info,  Node: The two axes,  Next: Entry specials,  Prev: The four positions,  Up: The query language

4.4 The two axes
================

There are two ways a value can reach an entry that does not spell it,
and this package gives each a spelling of its own:

(no suffix)
     The entry's own drawer, and nothing else.  ‘$URL’ is ‘(property
     "URL")’, which is org-ql reading the drawer.

‘*’ -- the outline axis
     ‘org-entry-get’ with its INHERIT argument, so an ancestor's value
     answers, and so do a ‘#+PROPERTY:’ keyword and
     ‘org-global-properties’.  This is Org's own property inheritance,
     unchanged.

‘^’ -- the prototype axis
     The entry, then its ‘:PROTOTYPE:’ chain nearest hop first, then the
     registry's ‘:ATTR_DEFAULT:’, then nothing.  See *note The
     resolution order:: for the order in full.

   They are two axes and not two settings of one, because containment is
not inheritance.  A task filed under a project is not a kind of project.
A query has to say which relation it means, and the difference is not
academic -- asked along the two axes over the same corpus, the same
question gives two answers with no entry in common.

   Motivation, along the outline: everything actionable inside a project
I own, where ownership is written once on the project.

     * Anything johnw owns, by containment
       :PROPERTIES:
       :AGENT_QUERY:  (and (todo "TODO") $OWNER*)
       :AGENT_VIEW:   list
       :END:

     - [[id:e100…001][Rewrite the exporter]]
     - [[id:e100…002][Audit the prefilter]]

   The two entries under ‘Projects’, which is where ‘:OWNER:’ is
written.

   Now the same question along the other axis, over the same corpus:

     * Owner resolved through the prototype
       :PROPERTIES:
       :AGENT_QUERY:  (equal $OWNER^ "johnw")
       :AGENT_VIEW:   list
       :END:

     - [[file:…/corpus.org::*Projects][Projects]]
     - [[id:e100…002][Audit the prefilter]]
     - [[id:e100…005][Collect the print]]

   Three matches, and not one of them is in the first answer's set of
two.  ‘Projects’ spells ‘:OWNER:’ in its own drawer.  The other two
reach it through a chain of masters.  ‘Rewrite the exporter’, which the
outline query found, does not match here, because the prototype axis
does not walk the outline.  Reversing that -- one entry answering both
-- would take an entry that is both filed under an owning project and
given an owning master.

   At most one suffix is read.  ‘$’, ‘$*’, ‘$N*^’ and ‘$N^*’ name no
property or no axis, and rather than being read as something almost
right they are refused by name:

     org-agents: no expansion for ‘$N*^’ in ‘$N*^’

   The reason for refusing rather than guessing is worth knowing,
because it is the shape of every refusal in this package.  Reading ‘$*’
as a reference would hand ‘org-entry-get’ the empty name, which answers
nil at every entry; reading ‘$N*^’ as one would name a property whose
key holds a ‘^’, which no drawer spells.  Either way you would have an
agent that matches nothing with nothing said about why.

   One asymmetry between the axes will meet you, and it is real rather
than an oversight.  In boolean position the prototype axis expands to
‘(property-resolved NAME)’, which is a predicate, so the gate admits it
without a word.  The outline axis expands to ‘(org-entry-get nil NAME
t)’, which is a function call, so it is residual Lisp and you are asked
about it once.  See *note The structural gate::.  The two axes also
behave differently in the prefilter; see *note Inheritance and
pushability::.


File: org-agents.info,  Node: Entry specials,  Next: The property-resolved predicate,  Prev: The two axes,  Up: The query language

4.5 Entry specials
==================

Seven names refer to the entry itself rather than to a row of its
property drawer.  They exist because the obvious spelling does not work:
‘(property "TODO")’ is a perfectly valid query that never matches
anything, since no drawer holds a ‘:TODO:’ line.  A special is expanded
to an accessor instead:

‘$ITEM’
     ‘(org-get-heading t t t t)’ -- the heading text, without TODO
     keyword, priority cookie, tags or comment marker.
‘$TODO’
     ‘(org-get-todo-state)’.
‘$PRIORITY’
     ‘(org-entry-get nil "PRIORITY")’.
‘$TAGS’
     ‘(org-get-tags)’ -- a list of strings, not a string.
‘$CATEGORY’
     ‘(org-get-category)’.
‘$LEVEL’
     ‘(org-current-level)’ -- a number.
‘$FILE’
     ‘(buffer-file-name)’.

   Three things follow from that table.

   A special manages its own type.  In numeric position it is _not_
wrapped in ‘string-to-number’, because ‘org-current-level’ already
answers with a number: ‘(< $LEVEL 3)’ expands to ‘(< (org-current-level)
3)’.  In value position it is not wrapped in ‘(or … "")’ either, so
‘$TAGS’ reaches ‘member’ as the list it is and ‘$TODO’ can be compared
against nil.

   A suffix on a special is _ignored_, not refused: ‘$ITEM’, ‘$ITEM*’
and ‘$ITEM^’ all expand to ‘(org-get-heading t t t t)’.  There is
nothing to inherit and nothing for a prototype to carry, so there is no
reading for a suffix to have; it is dropped silently rather than
diagnosed, which is the one place in this layer where a spelling that
means nothing is not named.

   The seven accessors are a closed set of read-only readers of the
entry at point, so a query is no less safe for holding one, and a bare
special passes the structural gate unremarked.

   Motivation: headings that mention Tinderbox, wherever they are.

     * Headings that mention Tinderbox
       :PROPERTIES:
       :AGENT_QUERY:  (string-match "Tinderbox" $ITEM)
       :AGENT_VIEW:   list
       :END:

     - [[id:e100…006][Tinderbox notes]]

   Here too, prefer org-ql where org-ql has an answer.  ‘heading’,
‘todo’, ‘tags’, ‘level’, ‘priority’ and ‘category’ are all predicates,
and a predicate is admitted without a prompt and may narrow the file
set; a special is for when you need the value inside an expression, as
‘$ITEM’ is needed above and ‘$TAGS’ is needed in ‘(member "emacs"
$TAGS)’.


File: org-agents.info,  Node: The property-resolved predicate,  Next: Putting a query in a property drawer,  Prev: Entry specials,  Up: The query language

4.6 The property-resolved predicate
===================================

This package defines one org-ql predicate:

     (property-resolved NAME &optional VALUE)

   It answers non-nil when the entry _resolves_ NAME -- or resolves it
to VALUE -- along the prototype axis: the entry's own drawer, then its
‘:PROTOTYPE:’ chain, then the registry's ‘:ATTR_DEFAULT:’.  VALUE is
compared with ‘string-equal’, case-sensitively, and the argument order
is org-ql's own ‘property’.

   It exists because org-ql's ‘property’ cannot see such a value, and no
amount of configuration makes it.  The plain ‘(property NAME)’ and
‘(property NAME VALUE)’ forms read the entry's own drawer, which is
exactly what a ‘:NAME:’ line in the file is; a value that arrives
through a master is written in another file entirely, and the inheriting
entry spells nothing at all.

   Motivation: everything blocked, however it came to say so.

     * Blocked, however it says so
       :PROPERTIES:
       :AGENT_QUERY:  (property-resolved "STATUS" "blocked")
       :AGENT_VIEW:   list
       :END:

     - [[id:e100…002][Audit the prefilter]]

   ‘Audit the prefilter’ spells no ‘:STATUS:’ at all.  Its ‘:PROTOTYPE:
Blocked Task’ resolves to ‘blocked’ through the chain.  ‘(property
"STATUS" "blocked")’ matches nothing here, and ‘grep’ over the corpus
for ‘blocked’ finds only the registry.  Those are the two cases to reach
for this predicate for -- a value that arrives through a chain, and one
that comes from a registry default.  Where the value is in the entry's
own drawer, ‘property’ answers and is cheaper.

   The sugar covers only half of this predicate.  In boolean position
‘$STATUS^’ expands to ‘(property-resolved "STATUS")’, the existence
form; there is no reference spelling for the value form.  So write the
predicate -- and prefer writing it to the equivalent Lisp even where the
sugar would serve.  ‘(property-resolved "OWNER" "johnw")’ is a predicate
call, so it is admitted without a prompt and it can narrow the file set,
while ‘(equal $OWNER^ "johnw")’ is residual Lisp and does neither.  The
sugar is a convenience; the predicate is the interface.

   Two diagnostics you may meet.  The predicate's arity is checked
before any entry is examined, because it carries no normalizer and
therefore has nothing to swallow a stray keyword argument with:

     org-agents: ‘property-resolved’ takes a property name and an optional
     value, both strings: ‘(property-resolved "STATUS" :inherit t)’

   And a prototype chain that loops is reported rather than signalled
from inside a predicate body, once per update, naming the hops -- a
‘user-error’ raised inside org-ql's generated matcher would abort the
whole update.  For a caller in Lisp, ‘org-agents-resolve-property’
signals on a cycle and ‘org-agents-resolve-property-quietly’ reports
one; see *note Functions for Lisp callers::.

   For what "resolves" means, hop by hop, see *note The resolution
order::; for what it costs, see *note Reads are virtual::.


File: org-agents.info,  Node: Putting a query in a property drawer,  Next: Limitations of the query layer,  Prev: The property-resolved predicate,  Up: The query language

4.7 Putting a query in a property drawer
========================================

An Org property value is a single line, and ‘:AGENT_QUERY:’ is an Org
property value.  Keep the query on one line.

     * Up for review
       :PROPERTIES:
       :AGENT_QUERY: (and (todo "TODO") (property "NEXT_REVIEW"))
       :END:

   Four things about that one line are worth knowing, because each of
them can turn a query you wrote into something you did not.

   A property line written with nothing after it reads as absent, not as
empty.  ‘:AGENT_QUERY:’ alone is an entry with no query, and you get ‘No
:AGENT_QUERY: at point’ rather than a diagnosis about unreadable text.
The same rule applies to every agent property: written-but-empty and
not-written mean the same thing.

   Org reads the literal value ‘nil’ as no value at all.  So
‘:AGENT_VIEW: nil’ is an agent with no view, which takes the default,
rather than an agent whose view is refused.

   Text that will not read as a Lisp form is diagnosed as itself, naming
what was read and what ‘read-from-string’ said about it:

     org-agents: unreadable :AGENT_QUERY: ‘(and (todo)’: End of file during parsing

   A ‘:AGENT_QUERY+:’ continuation line _is_ read.  ‘org-entry-get’
joins the pieces with ‘org--property-get-separator’ -- a space, unless
‘org-property-separators’ says otherwise -- so a query split at a
whitespace boundary does read back as the query you meant.  It is still
a bad idea.  Split anywhere else and the pieces join into a different
sexp, or into one that will not read at all, and nothing about the
drawer says which happened.

   Where a query genuinely will not fit on a line, the answer is not to
continue it but to name it.  Define a predicate of your own with
‘org-ql-defpred’ in your init file and call it from the drawer: the
drawer then holds ‘(and (todo "TODO") (my-stalled-p))’, the definition
lives in a file you wrote, and the query stays a predicate call, which
is what the gate admits without asking.  Note the other half of that
bargain: a predicate you define is a predicate the gate now admits by
name, and org-ql runs its normalizer when it compiles a query.  See
*note Refused heads::.


File: org-agents.info,  Node: Limitations of the query layer,  Prev: Putting a query in a property drawer,  Up: The query language

4.8 Limitations of the query layer
==================================

The ‘$PROP’ layer is a rewrite over four positions, and it is worth
knowing where it stops.

   A property name in a reference is a literal.  Nothing computes one,
and there is no spelling for "the property this other property names".

   The two axes cannot be composed.  At most one suffix is read, so
there is no way to ask for "an ancestor's resolved value" or "a master's
inherited value" in the sugar.  Where you want a combination, write the
Lisp yourself, and write it with ‘org-agents-resolve-property-quietly’
rather than the signalling resolver: residual Lisp runs at every
candidate entry, and a ‘user-error’ from inside org-ql's generated
matcher aborts the whole update.

   A reference is expanded only in the four positions.  Inside any other
known predicate's argument list it is refused by name rather than read
as a value:

     org-agents: no expansion for ‘$TOPICS’ in ‘(tags $TOPICS)’
     org-agents: no expansion for ‘$STATUS’ in ‘(property "STATUS" $STATUS)’

   That is deliberate, and it is the safe direction: an argument
silently read the wrong way is an agent that matches the wrong thing.
Where you want a property's value as an argument, put the predicate's
job into Lisp -- ‘(member "emacs" (split-string $TOPICS))’ rather than
‘(tags $TOPICS)’.

   A suffix on an entry special is ignored rather than diagnosed.  See
*note Entry specials::.

   The layer covers the query and nothing else.  ‘:AGENT_FORMAT:’ and
‘:AGENT_COLUMNS:’ take bare property names, and both read with
‘org-entry-get’ at the match with no inheritance and no resolution -- so
a format naming ‘OWNER’ is empty for a match that inherits it from an
ancestor or a master, even where the query that found the match read it
along one of those axes.  See *note Formats:: and *note The table
view::.


File: org-agents.info,  Node: What is evaluated and what is refused,  Next: Scope and the ripgrep prefilter,  Prev: The query language,  Up: Top

5 What is evaluated, and what is refused
****************************************

An org-ql query is Lisp, and org-ql evaluates the Lisp in it at every
candidate entry.  A query you type at a prompt is your own Lisp and
needs no ceremony.  A query read out of a property drawer is code read
out of a file, and a file may have arrived from anywhere -- a colleague,
a mailing-list attachment, a repository you cloned to read.

   This chapter says exactly what runs without a word, what you are
asked about, what is refused whatever you answer, and how a decision you
made once is remembered.  A reader who finishes it should be able to
look at a file someone sent them and decide whether to update its
agents.

* Menu:

* Why a gate exists::            A query out of a drawer is code out of a file.
* The structural gate::          What passes without a word, and what the gate is shown.
* Remembered approvals::         The hash, where it is kept, and what invalidates it.
* Refused heads::                A predicate whose name is not a question.
* Refused queries::              Saying no to one form, permanently.
* Reviewing what you approved::  The approvals listing, its keys, and how to read a file you were sent.


File: org-agents.info,  Node: Why a gate exists,  Next: The structural gate,  Up: What is evaluated and what is refused

5.1 Why a gate exists
=====================

org-ql compiles a query into a predicate function and tests it with
point at each candidate heading.  Any part of the query that is not a
predicate call is compiled in as it stands, so it runs once per
candidate entry with whatever privileges Emacs has.  Over a corpus scope
that is thousands of files and hundreds of thousands of entries.

   A query built only out of org-ql's predicates is a different kind of
thing.  What it can do is bounded by what those predicates do, which is
read Org buffers and answer yes or no -- with one exception, which has a
node of its own in *note Refused heads::.  The gate's whole job is to
tell those two kinds apart: to let the first run without interrupting
you, and to put the second to you before it runs.

   It is not a sandbox, and it does not try to be.  Nothing here
analyses what a piece of Lisp would do if it ran; the distinction drawn
is structural, and everything that falls on the wrong side of it is
handed to you to decide.  What the gate buys is that you are asked,
once, with the form printed in front of you -- and that a decision you
make is remembered and reviewable.


File: org-agents.info,  Node: The structural gate,  Next: Remembered approvals,  Prev: Why a gate exists,  Up: What is evaluated and what is refused

5.2 The structural gate
=======================

A form passes the structural gate when it is built only out of:

   • known org-ql predicates -- built-in, their aliases, and any defined
     with ‘org-ql-defpred’, including this package's
     ‘property-resolved’;
   • the combinators ‘and’, ‘or’, ‘not’, ‘when’ and ‘unless’, and the
     relational predicates ‘parent’, ‘ancestors’, ‘children’ and
     ‘descendants’;
   • literals in argument position;
   • quoted data, which is returned rather than evaluated, whatever it
     holds;
   • the seven accessor forms a bare entry special expands to.

   Every argument must answer for itself, because org-ql evaluates a
predicate's arguments as Lisp too.  ‘(tags (shell-command "x"))’ is a
predicate call with a predicate's name on it and does not pass.  A
dotted list fails closed.  A list whose first element is not a symbol is
the data it looks like -- which is how org-ql's own ‘(src :regexps
("defun"))’ is written -- unless that first element is itself callable,
since a byte-code object reads in from a property drawer like any other
text.

   What the gate is shown is the _form_, not the query.
‘org-agents-exclude’ is conjoined in first, so a query written

     (> $REVIEWS 3)

   reaches the gate -- and org-ql -- as

     (and (> (string-to-number (or (org-entry-get nil "REVIEWS") "0")) 3)
          (not (property "AGENT_MATCH")))

   One function says what that form is, so the form that is gated, the
form that is hashed and the form that is evaluated cannot come to mean
different things.  A gate that judged the query alone would have you
approving a form nobody had been shown.

   Here is how a handful of real forms are classified.  The expanded
form is what is judged, so the reference layer's position rules decide
this as much as anything you wrote:

     passes  (and (todo "TODO") (property "NEXT_REVIEW"))
     passes  (property-resolved "STATUS" "blocked")
     passes  (org-get-heading t t t t)                          <- $ITEM
     passes  (and (todo) (org-get-todo-state))                  <- $TODO
     asks    (org-entry-get nil "OWNER" t)                      <- $OWNER*
     asks    (string-match "github" (or (org-entry-get nil "URL") ""))
     asks    (> (string-to-number (or (org-entry-get nil "REVIEWS") "0")) 3)

   What "asks" means depends on where you are.  Interactively you get
one ‘yes-or-no-p’ with the whole form printed in the prompt.  In batch,
or under ‘inhibit-interaction’, there is nobody to ask, so the query is
skipped with a message naming it and the update reports ‘query not
approved’:

     org-agents: skipping unapproved query (and (string-match "github" (or
     (org-entry-get nil "URL") "")) (not (property "AGENT_MATCH")))

   And if org-ql's own ‘org-ql-ask-unsafe-queries’ is nil, nothing is
asked and the form runs.  That variable is org-ql's and it governs
whether you are asked; setting it to nil turns off the prompting in this
package as well.  A refusal is not affected by it (see *note Refused
heads::).

   The text you are shown is a function of the form and of nothing else.
Every printer variable that could change it is bound while it is printed
-- ‘print-length’ and ‘print-level’ to nil so nothing is elided,
‘print-escape-newlines’ so a string in the query cannot break the prompt
into lines and push the interesting conjunct off the top of the window,
‘print-gensym’ so an uninterned symbol cannot share text with the
interned symbol of the same name.  What you read is what is hashed.


File: org-agents.info,  Node: Remembered approvals,  Next: Refused heads,  Prev: The structural gate,  Up: What is evaluated and what is refused

5.3 Remembered approvals
========================

Say yes to a prompt and the decision is remembered, keyed on the SHA-1
of the printed form.  There are two records.  The session table holds it
until Emacs is restarted.  ‘org-agents-safe-queries’ holds it across
sessions, as a list of ‘(HASH . TEXT)’ conses where TEXT is the very
text the hash was taken of -- so a stored approval can afterwards be
read for what it is rather than taken on trust.

   You are offered the permanent record only where customize has a file
to write.  ‘customize-save-variable’ writes to the file ‘(custom-file
t)’ names, which answers nil, and writes nothing, unless
‘user-init-file’ is set.  Where your real init is an Org file that you
tangle, ‘user-init-file’ is the tangled output and a saved approval is
overwritten the next time it is generated.  Set ‘custom-file’ to a file
of its own, or every approval you make is a session approval.  Emacs
says so when it happens:

     org-agents: ‘org-agents-safe-queries’ changed for this session only;
     customize has no file to save it in

   Editing ‘org-agents-exclude’ invalidates every approval you hold.
That follows from the gate being shown the form: the exclusion is part
of the form, so changing it changes the text, and therefore the hash.
Measured over the same query with the default exclusion and with a
different one:

     (and (> (string-to-number (or (org-entry-get nil "REVIEWS") "0")) 3)
          (not (property "AGENT_MATCH")))
         8c7df326f437dfec6f233c25527319074d1a9fe1

     (and (> (string-to-number (or (org-entry-get nil "REVIEWS") "0")) 3)
          (not (tags "alias")))
         0fc9c317d66ac204ff69d4b677b44d9df985c7ef

   (A form is printed as one line, and hashed as one line; the forms
above are wrapped only to fit the page.)

   This is the behaviour you want.  An approval names a form, and you
were shown a form; being asked again after the form changed is the
fail-safe direction.  A _refusal_ is deliberately not invalidated by it;
see *note Refused queries::.

   A hash identifies one printed form and not a query up to meaning.
‘(and (todo) (property "URL"))’ and ‘(and (property "URL") (todo))’ ask
the same question and hash differently:

     (and (todo) (property "URL"))    79a5340c2ae90ab017c6cb55cc2db16adadd6526
     (and (property "URL") (todo))    a5282dd174fe90eeae4ddd8e0f5297cceaae2029

   So an approval is narrow, which is the right way for an approval to
be wrong: reordering a query costs you one more prompt.  Where what you
want to allow or forbid is a _predicate_ rather than a particular query,
the instrument is ‘org-agents-refused-heads’ and not a hash.

   Both records are marked risky, and so is the session table.  That is
not hygiene.  Either record _is_ what the gate consults, the default
exclusion is published, and the hash of a form is computable offline --
so a file able to set one of them could pre-approve its own arbitrary
Lisp and never be asked about it.  Marked risky, Emacs asks you before a
file-local value is applied.


File: org-agents.info,  Node: Refused heads,  Next: Refused queries,  Prev: Remembered approvals,  Up: What is evaluated and what is refused

5.4 Refused heads
=================

‘org-agents-refused-heads’ is a list of predicate heads this package
will not hand org-ql, whatever else says.  It is consulted _first_:
before structural safety, before the safe list, before any remembered
approval, and ‘org-ql-ask-unsafe-queries’ does not override it.  The
switch governs whether you are asked, and a head on this list is not a
question.

   The reason such a list is needed is that a head with no dangerous
argument of its own can still be dangerous.  What a predicate head
vouches for is its own name.  org-ql runs each predicate's _normalizer_
when it compiles a query -- after the gate has admitted the head, and
before any entry is examined -- and a normalizer is arbitrary code from
wherever that predicate was defined.

   The default value lists one head, ‘semantic’, and it is a shipped
example rather than a placeholder.  ‘org-ql-semantic.el’ defines it with
a normalizer that calls ‘org-ql-semantic--ensure-cache’, which
‘call-process’-es ‘org db search’.  Structurally ‘(semantic "x")’ is a
predicate call like any other, so the structural gate admits it without
a prompt and the subprocess runs.  A refused head is where such a
predicate goes.

     org-agents: ‘semantic’ is refused by ‘org-agents-refused-heads’

   Three properties of the matching are worth stating.  It is by literal
symbol, so an alias of an IO-bearing predicate has to be listed as well
as the predicate.  It descends every cons cell, so a refused head is
reported wherever it appears -- in argument position, under a nested
query, not only as the head of the whole form.  And unlike the
structural check it does not exempt quoted data: ‘(quote (semantic
"x"))’ is refused too.  That fails closed, and the shape it costs is one
nobody writes on purpose, while what failing open costs is a subprocess.

   A refused head outranks an approval.  A query you approved last week
whose head you add to this list today is refused today, with no need to
hunt down the approval.


File: org-agents.info,  Node: Refused queries,  Next: Reviewing what you approved,  Prev: Refused heads,  Up: What is evaluated and what is refused

5.5 Refused queries
===================

‘org-agents-refused-queries’ is the negative twin of the approval
record, with the same ‘(HASH . TEXT)’ shape, so one listing can show an
approval and a refusal side by side.  A form whose hash is there is
refused before the safe list is consulted and before
‘org-ql-ask-unsafe-queries’ is looked at, and the refusal outlives the
session that made it:

     org-agents: this query is refused; see ‘org-agents-refused-queries’

   Manage it through the listing rather than by hand; see *note
Reviewing what you approved::.

   Approval and refusal are keyed on the same hash and looked up
differently, because their fail-safe directions are opposite.  An
approval that stops matching asks again.  A refusal that stops matching
_runs_.  So an approval names the whole form and nothing else, while a
refusal is looked up for the form _and_ for the query inside it -- and
refusing through the listing records both entries where the form was
built by appending the exclusion.  The consequence is the one you want:
editing ‘org-agents-exclude’ invalidates every approval and lifts no
refusal.  Verified both ways round: a form refused through the listing
under the default exclusion is still refused after the exclusion has
been edited, while an approval of the same form is not honoured after
the same edit.

   A hand-written entry covers only the exact form it names, and a hash
covers one printed form.  A refusal of ‘(and (todo) (evil))’ does not
cover ‘(and (evil) (todo))’ or ‘(and (and (todo) (evil)))’, which print
differently and hash differently.  Where what you object to is a
predicate, use ‘org-agents-refused-heads’, which refuses it however the
query around it is spelled.


File: org-agents.info,  Node: Reviewing what you approved,  Prev: Refused queries,  Up: What is evaluated and what is refused

5.6 Reviewing what you approved
===============================

The records the gate keeps are hashes, and a hash says nothing about
what it stands for.  ‘org-agents-list-approvals’ pops ‘*org-agents
approvals*’, one row per remembered decision, with three columns: the
State, the first twelve characters of the Hash, and the Query text that
hash covers.

   All three records are listed, the session table included.  A row
reading ‘approved (session)’ was approved at a prompt and not saved; it
is listed and revocable exactly like a saved one, which matters because
on a setup where customize has no file to write _every_ approval is a
session approval.  A row written by an older version that recorded no
text says ‘(query text not recorded)’ rather than showing an empty cell,
and its state is marked ‘(legacy)’ -- nothing can recover the query such
a hash stood for.

   The buffer is in ‘org-agents-approvals-mode’, derived from
‘tabulated-list-mode’, and its keys are:

‘d’ (‘org-agents-approvals-revoke’)
     Forget the approval on this line, in this session and on disk.  On
     a refusal row this says ‘org-agents: this line is a refusal; ‘u’
     lifts one’.

‘r’ (‘org-agents-approvals-refuse’)
     Forget the approval and record the form as a refusal, which the
     gate consults before anything else and which no later approval
     undoes.  Where the form carries the current exclusion, the query
     inside it is recorded too, so that editing the exclusion afterwards
     cannot lift the refusal.

‘u’ (‘org-agents-approvals-unrefuse’)
     Lift a refusal.  The query goes back to needing approval, not to
     having it: lifting a refusal is not the same as saying yes.  On an
     approval row this says ‘org-agents: this line is an approval; ‘d’
     forgets one’.

‘g’ (‘tabulated-list-revert’)
     Reread all three records.

Deciding whether to trust a file
--------------------------------

Put together, that is enough to work through a file someone sent you.

   Read the drawer first.  A query is one line and you can read it.  If
it is built only out of org-ql predicates and combinators, what it can
do is what those predicates do, and it will run without asking you -- so
the reading is the whole of your review.  Check the heads: a name you do
not recognise may be a predicate someone defined, and admitting a head
admits its normalizer.  Where you do not know a head, do not update the
agent.

   If the query holds Lisp, you will be asked, once, with the form
printed whole.  Nothing is approved by opening the file, and nothing is
approved by turning on a minor mode.  ‘org-agents-mode’ updates on save,
which means the prompt arrives when you save, not silently; and no save
ever spawns a prefilter.  Say no and the agent renders nothing and
reports ‘query not approved’.

   Look at the rest of the file too.  A query is not the only thing an
Org file can carry.  A ‘Local Variables’ block could try to set
‘org-agents-safe-queries’ to pre-approve its own Lisp, or empty
‘org-agents-refused-heads’ to remove the protection for every file read
afterwards in the session.  Every option this package defines is marked
risky for exactly that reason, so Emacs asks before applying one from a
file -- that protection is Emacs's rather than this package's, and it
depends on your answering those prompts as carefully as this one.

   An ‘:AGENT_ACTION:’ property is a separate question with a separate
answer: nothing runs it but ‘org-agents-apply-actions’, typed, and that
command shows you every intended edit before it writes one.  See *note
The dry run::.

   Finally, ‘M-x org-agents-list-approvals’ after the fact.  If you
approved something you would rather not have, ‘d’ forgets it and ‘r’
makes sure you are not asked again.


File: org-agents.info,  Node: Scope and the ripgrep prefilter,  Next: Views,  Prev: What is evaluated and what is refused,  Up: Top

6 Scope and the ripgrep prefilter
*********************************

An agent's scope says which files it reads.  Two of the five forms name
their files; the other three describe a set, and nothing about
describing a set bounds how much it holds.  This chapter is about the
difference, and about the optional ripgrep prefilter that makes the
second kind affordable.

   One sentence to hold on to throughout: the prefilter chooses which
files org-ql opens, and can never change what matches.

* Menu:

* Scopes::                       The five forms of :AGENT_SCOPE:.
* Which scopes get narrowed, and why: Which scopes get narrowed and why.  The measured cost of each kind of scope.
* What the prefilter can and cannot do:: One evaluation engine, and a candidate-file filter in front of it.
* The push-down rules::          Which conjuncts can narrow a file set, and what they compile to.
* Inheritance and pushability::  What a broad inheritance setting costs, and the one exception.
* Bounding a run::               org-agents-prefilter, org-agents-rg-executable, org-agents-rg-timeout.
* A file the scope names but cannot open:: Refused for an enumerated scope, skipped for a described one, never silently.
* Limitations of the prefilter:: What is never pushed, and what one bad file costs.


File: org-agents.info,  Node: Scopes,  Next: Which scopes get narrowed and why,  Up: Scope and the ripgrep prefilter

6.1 Scopes
==========

‘:AGENT_SCOPE:’ takes one of five forms:

‘agenda’
     ‘(org-agenda-files)’.  The default, and the one most agents want:
     it names its files, and it is the set you already keep curated.
‘active’
     Every ‘.org’ file below ‘org-directory’, recursively, skipping any
     directory named ‘archive’ and everything below it.
‘all’
     Every ‘.org’ file below ‘org-directory’, recursively, archives
     included.
a directory name
     Any other bare value is read as a directory, expanded against
     ‘org-directory’, and read recursively.  ‘.’ is the corpus root
     itself.
a list of file names
     A value beginning with ‘(’ is read as a Lisp list of file names --
     ‘("corpus.org" "notes.org")’ -- each expanded against
     ‘default-directory’, which for an agent is the directory of the
     file it lives in.

   The distinction that matters is between a scope that _enumerates_ its
files and one that _describes_ a set.  ‘agenda’ and an explicit list
enumerate: the file names exist as a list before anything is read.
‘active’, ‘all’ and a directory describe: the only way to know what they
hold is to walk the tree.  That difference decides which scopes are
prefiltered (*note Which scopes get narrowed and why::) and how an
unreadable file is reported (*note A file the scope names but cannot
open::).

   A scope that names no directory is refused, before anything is
spawned, so that a mistyped scope is diagnosed as one rather than as a
prefilter failure:

     org-agents: scope ‘nowhere’ names no directory (/Users/you/org/nowhere)

   and a value that is neither a known name, a directory nor a list of
strings is named as the bad scope it is -- ‘:AGENT_SCOPE: (1 2)’ gets
‘org-agents: bad scope (1 2)’ rather than a type error from further
along.  A scope that resolves to no files selects nothing; it does not
fall back to searching the buffer the agent lives in.

   ‘org-agents-files’ is a different thing and the two are easy to
confuse.  A scope says which files one agent _reads_.
‘org-agents-files’ says which files ‘org-agents-update-all’ opens and
_writes_.  See *note By hand::.


File: org-agents.info,  Node: Which scopes get narrowed and why,  Next: What the prefilter can and cannot do,  Prev: Scopes,  Up: Scope and the ripgrep prefilter

6.2 Which scopes get narrowed, and why
======================================

Only ‘active’, ‘all’ and a directory scope are ever prefiltered.
‘agenda’ and an explicit file list are always read live.  Both halves of
that were measured on the author's corpus rather than assumed.

   For an enumerated scope, a prefilter is pure overhead.  Measured over
the eight largest files of that corpus, 8.9 MB and 373 matches: an
‘agenda’-scope update costs 0.03 to 0.05 seconds once the buffers are
visited, and spawns no subprocess at all.  One ripgrep run over the
whole corpus costs 0.10 to 0.45 seconds.  Prefiltering a set that is
already small and already named makes the common case 5 to 25 times
slower to reach the same answer.

   For a described scope the numbers are of a different kind.  Measured
with ‘(and (todo) (property "NEXT_REVIEW"))’ over an ‘active’ scope: one
ripgrep run narrows 3,634 files to 309 in half a second, and org-ql over
those 309 finishes in ten seconds with 764 matches over 316 buffers.
With no prefilter at all, the same query had not finished after nine
minutes.  The same experiment on a 3,669-file corpus narrowed to 314
files in 0.09 seconds of ripgrep and finished in 12 seconds.

   Do not budget the unprefiltered path in seconds.  It is not a slower
way to the same place within one attention span; it is the thing the
option exists to avoid.  (Longer still at a low ‘gc-cons-threshold’ --
past nine minutes at the batch default -- since opening that many Org
buffers makes garbage faster than the default budget expects.)

   Read those figures as an order of magnitude and not as a benchmark.
Machine load moves them: the package's own measurement of one ripgrep
census over one scope came out at 3.5 seconds on an idle machine and
12.8 on one busy with other work.  A figure outside a stated range is
load rather than a regression.

   One sharpening that is _not_ implemented, so that you do not look for
it: there is no file-count ceiling above which a live walk is refused
and below which it is allowed.  The choice is per-scope-kind, not
per-size.


