6.3 What the prefilter can and cannot do
========================================

Evaluation is always org-ql's, against live buffers.  There is exactly
one evaluation engine and therefore exactly one answer.  ripgrep is a
candidate-_file_ prefilter: it chooses which files org-ql opens, and
nothing else.

   That asymmetry sets the whole design.  Narrowing too little is merely
slow.  Narrowing too much is a wrong answer with no error at all -- an
agent that renders fewer matches than it has, and says nothing about it.
So a conjunct is pushed into ripgrep only where the files ripgrep
answers with are provably a _superset_ of the files org-ql's own matches
could come from, and whatever cannot be proven a superset stays
residual.  See *note The push-down rules::.

   ripgrep distinguishes "no file matches" from "I could not answer" by
exit status, and this package keeps the two apart.  An empty answer is
an answer: an agent over a corpus scope whose query genuinely matches
nothing renders zero matches, rather than reporting a broken prefilter.
Only a real failure -- no executable, any exit status but 0 or 1, a
death by signal, an expired deadline -- means "no answer", and then the
scope is scanned live.  A failing run says so on its own account first,
naming the status and quoting whatever the program wrote to standard
error:

     org-agents: /usr/local/bin/rg exited 2: …

   Four conditions send a described scope down the live path, and each
says which it was:

     org-agents: scope ‘.’ not narrowed (prefiltering off); scanning 9 files live
     org-agents: scope ‘.’ not narrowed (no pushable conjunct); scanning 9 files live
     org-agents: scope ‘.’ not narrowed (ripgrep not found); scanning 9 files live
     org-agents: scope ‘.’ not narrowed (ripgrep failed); scanning 9 files live

   The file count is in the message because the slowness should be
explained rather than mysterious: on the author's corpus that walk is
3,634 files and a query of 172 seconds, which is a shocking thing to
happen without a reason attached.

   So: ‘org-agents-prefilter’ changes what an agent costs and never what
it answers.  If you suspect it of changing an answer, set it to nil and
compare -- the two runs should agree, and a disagreement is a bug in
this package rather than a setting to be tuned.


