6.4 The push-down rules
=======================

The analysis is in two layers on purpose.  The splitter decides _which_
conjuncts are superset-safe, in an abstract vocabulary; the emitter
decides _how_ ripgrep expresses one, or declines to.  A pattern that
cannot be written soundly is not written, and the conjunct then
contributes no narrowing -- slow and correct rather than fast and wrong.

   Seven predicate heads can push:

     property   property-resolved   property-ts
     scheduled  deadline   closed   heading

   and only the top-level conjuncts of an ‘and’ are considered -- or the
whole query, where it is a single pushable predicate.  A conjunct under
‘or’ or ‘not’, and every nested query, is residual by omission, which
widens the candidate set and is therefore safe.

   Here is the splitter and the emitter over real queries, with the
patterns ripgrep is actually handed:

     (and (todo "TODO") (property "NEXT_REVIEW"))
         (property "NEXT_REVIEW")        ^[ \t]*:NEXT_REVIEW\+?:
     (property "URL" "https://x")
         (property "URL" "https://x")    ^[ \t]*:URL\+?:[ \t]+https://x[ \t]*$
     (and (scheduled :to "+7d") (property "OWNER"))
         (scheduled)                     SCHEDULED:[ \t]*<
         (property "OWNER")              ^[ \t]*:OWNER\+?:
     (deadline :from -7 :to 7)
         (deadline)                      DEADLINE:[ \t]*<
     (closed :on "2026-08-20")
         (closed)                        CLOSED:[ \t]*\[
     (heading "Tinderbox")
         (heading "Tinderbox")           ^\*+(?-u:.)*Tinderbox
     (heading "Ship it (finally)")
         (heading "Ship it (finally)")   ^\*+(?-u:.)*Ship it \(finally\)
     (property-ts "NEXT_REVIEW" :from -7)
         (property "NEXT_REVIEW")        ^[ \t]*:NEXT_REVIEW\+?:
     (and (property "OWNER" "a b") (todo))
         (property "OWNER")              ^[ \t]*:OWNER\+?:
     (property "CATEGORY" "work")        nothing pushed
     (or (property "URL") (property "LOCATION"))
                                         nothing pushed
     (string-match "gh" (or (org-entry-get nil "URL") ""))
                                         nothing pushed
     (heading "Café")                    conjunct pushed, no pattern emitted

   Read that table with these rules in mind.

   A property _name_ pushes as the existence of a drawer line, and the
‘\+?’ is exact rather than generous: Org looks up ‘:NAME:’ and ‘:NAME+:’
and nothing else, and an entry whose drawer holds only ‘:NEXT_REVIEW+:
plusvalue’ does match ‘(property "NEXT_REVIEW")’.

   A property _value_ pushes only where one drawer line spells it.
‘org-entry-get’ builds a value by joining the ‘:NAME:’ line with every
‘:NAME+:’ line, separated by ‘org--property-get-separator’, so a value
assembled from more than one line is spelled on no line of the file and
a line-oriented matcher cannot find it.  Where that cannot be argued --
‘(property "OWNER" "a b")’ above -- the name arm is pushed instead,
which is wider and always sound.  Note what the rule is _not_: it is not
a test for whitespace.  With a separator of ‘/’, ‘:P: al’ plus ‘:P+:
pha’ answers ‘al/pha’, a value holding no whitespace that no single line
spells.

   A special property never pushes.  ‘org-entry-get’ answers ‘CATEGORY’,
‘TODO’, ‘DEADLINE’ and the rest out of entry structure, and no drawer
holds a line for one, so pushing it would return no files at all for a
query that matches thousands.

   A planning bound is never pushed, only the presence of the stamp:
ripgrep cannot compare dates.  So ‘(deadline)’, ‘(deadline :from -7 :to
7)’, ‘(deadline 7)’ and ‘(deadline auto)’ all push the same thing, all
soundly, and org-ql applies the bound afterwards.  The opening bracket
in the pattern is not decoration -- an active timestamp begins with ‘<’
and an inactive one with ‘[’, and demanding it narrowed 216, 91 and 18
candidate files down to 203, 76 and 3 over a 3,669-file corpus.

   A ‘heading’ argument is always a literal, never a regexp: org-ql
normalizes ‘heading’ to ‘heading-regexp’ with every argument
‘regexp-quote’-d.  The emitter therefore quotes for ripgrep's Rust
dialect instead -- note ‘\(finally\)’ above, where an Emacs-style
quoting would have left the parentheses as grouping and demanded the
text ‘Ship it finally’.  The splitter refuses one character in a heading
literal and only one: ‘]’.  ‘org-get-heading’ does not return a slice of
the line -- it reassembles it from the groups of a regexp -- and the
only substring org-ql can match that the raw line need not spell is one
crossing the junction between a priority cookie and the title.  Every
such substring holds the cookie's closing ‘]’.  (The emitter has
refusals of its own, over any literal; see *note Limitations of the
prefilter::.)  Several literals of one ‘heading’ are emitted as several
patterns and _intersected_, because org-ql requires all of them in one
heading.

   The authority for all of this is ‘org-agents--pushdown-fns’ in the
source, whose every row states its own superset argument.  One warning
for anyone reading the repository's design notes: ‘docs/design.md’
carries a push-down table describing a PostgreSQL prefilter this package
no longer has, and its per-row justifications are facts about SQL
operators that no longer run.  Extending the rules on those arguments
can push something ripgrep cannot see, which drops files with no error.


