6.5 Inheritance and pushability
===============================

A broad ‘org-use-property-inheritance’ -- ‘t’ most of all -- makes no
‘property’ or ‘property-ts’ conjunct pushable at all.  An agent built
only out of those then falls back to its scope's whole file set: still
correct, and much slower.  Measured, with the option at ‘t’:

     (and (todo "TODO") (property "NEXT_REVIEW"))    nothing pushed

   So the prefilter's value depends on keeping inheritance narrow.  If
you have an agent over a corpus scope that is unaccountably slow, that
option is the first thing to look at.

   Two honest notes about that guard.  It is conservative rather than
required: org-ql applies the ‘org-use-property-inheritance’ default only
to a ‘property’ form that carries an extra plist, and the plain
‘(property NAME)’ and ‘(property NAME VALUE)’ forms -- the only two that
are ever pushed -- read the entry's own drawer, which is exactly what a
‘:NAME:’ line is.  The guard is kept anyway, because org-ql's own
‘property’ docstring reads the other way, and if a future org-ql made
that true of the plain forms this guard is the only thing between that
change and a silent wrong answer.

   ‘property-resolved’ is the deliberate exception, and it stays
pushable at every setting:

     (property-resolved "OWNER")   (?:^[ \t]*:OWNER\+?:|^[ \t]*:PROTOTYPE\+?:)

   Two things there.  The resolver never reads
‘org-use-property-inheritance’ and never passes an INHERIT argument, so
that option cannot change what the predicate answers -- keeping the
guard would be a pure loss of narrowing.  And the pattern is _widened_:
the local ‘:NAME:’ line OR a ‘:PROTOTYPE:’ line, as one alternation.  It
has to be.  An entry that resolves a name through a master never spells
the value, so the ordinary pattern would drop its file with no error,
which is the one failure mode this package must not have.  (The
alternation is one pattern rather than two on purpose: several patterns
of one conjunct are intersected, so two would mean "spells ‘:NAME:’ AND
carries ‘:PROTOTYPE:’" -- catastrophically narrow, and silent.)

   That widening has an exception of its own, and it is where a declared
default meets a query.  With an ‘:ATTR_DEFAULT:’ declared that the query
could match, an entry spelling _neither_ line resolves the name too --
so nothing can narrow, and the conjunct stays residual.  Measured
against a registry declaring ‘STATUS’ with ‘:ATTR_DEFAULT: active’:

     (property-resolved "STATUS" "blocked")
         (?:^[ \t]*:STATUS\+?:[ \t]+blocked[ \t]*$|^[ \t]*:PROTOTYPE\+?:)
     (property-resolved "STATUS" "active")     nothing pushed
     (property-resolved "OWNER")               pushed  (OWNER declares no default)

   ‘blocked’ is not the declared default, so only an entry that spells a
line can answer it and the alternation is sound.  ‘active’ is the
declared default, so every entry in the corpus resolves ‘STATUS’ to
something the query matches, and no file can be excluded.  The exception
costs narrowing and never an answer.  For the resolution order this
rests on, see *note The resolution order::; for the wider price of
virtual resolution, *note Reads are virtual::.


