6.6 Bounding a run
==================

Three options govern the prefilter.

   ‘org-agents-prefilter’ takes one of three values.  ‘auto’, the
default, uses ripgrep where the executable is found and the query offers
a conjunct to push, and otherwise scans live with the message naming the
reason and the file count.  nil never runs ripgrep.  ‘require’ refuses
an unbounded scope that cannot be narrowed, rather than scanning it
live:

     org-agents: scope ‘.’ cannot be narrowed (no pushable conjunct) and
     ‘org-agents-prefilter’ is ‘require’; for live evaluation set it to
     ‘auto’, or use ‘agenda’ or an explicit file list

   That is for someone who would rather be told an agent cannot be
answered affordably than wait for a live walk of the corpus.  ‘require’
refuses a _query_, and one caller is deliberately outside it:
‘org-agents-check-attributes’ scans live rather than refusing, with the
same message naming the file count, because a lint that declined to run
would be failing its own contract rather than declining an expense on
your behalf.  See *note Checking a corpus::.

   ‘org-agents-rg-executable’ names the program, resolved with
‘executable-find’, so a bare name is looked up on ‘exec-path’.  ripgrep
13 or later is wanted: the prefilter passes ‘--crlf’, without which a
value pattern cannot match in a file with CRLF line endings.  Every flag
was verified against 15.2.0 and against no other version; 13 is named as
a round, safely old floor.

   One thing that program will _not_ see: ‘RIPGREP_CONFIG_PATH’ is unset
for the child, and that is a soundness requirement rather than tidiness.
ripgrep prepends every argument in that file to its command line, and
the vector this package builds overrides only the flags it repeats.
Measured, with a config file holding one line: under ‘--max-depth=1’ a
matching file one directory down is not reported, and under
‘--max-filesize=10’ nothing is reported at all and ripgrep exits 1 --
which is correctly read as "an answer, and no file matches", so every
agent renders nothing with no error and no message.  A personal ripgrep
default is the mechanism ripgrep's own documentation recommends, and it
would silently empty every unbounded agent.

   ‘org-agents-rg-timeout’ is the number of seconds to wait for one run,
or nil for no bound.  A bound is needed because nothing else provides
one: on an unresponsive filesystem -- NFS, sshfs, a sleeping disk -- or
under ‘--follow’ over a pathological symlink arrangement, a run can take
arbitrarily long.

   A timer cannot rescue one, and that is why this is a deadline over an
asynchronous process rather than a timer over a synchronous call.
Measured: a ‘run-at-time’ set before a ‘call-process’ to a hanging child
never fires, because Emacs runs no timers while blocked there; one set
before a loop over ‘accept-process-output’ fires at +0.57 s.
‘timeout(1)’ was the other candidate and was rejected twice over --
‘/usr/bin/timeout’ does not exist on stock macOS, and it bounds the
_child_ rather than Emacs, so against a process in an uninterruptible
disk wait, which is the very case this is for, Emacs would stay blocked
waiting for ‘timeout’ to reap something that will not die.

   The default of 30 seconds is roughly fifty times the slowest run
measured for any pattern this package builds over a 3,649-file corpus:
0.564 s cold and 0.068 to 0.083 s warm.  On expiry the run answers
"unavailable", which is what a missing ripgrep answers, so the scope is
scanned live.  A spurious expiry therefore costs a slow correct answer
and never a wrong one, which is what makes having a bound safe at all.
With the bound off, ‘C-g’ is the only escape.

   The new cost of an asynchronous wait is reentrancy, and it is worth
stating plainly rather than leaving to be met.  Waiting on a subprocess
runs timers and process filters, where ‘call-process’ ran nothing, so an
update may execute an idle timer while its prefilter is in flight.  Two
mitigations cover most of it: an update of a ‘children’ agent collects
_outside_ its ‘atomic-change-group’, so no timer's edits can land in
this package's change group, and no save spawns a prefilter at all.

   What that does not cover: a ‘list’ or ‘table’ agent renders from
Org's dynamic-block writer, which Org calls after the block's body has
already been deleted.  The wait therefore happens with the block
emptied, and an idle timer that edits the buffer at that spot during the
wait either has its text glued into the freshly rendered body or loses
it when the writer's failure path puts the old body back.  Demonstrated
with a one-second stub and a 0.4 second timer: the timer ran, found the
body already gone, and its line came back inside the rendered item.
Hoisting that collect too would mean parsing the block's parameters
ahead of Org and matching its parser exactly, which trades a narrow
exposure for a silent divergence, so it is not done.  ‘C-g’ escapes, and
a corpus-scope agent updated by hand is the only way to reach the wait
at all.

   All three options are marked risky, and the last two for concrete
reasons: ‘org-agents-rg-executable’ names a program that will be run as
a subprocess, and a file-local ‘org-agents-rg-timeout’ of nil takes off
the bound this exists to provide while a file-local 0 expires every run
and sends every corpus-scope agent down the live whole-corpus walk.


