6.7 A file the scope names but cannot open
==========================================

A file that nothing can open is reported by this package rather than by
org-ql, and the two kinds of scope get different answers.

   An explicit ‘:AGENT_SCOPE:’ file list is a hard refusal, naming the
files:

     org-agents: :AGENT_SCOPE: names 1 file(s) that cannot be read, so this
     agent is not answered: …/notes.org

   You named this file in this agent.  Rendering a smaller answer with
nothing to say it was smaller is the failure mode this package refuses
everywhere else.

   ‘agenda’, a directory, ‘active’ and ‘all’ skip, with one message
naming the scope and the files:

     org-agents: scope ‘.’ names 1 file(s) that cannot be read; they are
     skipped: …/.#agents.org

   These describe a set rather than enumerating it -- ‘org-agenda-files’
is a list Org maintains and filters -- so one stale entry, or one
root-owned stray in the corpus, must not break every agent in the
buffer.  At most five names are spelled out and the rest are counted,
because the usual case is one stale path and naming it is the whole
point, while a root-owned corpus could put thousands in one message.

   Neither way is silent.  The message is the whole difference between a
skip and a drop.

   What this replaced is worth knowing, because you may still meet it in
an older report.  Handed a file it cannot open, ‘org-ql-select’ calls
‘display-warning’, which _returns_ the warning text as a string; that
string is left in the list org-ql then maps ‘buffer-name’ over, so the
diagnostic used to arrive as the payload of a type error -- ‘Wrong type
argument: bufferp, "Error (org-ql-select): Can not open file: …"’ -- and
one bad path made every agent in the buffer fail with what read like a
bug in this package.

   The test is ‘(or (file-readable-p f) (find-buffer-visiting f))’ and
both halves are needed: measured, a file visited while readable and then
‘chmod 000’ has ‘file-readable-p’ nil, ‘find-buffer-visiting’ non-nil,
and org-ql over it works and returns its match.  This mirrors org-ql's
own rule rather than inventing a stricter one.  Its cost, measured over
a 3,639-file ‘active’ scope, is 0.465 seconds, against 0.265 for the
directory walk that produced the list and the ten-odd seconds org-ql
then spends opening those files -- so it is roughly twice the walk and
negligible against the read it precedes, which is the comparison that
matters.

   This is a fallback-path diagnostic only.  ripgrep cannot read an
unreadable file either, so a narrowed scope never reports one; what it
does instead is the subject of the next node.


