6.8 Limitations of the prefilter
================================

Each of these costs candidate files or a whole run, and none of them can
cost a match.

   A literal is pushed only where every character in it is printable
ASCII.  ripgrep decodes as UTF-8 while Emacs may decode an Org file as
latin-1 through a coding cookie or ‘file-coding-system-alist’: measured,
a latin-1 file holding ‘* Cafe Review’ with an accented ‘e’ matches the
ASCII pattern ‘Caf’ and does not match the UTF-8 pattern for the
accented spelling.  No flag fixes it, since ‘--encoding’ is one global
setting and a corpus is mixed.  Such a conjunct emits no pattern, which
costs breadth and never an answer.

   A planning bound is never pushed, only the presence of the stamp;
org-ql applies the bound.  See *note The push-down rules::.

   A broad ‘org-use-property-inheritance’ removes narrowing from every
‘property’ and ‘property-ts’ conjunct.  See *note Inheritance and
pushability::.

   Only the top-level conjuncts of an ‘and’ are considered.  A query
whose whole shape is a disjunction -- ‘(or (property "URL") (property
"LOCATION"))’ -- pushes nothing, even though each arm would push on its
own.

   One unreadable file under the root costs the entire run.  Measured:
with one unreadable file among two, ripgrep prints the readable match,
writes ‘Permission denied’ to stderr, _and_ exits 2 -- and a partial
answer is exactly the unsound direction, so the whole answer is
discarded and the scope is scanned live.  A symlink loop does the same
by the same route, since ‘--follow’ over one makes ripgrep exit 2.
Either way every agent over that root falls back to a live scan until
the file or the loop is dealt with: slow, correct, and reported as
‘ripgrep failed’.

   Finally, the intersection of ripgrep's answer with the scope's own
file list depends on ripgrep's traversal not being narrower than
‘directory-files-recursively’'s.  That is what ‘--hidden’,
‘--no-ignore’, ‘--follow’ and an inclusive ‘--iglob '*.org'’ are in the
argument vector for: ripgrep honours ‘.gitignore’ by default and a
corpus is commonly a git repository, ripgrep skips dot-directories by
default and the Emacs walk descends into them, and the Emacs walk
matches its regexp with ‘case-fold-search’ so ‘NOTES.ORG’ is in the base
set.  A file ripgrep failed to visit would be a match lost with no
error, and those flags are the guarantee against it rather than
something the intersection makes safe.


