7 Views
*******

An agent's query says which entries it is interested in.  Its _view_
says what to write down about them.  There are three, and they differ
less in what they can express than in where they put it: the ‘children’
view writes into the outline, as headings under the agent, while the
‘list’ and ‘table’ views write inside a dynamic block and never touch
the outline around it.

   That difference is the one to choose on.  A ‘children’ view puts the
matches where Org's own outline machinery can reach them, so you can
fold them, refile beside them, annotate one, or let a second agent match
them; the price is that the agent owns a region of your outline and
rewrites it on every update.  A ‘list’ or ‘table’ view owns nothing but
the inside of its block, and can be dropped into the middle of prose.

   Evaluation does not change with the view.  All three ask the same
question of the same engine and get the same matches; only the writing
down differs.

* Menu:

* Choosing a view::              :AGENT_VIEW:, and the three values it takes.
* The children view::            The default: one child heading per match.
* The alias contract::           Which aliases the package owns, and which are yours.
* The list view::                An Org plain list inside a dynamic block.
* The table view::               :AGENT_COLUMNS:, ITEM_BY_ID, and one row per match.
* Formats::                      :AGENT_FORMAT:, and where its suffix appears.
* Sorting and limiting::         :AGENT_SORT: and :AGENT_LIMIT:, and which sorts a table owns.
* Limitations of the views::     What the renderers do not preserve.


