7.2 The children view
=====================

This is Tinderbox's arrangement: the agent is a container, and what it
finds appears inside it.  Each match becomes a child heading holding a
link to the match, one level below the agent, carrying ‘:AGENT_MATCH: t’
in its own drawer.  Following the tradition, such a heading is called an
_alias_.

   *note Your first agent:: builds one of these from nothing; what
follows is the rendering itself, in full.  An agent and an update
produce this:

     * Up for review
       :PROPERTIES:
       :AGENT_QUERY: (and (todo "TODO") (property "NEXT_REVIEW"))
       :AGENT_MATCHED: 3 [2026-08-20 Thu 17:46]
       :END:
     ** [[id:rewrite-exporter][Rewrite the exporter]]
     :PROPERTIES:
     :AGENT_MATCH: t
     :END:
     ** [[id:audit-prefilter][Audit the prefilter]]
     :PROPERTIES:
     :AGENT_MATCH: t
     :END:
     ** [[id:renew-passport][Renew the passport]]
     :PROPERTIES:
     :AGENT_MATCH: t
     :END:

   Three things in that output are worth naming.

   The link is an ‘id:’ link where the match carries an ‘:ID:’, and
building it registers that id in ‘org-id-locations’ so the link resolves
later.  A match with no ‘:ID:’ is linked as ‘file:PATH::*Heading’
instead, using Org's own headline-search syntax.  A match with neither
an id nor a file to link to cannot be linked at all: it is rendered as
its heading text with ‘(?)’ after it, because a link that goes nowhere
is worse than text saying there is none.

   The alias's description is the match's heading _as it read when the
alias was written_.  It is text, not a live proxy; see *note The alias
contract::.

   ‘:AGENT_MATCHED:’ is the agent's own note of what it last found and
when.  See *note What writing AGENT_MATCHED costs::.

   Because this is the only view that writes outside a dynamic block, it
is the only one that has to answer for what it deletes.  That answer is
the alias contract, and it is the next node.


