7.3 The alias contract
======================

An update rewrites the agent's children.  For that to be safe, there has
to be a rule saying which children it may delete, and the rule has to be
one you can predict without reading the code.  It is this:

   • An alias carrying ‘:AGENT_MATCH: t’ and holding _nothing but that
     drawer_ is *pristine*.  It belongs to the package.  Every update
     deletes it and writes it again.

   • The moment you write anything under it, it is *yours*.  No update
     deletes it.  When its match is gone, the alias gets ‘(stale)’
     appended to its title instead.

   • A child that never carried ‘:AGENT_MATCH: t’ is not the package's
     to touch at all.

   • An alias is recognized by the target it links to, never by its
     description.  The description is the match's old heading text, and
     headings drift.

   Here is the whole contract in two updates.  After one update,
annotate the second alias:

     * Up for review
       :PROPERTIES:
       :AGENT_QUERY: (and (todo "TODO") (property "NEXT_REVIEW"))
       :AGENT_MATCHED: 3 [2026-08-20 Thu 17:52]
       :END:
     ** [[id:rewrite-exporter][Rewrite the exporter]]
     :PROPERTIES:
     :AGENT_MATCH: t
     :END:
     ** [[id:audit-prefilter][Audit the prefilter]]
     :PROPERTIES:
     :AGENT_MATCH: t
     :END:
     Why this one matters: it is the slow one.
     ** [[id:renew-passport][Renew the passport]]
     :PROPERTIES:
     :AGENT_MATCH: t
     :END:

   Now narrow the query to ‘(and (todo "TODO") (property "URL"))’, so
that ‘Audit the prefilter’ no longer matches, and update again:

     * Up for review
       :PROPERTIES:
       :AGENT_QUERY: (and (todo "TODO") (property "URL"))
       :AGENT_MATCHED: 2 [2026-08-20 Thu 17:52]
       :END:
     ** [[id:audit-prefilter][Audit the prefilter]] (stale)
     :PROPERTIES:
     :AGENT_MATCH: t
     :END:
     Why this one matters: it is the slow one.
     ** [[id:rewrite-exporter][Rewrite the exporter]]
     :PROPERTIES:
     :AGENT_MATCH: t
     :END:
     ** [[id:renew-passport][Renew the passport]]
     :PROPERTIES:
     :AGENT_MATCH: t
     :END:

   Your note survived, and the alias it was written under is marked
rather than removed.  Notice also that the annotated alias is now
_first_: the two pristine ones were deleted and written again at the end
of the subtree, while the one you adopted stayed exactly where it was.
Annotating an alias moves it to the top of the list, in effect, and
keeps it there.

   A match that comes back unmarks the alias its absence marked, so the
stale mark answers for this update and not for an older one.

   Four consequences follow from the contract, and each is worth knowing
before you rely on it.

  1. Because a pristine alias is deleted rather than edited, extra
     drawer properties or tags you add to a body-less alias are
     *discarded* by the next update.  Write a line of text under the
     alias and they are kept along with it.

  2. A preserved alias keeps the description and the format suffix it
     was written with.  Only the stale mark changes.  So an alias whose
     match has since been renamed goes on showing the old title.

  3. An alias is recognized by its target.  A match that *gains an
     ‘:ID:’* between two updates is therefore a different target: the
     old alias is marked stale and a new one is written beside it.

  4. Two matches with no ‘:ID:’ and identical headings in one file share
     one ‘file:...::*Heading’ target, so a single alias may stand for
     both while ‘:AGENT_MATCHED:’ counts two.  Give them ids to tell
     them apart.

   An alias whose link you have edited past reading is left alone, and a
fresh alias is written beside it.  That is a duplicate, and it is
accepted: the alternative is guessing which match a mangled link stood
for.


