7.5 The table view
==================

A table is what you want when the answer is not "which entries" but
"which entries, and what do they say".  Name the columns in
‘:AGENT_COLUMNS:’, whitespace separated:

     * Review queue
       :PROPERTIES:
       :AGENT_QUERY:   (and (todo "TODO") (property "NEXT_REVIEW"))
       :AGENT_VIEW:    table
       :AGENT_COLUMNS: ITEM_BY_ID NEXT_REVIEW REVIEWS
       :AGENT_SORT:    (ts-column 2)
       :AGENT_MATCHED: 3 [2026-08-20 Thu 17:50]
       :END:
     #+BEGIN: org-agents
     | ITEM_BY_ID           | NEXT_REVIEW | REVIEWS |
     |----------------------+-------------+---------|
     | [[id:renew-passport][Renew the passport]]   |  2026-08-21 |         |
     | [[id:audit-prefilter][Audit the prefilter]]  |  2026-08-25 |       2 |
     | [[id:rewrite-exporter][Rewrite the exporter]] |  2026-09-01 |       5 |
     #+END:

   One column name is special.  ‘ITEM_BY_ID’ is the link to the match,
and it may appear anywhere in the list, not only first.  Every other
name is read as a property at the match's own heading.  With
‘:AGENT_COLUMNS:’ absent you get ‘ITEM_BY_ID’ alone, which is the one
column no property could have supplied:

     * Everything with a location
       :PROPERTIES:
       :AGENT_QUERY: (property "LOCATION")
       :AGENT_VIEW:  table
       :AGENT_MATCHED: 2 [2026-08-20 Thu 17:50]
       :END:
     #+BEGIN: org-agents
     | ITEM_BY_ID         |
     |--------------------|
     | [[id:renew-passport][Renew the passport]] |
     | [[id:collect-print][Collect the print]]  |
     #+END:

   A name nothing at the match has set gives an empty cell, silently:
‘REVIEWS’ is empty for ‘Renew the passport’ in the first table above
because that entry has no such property.  That is a property nothing
set, not an error, and it is the same answer a column view would give.
A match this package cannot locate has no entry to read properties at,
so all of its cells but the link are empty.

   Two mechanical details, so the output is not surprising.  A ‘|’ in a
cell value is escaped to ‘\vert{}’, because a bar would read as the
boundary of a cell that is not there and would make the row one column
wider than the table.  And the rule is written literally as ‘|-|’ with
no cell padded: the renderer writes the table and then asks Org to align
it, which is the only place a column width can be measured.  If
alignment fails, the table is left unaligned rather than the whole
update being taken down with it.

   The table view has no format suffix; ‘:AGENT_COLUMNS:’ is the table's
version of that idea.  See *note Formats::.


File: org-agents.info,  Node: Formats,  Next: Sorting and limiting,  Prev: The table view,  Up: Views

7.6 Formats
===========

‘:AGENT_FORMAT:’ names properties to show _after the link_, so you can
read a value without opening the entry it belongs to.  It is a
whitespace-separated list of property names, and it applies to the
‘children’ and ‘list’ views only.

     * Up for review, in date order
       :PROPERTIES:
       :AGENT_QUERY:  (and (todo "TODO") (property "NEXT_REVIEW"))
       :AGENT_SORT:   date
       :AGENT_FORMAT: NEXT_REVIEW
       :AGENT_MATCHED: 3 [2026-08-20 Thu 17:50]
       :END:
     ** [[id:rewrite-exporter][Rewrite the exporter]] 2026-09-01
     :PROPERTIES:
     :AGENT_MATCH: t
     :END:
     ** [[id:audit-prefilter][Audit the prefilter]] 2026-08-25
     :PROPERTIES:
     :AGENT_MATCH: t
     :END:
     ** [[id:renew-passport][Renew the passport]] 2026-08-21
     :PROPERTIES:
     :AGENT_MATCH: t
     :END:

   The suffix goes on the alias heading in the ‘children’ view and after
the link in a ‘list’ item.  It has no place in a ‘table’ view, where
columns do the same work with a header to name them.

   Two properties of the reading are easy to trip over.

   The values are read with *no inheritance*.  A format naming ‘OWNER’
shows nothing for a match whose ‘:OWNER:’ is written on an ancestor,
even though that ancestor's value is what a query written ‘$OWNER*’
would see.  See *note The two axes:: for the two axes and what each of
them reaches.

   Values are joined with two spaces, and a property the match does not
have contributes the empty string.  A format naming two properties where
the match has only the first therefore leaves *two trailing spaces* on
the line.  Either keep a format to the properties your matches actually
carry, or expect the whitespace.  A suffix that is entirely empty
contributes nothing at all, not even the space that would have separated
it from the link.


