7.7 Sorting and limiting
========================

There are two kinds of sort, and the distinction is not decoration: one
orders _matches_ and is org-ql's, the other orders _rendered rows_ and
is this package's.

   ‘:AGENT_SORT:’ takes one of ‘date’, ‘todo’, ‘priority’, ‘reverse’, or
a list of those.  Those are org-ql's own sorting methods, applied to the
matched entries before anything is rendered, and they work in every
view.

   ‘:AGENT_SORT:’ also takes ‘(column N)’ and ‘(ts-column N)’, which
order the rows of a table: ‘(column N)’ compares the rendered cells as
strings, ‘(ts-column N)’ reads them as timestamps.  ‘N’ is 1-indexed
over ‘:AGENT_COLUMNS:’.  A cell naming no time sorts after every cell
that does, because an entry with no date is not an entry dated the
epoch.  These two are *table-only*, and asking for one in another view
is refused:

     (column 1) sorts table rows, but the view is ‘children’

   as is a column number naming no column:

     :AGENT_SORT: no column 4 among 3 columns

   A value that is neither kind is refused by name, with the whole
vocabulary in the message:

     cannot sort by ‘dtae’; expected date, todo, priority, reverse, a list of
     those, or (column N)/(ts-column N)

   ‘:AGENT_LIMIT:’ is a count, and it is applied *after* the sort.  That
ordering is what makes "the two nearest reviews" expressible:

     * The two nearest reviews
       :PROPERTIES:
       :AGENT_QUERY:   (property "NEXT_REVIEW")
       :AGENT_VIEW:    table
       :AGENT_COLUMNS: ITEM_BY_ID NEXT_REVIEW
       :AGENT_SORT:    (ts-column 2)
       :AGENT_LIMIT:   2
       :AGENT_MATCHED: 2 [2026-08-20 Thu 17:50]
       :END:
     #+BEGIN: org-agents
     | ITEM_BY_ID          | NEXT_REVIEW |
     |---------------------+-------------|
     | [[id:renew-passport][Renew the passport]]  |  2026-08-21 |
     | [[id:audit-prefilter][Audit the prefilter]] |  2026-08-25 |
     #+END:

   Those really are the two nearest, and not the first two org-ql
happened to find.  With a row sort the limit is applied by the table
renderer, after the rows exist to be ordered; without one it is applied
by the collector.  Either way the limit comes last.

   A limit that is not a count is refused rather than read charitably:

     :AGENT_LIMIT: must be a count, not ‘many’

   ‘string-to-number’ would read ‘many’ as 0, and an agent rendering
nothing looks exactly like an agent that matched nothing.


File: org-agents.info,  Node: Limitations of the views,  Prev: Sorting and limiting,  Up: Views

7.8 Limitations of the views
============================

*Drawer edits to a pristine alias are discarded.*  This is the sharp
edge of the alias contract and it is worth restating on its own, because
it is the one that looks like data loss.  An alias holding nothing but
‘:AGENT_MATCH: t’ is deleted and written again by every update, so a
property or a tag you add to it is gone at the next update, with nothing
said about it.  Write one line of text under the alias first and the
whole heading, drawer and tags included, is preserved from then on.

   *A hand-edited block body does not round-trip byte-exactly.*  The
renderer keeps a copy of the previous body so that a failed render can
put it back (see *note Limitations of dynamic blocks::).  That copy is
exact for a body a successful render wrote.  For a body you edited by
hand it is not quite: a trailing blank line is lost, and a hand-indented
line comes back at the block's own indentation column.

   *A table's alignment is cosmetic and is not guaranteed.*  Alignment
happens after the table is in the buffer, and a failure there is
reported and stepped over rather than allowed to abort the update.  An
unaligned table is a correct table.


File: org-agents.info,  Node: Dynamic blocks,  Next: Updating,  Prev: Views,  Up: Top

8 Dynamic blocks
****************

The ‘list’ and ‘table’ views write into an Org dynamic block whose type
is ‘org-agents’.  Using Org's own mechanism rather than a new one buys
three things: the block delimits exactly what the render owns, Org's
existing commands update it, and a block can carry parameters, which a
property drawer cannot.

   The parameters matter more than they first appear.  A block's
parameters override the enclosing agent's properties name by name, so
one agent can carry several views of itself; and a block with its own
‘:query’ parameter needs no agent at all, which is how you drop a query
into a prose document that has no properties to hang one on.

* Menu:

* Inserting a block::            org-agents-insert-dblock and C-c C-x x.
* Block parameters::             The seven parameters, and how a block overrides an agent.
* Several blocks under one agent:: Two views of one agent, or two different questions.
* Limitations of dynamic blocks:: What one :AGENT_MATCHED: line cannot say.


File: org-agents.info,  Node: Inserting a block,  Next: Block parameters,  Up: Dynamic blocks

8.1 Inserting a block
=====================

‘C-c C-x x’ is Org's command for inserting a dynamic block, and
‘org-agents’ is among the types it offers, beside ‘columnview’ and
‘clocktable’.  ‘M-x’ ‘org-agents-insert-dblock’ does the same thing
directly.

   What is inserted is empty, and deliberately so:

     #+BEGIN: org-agents
     #+END:

   That is what ‘columnview’ and ‘clocktable’ do at ‘C-c C-x x’, and it
keeps insertion separate from evaluation.  Writing the block is a
separate step: ‘C-c C-c’ inside it, ‘C-c C-x C-u’, or
‘org-agents-update’.  See *note By hand::.

   If point is not at the beginning of a line, a fresh line is opened
first.  If the enclosing entry is an agent whose view needs a block and
has none, an update opens one for you, so inserting the block by hand is
a convenience rather than a requirement.


File: org-agents.info,  Node: Block parameters,  Next: Several blocks under one agent,  Prev: Inserting a block,  Up: Dynamic blocks

8.2 Block parameters
====================

Org reads a block's parameters as Lisp.  That is what lets a block say
things a property drawer never could, and it is also what makes a block
get things wrong that a drawer cannot.

   Each parameter overrides the corresponding ‘AGENT_*’ property of the
entry the block sits under.  The test is presence, not truth, so a
parameter written ‘nil’ *clears* the property: ‘:limit nil’ asks for no
limit, which is not the same as a block that says nothing about limits.

‘:query’
     Overrides ‘:AGENT_QUERY:’.  A string, or the form itself, since Org
     has already read the parameters as Lisp.  Unreadable text is
     refused, as ‘unreadable :query ‘...’’.  A block with a ‘:query’
     belongs to no agent; see *note Several blocks under one agent::.

‘:view’
     Overrides ‘:AGENT_VIEW:’.  A *symbol*, one of ‘children’, ‘list’,
     ‘table’.  A string is not the symbol, and is refused: ‘a block’s
     ‘:view’ must be one of children, list, table, not "table"’.  May
     not be nil, because there is no such thing as no view.

‘:scope’
     Overrides ‘:AGENT_SCOPE:’.  Resolved exactly as the property is;
     see *note Scopes::.  May not be nil: no scope resolves to no files,
     and the block would then read as a query that matched nothing.

‘:sort’
     Overrides ‘:AGENT_SORT:’.  Checked against the same vocabulary,
     with the same table-only rule for ‘(column N)’ and ‘(ts-column N)’.

‘:limit’
     Overrides ‘:AGENT_LIMIT:’.  A non-negative integer, or nil to
     clear.  Refused otherwise: ‘a block’s ‘:limit’ must be a count, not
     "5"’.

‘:columns’
     Overrides ‘:AGENT_COLUMNS:’.  A *string*, or nil.

‘:format’
     Overrides ‘:AGENT_FORMAT:’.  A *string*, or nil.

   The one thing that surprises everybody: ‘:columns’ and ‘:format’ need
quoted strings, because a block's parameters are Lisp.

     #+BEGIN: org-agents :format NEXT_REVIEW
     #+END:

   is refused, and the message says exactly what happened:

     org-agents: a block’s ‘:format’ must be a string, not NEXT_REVIEW

   Write ‘:format "NEXT_REVIEW"’.  In a property drawer, ‘:AGENT_FORMAT:
NEXT_REVIEW’ needs no quotes, because a property value is already a
string.  The two spellings look alike and are read by different readers.

   A block that has neither a ‘:query’ of its own nor an enclosing agent
has nothing to render, and says so:

     org-agents: block has no :query and no enclosing agent


File: org-agents.info,  Node: Several blocks under one agent,  Next: Limitations of dynamic blocks,  Prev: Block parameters,  Up: Dynamic blocks

8.3 Several blocks under one agent
==================================

One agent may carry as many ‘org-agents’ blocks as you like.  An update
with point *outside* every block writes all of them, in buffer order; an
update with point *inside* one writes that block alone, which is how you
refresh one expensive view without the others.

   There is a precondition, and it is not obvious: *the entry's own
‘:AGENT_VIEW:’ must be ‘list’ or ‘table’, never ‘children’.*  The
entry's view decides which renderer runs, and ‘children’ renders aliases
without ever looking for a block.  An agent with no ‘:AGENT_VIEW:’ and
two blocks under it writes three child aliases, reports itself updated,
and leaves both blocks empty, saying nothing about them:

     * No view named
       :PROPERTIES:
       :AGENT_QUERY: (property "NEXT_REVIEW")
       :AGENT_MATCHED: 3 [2026-08-20 Thu 17:52]
       :END:
     #+BEGIN: org-agents
     #+END:
     ** [[id:rewrite-exporter][Rewrite the exporter]]
     :PROPERTIES:
     :AGENT_MATCH: t
     :END:
     ** [[id:audit-prefilter][Audit the prefilter]]
     :PROPERTIES:
     :AGENT_MATCH: t
     :END:
     ** [[id:renew-passport][Renew the passport]]
     :PROPERTIES:
     :AGENT_MATCH: t
     :END:

   With ‘:AGENT_VIEW: list’ on the entry, both blocks are written by one
‘org-agents-update-buffer’, each according to its own parameters:

     * Review desk
       :PROPERTIES:
       :AGENT_QUERY: (and (todo "TODO") (property "NEXT_REVIEW"))
       :AGENT_VIEW:  list
       :AGENT_MATCHED: 3 [2026-08-20 Thu 17:50]
       :END:
     #+BEGIN: org-agents :format "NEXT_REVIEW"
     - [[id:rewrite-exporter][Rewrite the exporter]] 2026-09-01
     - [[id:audit-prefilter][Audit the prefilter]] 2026-08-25
     - [[id:renew-passport][Renew the passport]] 2026-08-21
     #+END:

     #+BEGIN: org-agents :view table :columns "ITEM_BY_ID NEXT_REVIEW" :sort (ts-column 2)
     | ITEM_BY_ID           | NEXT_REVIEW |
     |----------------------+-------------|
     | [[id:renew-passport][Renew the passport]]   |  2026-08-21 |
     | [[id:audit-prefilter][Audit the prefilter]]  |  2026-08-25 |
     | [[id:rewrite-exporter][Rewrite the exporter]] |  2026-09-01 |
     #+END:

   Because ‘:query’ is among the parameters, two blocks of one agent
need not even be asking the same question:

     * Two questions
       :PROPERTIES:
       :AGENT_QUERY: (property "NEXT_REVIEW")
       :AGENT_VIEW:  list
       :AGENT_MATCHED: 3 [2026-08-20 Thu 17:52]
       :END:
     #+BEGIN: org-agents
     - [[id:rewrite-exporter][Rewrite the exporter]]
     - [[id:audit-prefilter][Audit the prefilter]]
     - [[id:renew-passport][Renew the passport]]
     #+END:

     #+BEGIN: org-agents :query (property "LOCATION")
     - [[id:renew-passport][Renew the passport]]
     - [[id:collect-print][Collect the print]]
     #+END:

   A block that supplies its own ‘:query’ and sits under no agent
renders standalone, over ‘org-agenda-files’ as a ‘list’ until its own
parameters say otherwise:

     * Notes
     #+BEGIN: org-agents :query (property "LOCATION") :format "LOCATION"
     - [[id:renew-passport][Renew the passport]] Pasadena
     - [[id:collect-print][Collect the print]] Altadena
     #+END:

   Write it with point inside the block, or with Org's own ‘C-c C-c’.
‘org-agents-update-buffer’ leaves such a block alone: it belongs to no
agent, there is nothing to record a count on, and refreshing every
dynamic block in a buffer is already ‘org-update-all-dblocks’'s job.


File: org-agents.info,  Node: Limitations of dynamic blocks,  Prev: Several blocks under one agent,  Up: Dynamic blocks

8.4 Limitations of dynamic blocks
=================================

*‘:AGENT_MATCHED:’ records the first block's count, in buffer order.*
For a multi-block agent there is no single right number to record.  A
block's parameters override the entry's, ‘:query’ included, so two
blocks of one agent may answer different questions, and "the agent's
count" is not one number.  The first block's count is what is written,
and it is what it says it is rather than a total.  In the ‘Two
questions’ example above, ‘:AGENT_MATCHED:’ is ‘3’, which is the first
block's three matches, and the second block's two are recorded nowhere.
Likewise an agent whose ‘:AGENT_LIMIT:’ is 1 and whose second block
clears it with ‘:limit nil’ records ‘1’, while the second block holds
three items.

   *A ‘children’ agent that owns blocks ignores them silently.*  Shown
above.  If you find an ‘org-agents’ block that stays empty however often
you update, check the enclosing entry's ‘:AGENT_VIEW:’.

   *An interrupted or failed render restores the previous body, not the
buffer.*  ‘org-prepare-dblock’ deletes the old body before the writer
runs, so everything a render needs is computed before anything is
written, and a failure puts the old body back.  ‘C-g’ is treated the
same way: the body goes back and the quit is then signalled again, so
the interrupt still interrupts.

   *An edit landing in the block during a prefilter wait is not
protected.*  A ‘list’ or ‘table’ agent over a corpus scope may wait on a
ripgrep subprocess from inside Org's dblock writer, with the block body
already deleted, and waiting on a subprocess runs timers.  The
‘children’ view does not have this exposure because it collects before
it edits.  See *note Bounding a run:: for what this costs and why it is
not closed.


