9 Updating
**********

Nothing in this package updates an agent by itself.  There is no timer,
no idle hook, and no background process; a render happens because you
asked for one, either by running a command or by saving a file you have
armed for it.  That is a design position rather than an omission, and it
is argued in *note What was deliberately not ported and why::.

   So there are exactly four ways in, and this chapter is about choosing
between them.

   • A command, over one agent, one buffer, or a named file set.  See
     *note By hand::.
   • Org's own dynamic-block commands, over one block or every block in
     a buffer.  See *note Inserting a block:: and *note By hand::.
   • A save, with ‘org-agents-mode’ on in that buffer.  See *note
     org-agents-mode::.
   • A save in any buffer that looks like it holds an agent, with
     ‘global-org-agents-mode’ on.  See *note global-org-agents-mode::.

   The package binds no keys of its own.  ‘C-c C-c’, ‘C-c C-x x’ and
‘C-c C-x C-u’ are Org's, and reach this package because an ‘org-agents’
block is an ordinary Org dynamic block.  Everything else you bind
yourself, or run with ‘M-x’.

* Menu:

* By hand::                      org-agents-update, -update-buffer and -update-all.
* Looking before you write::     org-agents-preview, which writes nothing.
* org-agents-mode::              Updating a buffer's agents before each save.
* global-org-agents-mode::       Arming every Org buffer that looks like it holds an agent.
