9.1 By hand
===========

Three commands, in order of how much they touch.

   *‘org-agents-update’* updates the agent point is in, or the
‘org-agents’ block point sits in.  Point may sit anywhere in the entry:
on the heading, in the drawer, in the body, or inside a block.  With
point inside a block, that block alone is written; otherwise the whole
agent is, which for a ‘children’ agent means its aliases and for a
block-view agent means every one of its blocks.  The echo area reports
‘org-agents: N match(es)’, or ‘org-agents: the block wrote N item(s)’
for a standalone block.  With neither an agent nor a block at point it
refuses:

     org-agents: no agent and no ‘org-agents’ block at point

   This is the command to bind.  The package does not bind it, so pick a
key in the Org map; ‘C-c C-x Q’ is free there, where the lower-case ‘C-c
C-x q’ and ‘C-c C-x a’ are both Org's own:

     (with-eval-after-load 'org
       (define-key org-mode-map (kbd "C-c C-x Q") #'org-agents-update))

   The ‘use-package’ form in *note Installation:: binds the same key.

   *‘org-agents-update-buffer’* updates every agent in the current
buffer, continuing past failures.  Headings are what it scans, and an
agent is a heading whose *own drawer* supplies a query; searching the
buffer text for the property name would find it in a note you wrote
under an alias, or in an agent's own prose, and would call one heading
two agents.  A dynamic block supplying its own ‘:query’ belongs to no
agent and is left alone.

   Each agent answers for itself.  One with a malformed property is
named in the summary and the rest are updated regardless, because an
update that stopped at the first bad query would leave every agent after
it stale:

     org-agents: updated 2 agents, 1 failed:
     org-agents: Broken limit (~/org/agents.org:31): :AGENT_LIMIT: must be a
     count, not ‘many’

   The failure names the agent's heading and its ‘FILE:LINE’, because
the same summary is read over a whole corpus where several agents may
share a heading.

   *‘org-agents-update-all’* updates every agent in the files
‘org-agents-files’ names.  That option is a list of files and
directories, or the symbol ‘agenda’ for ‘org-agenda-files’; a directory
contributes the Org files below it, recursively.  Names are winnowed to
the readable ones and compared as truenames, so a file named twice
over--by itself and by a directory that holds it--is updated once, and
one summary does not report the agents in it twice.  A file that is
readable but that Emacs cannot visit (one grown past
‘large-file-warning-threshold’, one that changed on disk since it was
last visited, one whose coding system cannot decode it) is recorded as a
single failure named by its file, and does not take with it the count of
the files already updated.

   Note what ‘org-agents-files’ is and is not.  It says which files
‘org-agents-update-all’ opens *and writes*.  It has nothing to do with
‘:AGENT_SCOPE:’, which says which files an agent _reads_.  See *note
Scopes::.

   *An update leaves its buffers modified and unsaved.*  All three
commands do, and it is deliberate: the result can be read over--and
undone with ‘C-/’--before it is kept.  ‘org-agents-update-all’ over a
hundred files leaves a hundred modified buffers and writes nothing to
disk.  Save them yourself, or do not.


