9.2 Looking before you write
============================

‘org-agents-preview’ reads a query from the minibuffer and shows what an
agent with that query would match, without writing anything anywhere.
It is the right way to develop a query, and the right way to find out
whether one will be approved before you commit it to a drawer.

   The query is read, expanded and gated exactly as an agent's is, and
‘org-agents-exclude’ is conjoined in before ‘org-ql-search’ sees the
form, so what is approved here is what runs.  See *note The structural
gate::.

   The scope is always ‘org-agenda-files’: a scope belongs to an agent,
and a preview has no agent.  Agenda files that cannot be read are
skipped with a message rather than refused.  With no agenda files at
all, the command says so instead of quietly searching the current
buffer:

     org-agents: ‘org-agenda-files’ is empty, so there is nothing to preview

   Results appear in an ‘org-ql-search’ buffer, which is org-ql's own
view and behaves as org-ql documents it.  A query the gate does not
admit is refused with ‘org-agents: query not approved’.


File: org-agents.info,  Node: org-agents-mode,  Next: global-org-agents-mode,  Prev: Looking before you write,  Up: Updating

9.3 org-agents-mode
===================

An agent is worth more refreshed than remembered, and the cheapest
moment to refresh one is when you are already writing the file it lives
in.  ‘org-agents-mode’ is a buffer-local minor mode that updates that
buffer's agents before each save, and shows ‘Agents’ in the mode line
while it is on.  It adds one function to ‘before-save-hook’
buffer-locally--on the global value the hook would scan every save in
the session, Org buffer or not, for agents it will not find.

   Turn it on with ‘M-x’ ‘org-agents-mode’, and the next ‘C-x C-s’
renders before writing:

     * Up for review
       :PROPERTIES:
       :AGENT_QUERY: (and (todo "TODO") (property "NEXT_REVIEW"))
       :AGENT_MATCHED: 3 [2026-08-20 Thu 17:51]
       :END:
     ** [[id:rewrite-exporter][Rewrite the exporter]]
     :PROPERTIES:
     :AGENT_MATCH: t
     :END:
     ** [[id:audit-prefilter][Audit the prefilter]]
     :PROPERTIES:
     :AGENT_MATCH: t
     :END:
     ** [[id:renew-passport][Renew the passport]]
     :PROPERTIES:
     :AGENT_MATCH: t
     :END:

   Two properties are what make this usable rather than a nuisance, and
both are described in *note The three refusals on the save path::.  A
save whose render changes nothing *writes no bytes at all*, old
timestamps included, and an agent whose scope has no bound is *named
once and skipped*.  Read that node before leaving the mode on.

   The mode refuses a buffer that is not an Org buffer, and turns itself
back off before refusing, so there is never a mode reporting itself on
with no hook under it:

     org-agents: ‘org-agents-mode’ needs an Org buffer

   *Arming one file.*  The targeted way to turn the mode on for a
particular file is a file-local mode line on its first line:

     # -*- mode: org; mode: org-agents; -*-

   Two ‘mode:’ specifications: the first names the major mode, the
second a minor mode to enable.  The value is ‘org-agents’, *not*
‘org-agents-mode’, because Emacs appends ‘-mode’ to whatever you write
there.  Write ‘mode: org-agents-mode’ and Emacs looks for a mode called
‘org-agents-mode-mode’, does not find one, and says so in a message you
will probably miss:

     Ignoring unknown mode ‘org-agents-mode-mode’

   leaving the mode off in a file you believed you had armed.

   *One Emacs behaviour that surprises people.*  ‘save-buffer’ runs
‘before-save-hook’ only when the buffer is actually modified.  So
visiting a file and typing ‘C-x C-s’ without having changed anything
does not render: there was no save.  This is Emacs rather than this
package, but it looks like the mode failing.


File: org-agents.info,  Node: global-org-agents-mode,  Next: The three refusals on the save path,  Prev: org-agents-mode,  Up: Updating

9.4 global-org-agents-mode
==========================

‘global-org-agents-mode’ turns ‘org-agents-mode’ on in every Org buffer
whose text matches ‘^[ \t]*:AGENT_QUERY:’.  A buffer that is not an Org
buffer is passed over silently; the mode's own refusal is for someone
who asked for it by name.

   The test is a single whole-buffer regexp scan, and it is deliberately
not the authoritative per-heading scan an update uses.  All it decides
is whether to arm a hook.  A buffer wrongly armed pays for the real scan
on each of its saves, and a real scan that finds no agent does nothing
and says nothing.  Asking every heading's drawer instead would cost that
in every Org buffer you visit, agent or not, to decide something a text
search decides well enough.

   In practice:

     agents.org       org-agents-mode=t     (holds an agent)
     corpus.org       org-agents-mode=nil   (an Org file with no agent)
     attributes.org   org-agents-mode=nil   (the registry)

   Choosing between the global mode and the file-local line is a
question about where you keep your agents.  If they live in one or two
files, the file-local line is better: it is visible in the file, it
travels with the file, and it arms nothing else.  If agents are
scattered through a corpus you edit constantly, the global mode is
better, because you will forget to add the line.  The cost of the global
mode is that the detector is a text scan: a buffer that merely
_mentions_ ‘:AGENT_QUERY:’ in prose, this manual's own sources among
them, gets the mode and the scan on every save.

   The generated option ‘global-org-agents-mode’ is marked risky,
because turning the mode on is what makes every save of an Org file run
its agents' queries.  A risky variable is not set from a file-local
variables block without your explicit confirmation.


File: org-agents.info,  Node: The three refusals on the save path,  Next: What writing AGENT_MATCHED costs,  Prev: global-org-agents-mode,  Up: Updating

9.5 The three refusals on the save path
=======================================

A save is a keystroke, and a keystroke has a budget.  Three refusals
keep the update inside it.

   *No save ever spawns a prefilter,* whatever ‘org-agents-prefilter’ is
set to; the option is bound to nil for the whole extent of the update.
This is safe because a prefilter can only narrow which files org-ql
opens and can never change what matches, so an agent updated without one
matches exactly what it would have matched, having merely opened more
files to find out.  It is also worth doing: for a scope that already
names its files, a prefilter is pure overhead, and measurably so--see
*note Which scopes get narrowed and why::.  Narrowing such a scope makes
a keystroke slower to reach the same answer.

   *An agent whose scope needs a prefilter is not updated on save.*
Those are the scopes with no bound on what they hold: ‘active’, ‘all’,
and any directory.  There is no telling how many files one of those
would open, so it is left for a command you asked for.  Such agents are
named once, in one message however many there are, and left exactly as
they were:

     org-agents: not updated on save, the scope needs a prefilter: Up for
     review (~/org/onsave.org:1)

   Use ‘org-agents-update’ on one of those.  Note the two rules answer
different questions and both are wanted: this one is about what an
update would _cost_, the first is about a save doing no work that cannot
pay for itself.

   *A render that changes nothing puts the buffer back as it was.*  This
is the property that makes writing ‘:AGENT_MATCHED:’ on every save
affordable at all.  The stamp says when an update ran, so it differs on
every run whatever the query found; without this rule every save would
rewrite the file and move the dates you rely on, for nothing.  So the
render is compared with the ‘:AGENT_MATCHED:’ lines masked out, and
where nothing else moved the *old stamps stay* and the file reaches disk
byte-identical.  Measured: a second save with nothing new to find is
byte-for-byte identical to the first.

   The buffer is restored with ‘replace-buffer-contents’, so point, the
mark, and every marker and overlay survive.

   Two further behaviours belong here.

   Any failure is reported and the save then goes on regardless:

     org-agents: the update on save failed: ...

   A file must not become unsavable because a query written in it is
wrong; fixing the query is exactly what the next save is for.

   A quit is deliberately *not* caught, so *‘C-g’ during an update on
save aborts the save along with the update*.  A half-written render is
not what you asked to keep.


File: org-agents.info,  Node: What writing AGENT_MATCHED costs,  Next: Limitations of updating,  Prev: The three refusals on the save path,  Up: Updating

9.6 What writing AGENT_MATCHED costs
====================================

After a render, the update writes ‘:AGENT_MATCHED:’ on the agent: how
many entries matched, and when.

     :AGENT_MATCHED: 3 [2026-08-20 Thu 17:46]

   It is written only once the view has been rendered, never during one:
adding a line to a drawer moves every position below it, among them the
bounds of the very dynamic block a render was called for.  The time is
written as an inactive timestamp, so Org reads it back as one.

   The point of the property is that an agent says what it last found
without being run again, which matters more here than in a system whose
agents update continuously.  It has three consequences, accepted rather
than avoided.

  1. *It modifies the buffer.*  Which is to say an update always leaves
     its files modified and unsaved, even where the render itself found
     nothing new.  That is the feature *note By hand:: describes, and
     the result can be read over and undone before it is kept.  On the
     save path the buffer is put back where the render changed nothing,
     so the stamp does not make every save rewrite the file.

  2. *Its timestamp is inactive, and therefore visible to a query.*  A
     query written ‘(ts-inactive :from ...)’ over a corpus holding
     agents will match the agents themselves.  This is not a bug in the
     query; it is a real inactive timestamp in a real drawer.

  3. *It leaves a timestamp in the file,* so a version-control diff of a
     saved update shows the stamp even where the render itself did not
     move.

   For what the count means when an agent carries several blocks, see
*note Limitations of dynamic blocks::.


File: org-agents.info,  Node: Limitations of updating,  Prev: What writing AGENT_MATCHED costs,  Up: Updating

9.7 Limitations of updating
===========================

*There is no timer-driven update, and there will not be one.*  Nothing
in this package renders an agent while you are not looking at it.  The
reason is the medium: an Org corpus is thousands of text files under
version control that other tools also write, and a timer that rewrote
them would put edits into files you are not looking at and stamps into
commits you did not make.  This is the largest divergence from the
system these ideas come from, and it is argued at length, with the
alternatives named, in *note What was deliberately not ported and why::.

   *The first save of a stale multi-block agent writes the file, and
only that save.*  A save refreshes every block of a multi-block agent,
so a buffer whose second block was stale reaches disk changed.  Every
save after it is byte-identical again.  This is worth saying because the
byte-identical save is the property you come to rely on, and the one
save that breaks it looks like the property failing.

   *An update over a corpus scope leaves the files it read visited.*
org-ql opens a file it has to evaluate against with
‘find-file-noselect’, and nothing here reaps it, so an unnarrowed walk
of ‘all’ can leave thousands of Org buffers behind.  The prefilter is
what keeps that number down.  A file-count ceiling, above which a live
walk would be refused and below which it would be allowed, is the
obvious way to sharpen this and is not implemented.  See *note
Limitations of the prefilter::.


