10 The attribute registry
*************************

An agent asks a question about your corpus.  The rest of this manual is
about the corpus itself: what its properties mean, where their values
come from, how they look on the screen, and what may be done to them in
bulk.  Tinderbox calls that model *dynamic properties*, and it is the
half of Tinderbox that org-agents borrows beyond the agent.  See *note
The five ideas::.

   The registry is where the model starts.  It is one optional Org file,
named by ‘org-agents-attributes-file’, in which you write down what your
corpus's own properties are.

* Menu:

* Why declare attributes::       What typing an untyped drawer buys you.
* The registry file::            One file, one top-level entry per attribute.
* The declaration::              The four ATTR_ properties.
* The six types::                What each type admits, and what it refuses.
* It is pure data::              Nothing a registry holds is ever evaluated.
* Checking a corpus::            org-agents-check-attributes, which reports and never edits.
* Completing declared values::   org-agents-allowed-values on Org's own hook.
* A corpus-wide column view::    org-agents-attribute-columns, and the recipe it completes.
* Limitations of the registry::  What a corpus-scope lint leaves behind.


