10.1 Why declare attributes
===========================

Org's property drawer is untyped and open.  Any entry may carry any
property, spelled any way, holding anything; nothing anywhere says which
names your corpus actually uses, what values each of them admits, or
which of them you meant.  That openness is why the drawer is useful, and
it is also why a corpus that has grown for years contains ‘STATUS’,
‘Status’, ‘STATE’ and ‘PHASE’, three of which were the same idea once.

   Tinderbox has no such problem, because a Tinderbox attribute must be
declared before a note can hold it: you name it, choose its type, give
it a default, and optionally list the values it should suggest.  See
*note Attributes in Tinderbox:: for what that pane looks like and what
it is for.

   A registry is that declaration, written as Org.  Declaring an
attribute buys four things, and each of them is a separate feature of
this package:

   • *completion*, corpus wide.  ‘org-agents-allowed-values’ offers a
     declared vocabulary to ‘org-set-property’ in every Org buffer,
     where Org's own ‘NAME_ALL’ convention offers it only in the file or
     subtree that spells it.  See *note Completing declared values::.
   • *a lint*.  ‘org-agents-check-attributes’ compares a scope against
     the registry and reports where they disagree: a name nothing
     declares, a value outside its vocabulary, a value that is not of
     its type.  See *note Checking a corpus::.
   • *column formats*.  ‘org-agents-attribute-columns’ builds a
     ‘COLUMNS’ format out of chosen declarations, with a summary
     operator on the numbers.  See *note A corpus-wide column view::.
   • *defaults, for prototype resolution*.  An attribute's
     ‘:ATTR_DEFAULT:’ is the last step of the resolution order, so a
     declared default is a value every entry in the corpus has without
     any entry spelling it.  See *note The resolution order::.

   Nothing else in the package requires a registry.  Agents, queries,
views and updating all work with no registry at all, and a missing or
unreadable registry declares nothing and says nothing about it.


File: org-agents.info,  Node: The registry file,  Next: The declaration,  Prev: Why declare attributes,  Up: The attribute registry

10.2 The registry file
======================

Set ‘org-agents-attributes-file’ to the file you want to keep the
declarations in.  The default is ‘~/org/attributes.org’, which this
package will never create for you and never assumes exists.

   Each *top-level* entry of that file declares one attribute:

   • the *heading* is the attribute's name, spelled exactly as the
     property is spelled in a drawer;
   • the *drawer* carries the declaration;
   • the *body* is the attribute's documentation, for you and for anyone
     else reading the file.

     * STATUS
       :PROPERTIES:
       :ATTR_TYPE:    string
       :ATTR_VALUES:  active paused blocked done
       :ATTR_DEFAULT: active
       :ATTR_FACES:   blocked error | paused shadow | done success
       :END:
       The state of a piece of work, independent of its TODO keyword.

     * NEXT_REVIEW
       :PROPERTIES:
       :ATTR_TYPE: date
       :END:
       The day this entry should next be looked at.

     * REVIEWS
       :PROPERTIES:
       :ATTR_TYPE:    number
       :ATTR_DEFAULT: 0
       :END:
       How many times this entry has been reviewed.

   *The drawer must sit immediately under the heading.*  Org keeps a
property drawer there and looks nowhere else, so a drawer written after
the body text is not a property drawer at all.  The entry is then
reported as declaring no type, which is a true statement about a file
whose real mistake is one blank line higher up.  This is the single
easiest way to write a registry that reports itself empty, so put the
drawer first in every entry you write.

   One heading in the file is reserved: a top-level ‘Prototypes’
section, whose entries are not declarations but masters.  See *note
Declaring masters::.


File: org-agents.info,  Node: The declaration,  Next: The six types,  Prev: The registry file,  Up: The attribute registry

10.3 The declaration
====================

Four properties make up a declaration.  Only the first is required, and
it is what makes an entry a declaration at all.  Their exact contracts
are in *note Registry properties::; what follows is what they are for.

   ‘:ATTR_TYPE:’ is one of ‘string’, ‘number’, ‘date’, ‘boolean’, ‘set’
or ‘list’.  See *note The six types::.

   ‘:ATTR_VALUES:’ is the values the attribute admits, separated by
whitespace.  Tinderbox calls these *suggested values*, and separates
them with semicolons; here the separator is whitespace, because
whitespace is what Org's own ‘NAME_ALL’ convention uses and therefore
what ‘org-property-get-allowed-values’ already reads.  A trailing ‘:ETC’
leaves the vocabulary open, which is Org's own spelling of that: the
values are offered for completion, and a value not among them is still
admitted.  ‘:ETC’ reaches Org unfiltered, because Org is what reads it.

   ‘:ATTR_DEFAULT:’ is the default, written as text.  It is the last
step of the prototype resolution order, and it stays the trimmed string
the file holds: never a parsed number, never a decoded time.

   ‘:ATTR_FACES:’ maps values to Emacs faces, for
‘org-agents-faces-mode’.  See *note The ATTR_FACES syntax::.

   A declaration is available to Lisp as a plist, which is the most
direct way to see what the reader made of your file:

     (org-agents-attribute "STATUS")
       => (:name "STATUS" :type string
           :values ("active" "paused" "blocked" "done")
           :default "active"
           :faces (("blocked" . error) ("paused" . shadow) ("done" . success))
           :doc "The state of a piece of work, independent of its TODO keyword."
           :file "…/attributes.org" :line 1)


File: org-agents.info,  Node: The six types,  Next: It is pure data,  Prev: The declaration,  Up: The attribute registry

10.4 The six types
==================

‘string’
     Any text.  With a declared vocabulary the whole trimmed value must
     be a member of it, compared case sensitively: a declared vocabulary
     is one you wrote down.

‘number’
     A signed integer or decimal, with *no exponent*: ‘3’, ‘3.5’, ‘.5’,
     ‘-3’.  Anchored at both ends, so the whole value must be one
     number.  That is what catches an accumulated property: a ‘:REVIEWS:
     3’ beside a ‘:REVIEWS+: 4’ reads back as the text ‘3 4’, and that
     is not a number.  ‘1e3’ is refused, on the grounds that in an Org
     drawer it is far more likely to be a product code than a thousand.

‘date’
     A value that parses *and names a real day*.  Both halves are
     needed: Org's own parser reads ‘[2026-02-30 Fri]’ as day 30 of
     February without complaint, so the round trip through ‘encode-time’
     and ‘decode-time’ is what catches it.

‘boolean’
     ‘true’ or ‘false’, unquoted, lower case, and nothing else.  Not
     ‘t’, not ‘yes’, not ‘True’.  The reader synthesizes the two values
     onto every boolean declaration, so a ‘:ATTR_VALUES:’ on a boolean
     has no meaning and is ignored with a warning.

‘set’
     Whitespace-separated members, every one of them admitted by the
     vocabulary, *and no member repeated*.  The empty set is valid.

‘list’
     Whitespace-separated members, every one admitted by the vocabulary.
     Duplicates are fine and order is significant.

   ‘set’ and ‘list’ are Tinderbox's, with Tinderbox's meanings kept
exactly: unordered-unique and ordered.  Only the separator changed.  See
*note Attributes in Tinderbox:: for the correspondence, and *note What
it changed:: for why.

   The predicate behind all six is available to Lisp as
‘org-agents-attribute-valid-p’.  Some measured answers:

     (org-agents-attribute-valid-p 'boolean "true")            => t
     (org-agents-attribute-valid-p 'boolean "t")               => nil
     (org-agents-attribute-valid-p 'boolean "True")            => nil
     (org-agents-attribute-valid-p 'number  "3.5")             => t
     (org-agents-attribute-valid-p 'number  "1e3")             => nil
     (org-agents-attribute-valid-p 'number  "3 4")             => nil
     (org-agents-attribute-valid-p 'date    "2026-09-01")      => t
     (org-agents-attribute-valid-p 'date    "2026-02-30")      => nil
     (org-agents-attribute-valid-p 'set  "emacs org"   '("emacs" "org")) => t
     (org-agents-attribute-valid-p 'set  "emacs emacs" '("emacs" "org")) => nil
     (org-agents-attribute-valid-p 'list "emacs emacs" '("emacs" "org")) => t


File: org-agents.info,  Node: It is pure data,  Next: Checking a corpus,  Prev: The six types,  Up: The attribute registry

10.5 It is pure data
====================

A registry file is *data*, and that is a guarantee rather than a
description of the present implementation.  Every field is read with
‘org-entry-get’ and then used as a string, as one of six symbols out of
a fixed table, or as a face name interned from text.  There is nothing a
registry file can hold that this package will ‘read’, ‘eval’, or run.

   This is a deliberate divergence from Tinderbox, which has an ‘Action’
attribute type whose values are strings of action code *evaluated when
used*.  A type like that is precisely what could not be ported without
giving up the guarantee above.  See *note What was deliberately not
ported and why::.

   Two further properties of the reader are worth knowing:

   • *Malformation is contained.*  A bad ‘:ATTR_TYPE:’ costs its entry:
     the entry is named once and skipped.  A bad anything else costs
     only that field, warned once, with the rest of the declaration
     intact.  A missing or unreadable registry declares nothing and says
     nothing about it.
   • *The file is read lazily, and at most once per edit,* including an
     edit you have not saved.  So a correction to a declaration takes
     effect at the next thing that reads it, without a reload and
     without a cache to clear.

   A name that is one of Org's special properties may be declared, and
the reader warns once that no completion is possible for it, because Org
answers for those names out of clauses of its own.  The declaration is
still worth having: the lint reads it.


File: org-agents.info,  Node: Checking a corpus,  Next: Completing declared values,  Prev: It is pure data,  Up: The attribute registry

10.6 Checking a corpus
======================

A registry is easiest to write against a corpus that already exists, and
‘M-x’ ‘org-agents-check-attributes’ is how you find out what is in
yours.  It takes a scope, with exactly the vocabulary ‘:AGENT_SCOPE:’
takes, and it *reports*.  Nothing is edited, ever.

   It asks three questions:

  1. is a property name in use that the registry does not declare?
  2. is a value one that does not parse as its ‘:ATTR_TYPE:’?
  3. is a value one that is outside its ‘:ATTR_VALUES:’?

   Given a registry declaring ‘STATUS’, ‘NEXT_REVIEW’, ‘REVIEWS’ and
‘TOPICS’, and this file in scope:

     * One
       :PROPERTIES:
       :STATUS:      wedged
       :NEXT_REVIEW: 2026-02-30
       :REVIEWS:     lots
       :WIDGET:      3
       :TOPICS:      emacs emacs
       :END:

   the echo area says ‘org-agents: 5 findings over 1 file’ and
‘*org-agents attributes*’, in ‘compilation-mode’, holds:

     lint.org:3: STATUS: `wedged' is not one of active paused blocked done
     lint.org:4: NEXT_REVIEW: `2026-02-30' is not a date
     lint.org:5: REVIEWS: `lots' is not a number
     lint.org:6: WIDGET is not declared in …/attributes.org (1 use)
     lint.org:7: TOPICS: `emacs emacs' is not a set

   All five kinds in one report: a value outside its vocabulary; a date
that parses but names no day; a value not of its type; an undeclared
name, with a *site count*; and a ‘set’ with a repeated member.  Each
line begins ‘FILE:LINE:’, so ‘<RET>’ and ‘next-error’ walk them.  A
clean run says so with its counts rather than popping an empty buffer:

     org-agents: no findings; every property in scope is declared and valid
     (1 file in scope, 1 read, 1 entry, 7 declarations)

   Read ‘read’ as it is written: how many files the run actually opened,
which on the fast path is fewer than the files in scope.

   *A finding is one per name, not one per line.*  An undeclared name in
use four thousand times is one finding with a count and one confirmed
example site.  Org's own vocabulary is never asked about, nor this
package's ‘AGENT_’ and ‘ATTR_’ names, nor a ‘_ALL’ suffix, nor ‘ID’, nor
the six properties ‘org-archive-subtree’ writes.  Without those
exemptions ‘ID’ alone would be about thirty-seven thousand findings on
the author's corpus.

   *It never refuses.*  Setting ‘org-agents-prefilter’ to ‘require’ does
not make it refuse: a lint that declined to run would fail its own
contract.

   *This command is how a registry gets seeded.*  Run it over ‘all’
against an empty registry and every finding is of the first kind, which
is a census of your corpus's property vocabulary.  Measured on the
author's corpus, that vocabulary is 111 names, once the two drawer
delimiters are set aside.  Declare the ones you meant, ignore the ones
you did not, and the second run is about values rather than names.

   The command runs in *two tiers*, because its two questions have
different costs.  The vocabulary question needs no Org semantics and is
answered by one ripgrep census over the whole scope, with each candidate
then confirmed against a real property drawer.  The value question needs
per-entry reading, but only for the *declared* names and only in the
files holding them.  Measured over a 3,616-file scope: 7 to 10 seconds
warm and 64 to 110 seconds cold, against a single-tier version that was
killed at 600 seconds with no report at all.  Treat that range as an
order of magnitude and not as a benchmark; the same vocabulary-only run
over the same scope measured 3.5 seconds on an idle machine and 12.8
seconds on one busy with other work, so a figure outside the range is
machine load and not a regression.


File: org-agents.info,  Node: Completing declared values,  Next: A corpus-wide column view,  Prev: Checking a corpus,  Up: The attribute registry

10.7 Completing declared values
===============================

A declared vocabulary becomes completion by putting
‘org-agents-allowed-values’ on Org's own hook:

     (add-hook 'org-property-allowed-value-functions
               #'org-agents-allowed-values)

   After that, ‘org-set-property’ completes declared values in every Org
buffer, wherever the entry lives, which is what Tinderbox's pull-down
menu of suggested values does.  This is the corpus-wide counterpart of
Org's own ‘NAME_ALL’ convention, which offers a vocabulary only in the
file or subtree that spells it.

   *The hook is not added for you.*  A library has no business mutating
a user's hook, and this one in particular decides what vocabulary is
offered for every property in every Org buffer you open.

   The function is not a command; it takes one argument and answers a
flat list of strings, or nil for a property it is not responsible for.
Nil is the important half: a non-nil answer *shadows* every ‘NAME_ALL’
line in the corpus, so it answers only for a name the registry declares
with a vocabulary.

     (org-agents-allowed-values "STATUS")       => ("active" "paused" "blocked" "done")
     (org-agents-allowed-values "NEXT_REVIEW")  => nil     ; declared, no values
     (org-agents-allowed-values "WIDGET")       => nil     ; not declared

   A boolean answers ‘("true" "false")’ from the two values the reader
synthesizes, so completion needs no special case for the type.  A
vocabulary that is ‘:ETC’ and nothing else is declined, because Org
removes the marker and would be left with nothing after the non-nil
answer had already shadowed the corpus.


File: org-agents.info,  Node: A corpus-wide column view,  Next: Limitations of the registry,  Prev: Completing declared values,  Up: The attribute registry

10.8 A corpus-wide column view
==============================

Tinderbox shows a table of chosen attributes across a set of notes, and
lets you edit the values in it.  Org has all of the pieces for that
already: ‘COLUMNS’ formats, ‘org-columns’, ‘org-agenda-columns’, and
write-back editing in a column view.  Measured, ‘org-agenda-columns’
already runs inside an ‘org-ql-search’ results buffer, so corpus-wide
displayed attributes *with write-back editing exist today*.  What was
missing was the format string, and this command is it.

     M-x org-agents-attribute-columns RET STATUS,NEXT_REVIEW,REVIEWS RET
       => "%ITEM %STATUS %NEXT_REVIEW %REVIEWS{+}"

   ‘%ITEM’ comes first, so a column view has something to name its rows
by, then one ‘%NAME’ per attribute in the order you gave, with a ‘{+}’
summary operator on the numbers.  ‘number’ is the one type that gets an
operator.

   With a prefix argument the command also puts the format on the entry
at point, as the ‘:COLUMNS:’ *property* rather than a ‘#+COLUMNS:’
keyword: the property is what descendants inherit, and it is what
‘org-agenda-columns’ reads for a matched entry.

   The recipe, then, is three steps:

  1. build the format with this command, and put it where the entries
     you care about will inherit it;
  2. run ‘org-ql-search’ over the corpus for the entries you want in the
     table;
  3. press ‘C-c C-x C-c’ in the results buffer, which is
     ‘org-agenda-columns’.

   Two names are refused rather than emitted, and each refusal closes
the same hole.  An *undeclared* name is refused, because a ‘COLUMNS’
line naming a property nothing declares renders an empty column, which
looks exactly like a property nothing has set: the mistake would show up
as a corpus with no data in it rather than as a mistake.  So is a
declared name that a ‘COLUMNS’ format cannot spell, such as
‘PROJECT.PHASE’, which would render as a column headed ‘PROJECT’ that
nothing ever fills.


File: org-agents.info,  Node: Limitations of the registry,  Prev: A corpus-wide column view,  Up: The attribute registry

10.9 Limitations of the registry
================================

*A corpus-scope lint leaves every file in scope visited.*
‘org-agents-check-attributes’ opens each file it reads with
‘find-file-noselect’ and kills nothing afterwards: a buffer this command
opened is indistinguishable, from inside, from one you had open already.
Over ‘all’ that can be a few thousand buffers, with no message saying
so.  They are ordinary file buffers, unmodified, because the lint edits
nothing, so nothing is at risk; but memory and every buffer-list
operation afterwards feel it.

   The two-tier split makes this much better in the common case and does
not remove it.  Against an *empty* registry the value tier reads nothing
at all, and the run's whole footprint is the confirmation pass:
measured, about 150 file opens for 112 candidate names, not 3,616.
Against a registry that declares a name appearing in every file, the
tier reads every file and the old footprint is back.  The clean report's
counts say which happened, which is what ‘3,616 files in scope, 3,616
read’ against ‘3,616 files in scope, 0 read’ is for.

   The remedy is the ordinary one: kill the unmodified Org buffers once
you have worked through the report, with ‘clean-buffer-list’, with ‘M-x’
‘ibuffer’ and ‘* u’, or by restarting the session you ran the seeding
lint in.  Run the ‘all’ scope deliberately rather than casually.


File: org-agents.info,  Node: Prototypes,  Next: Appearance,  Prev: The attribute registry,  Up: Top

11 Prototypes
*************

Org already has one kind of property inheritance: a value written on an
ancestor is visible to its descendants.  That is inheritance along the
*outline*, and it answers the question "where is this entry filed?".

   Prototypes answer a different question: "what kind of thing is this
entry?".  They are Tinderbox's, and like Tinderbox's they have nothing
to do with the outline.  See *note Prototypes in Tinderbox:: for the
idea in Tinderbox's own words.

* Menu:

* What a prototype is::          A relation between two entries.
* Declaring masters::            The reserved Prototypes section.
* Naming a master::              By name, or by id:UUID.
* The resolution order::         Where a resolved value comes from, in order.
* Why outline inheritance is not in that order:: Containment is not inheritance.
* Reads are virtual::            Nothing is written into the inheriting entry, and what that costs.
* Names that never travel::      What a master does not lend.
* The two diagnostics::          A dangling reference, and a cycle.
* Limitations of prototypes::    What virtual resolution costs the prefilter.


File: org-agents.info,  Node: What a prototype is,  Next: Declaring masters,  Up: Prototypes

11.1 What a prototype is
========================

A prototype is a relation between two entries.  One entry, the *master*,
holds properties; another entry names it in ‘:PROTOTYPE:’ and thereby
reads through it.  Where the reading entry says nothing about a
property, the master's value answers.

     * TODO Audit the prefilter
        :PROPERTIES:
        :PROTOTYPE: Blocked Task
        :END:

   That entry's drawer spells no ‘:STATUS:’ at all, and yet its ‘STATUS’
resolves to ‘blocked’, because the master named ‘Blocked Task’ says so.

   Two facts follow from calling this a relation rather than a position:

   • *The master may be any entry.*  It need not be an ancestor, a
     sibling, or in the same file.  Nothing about where either entry
     sits is involved.
   • *An entry has one prototype, and a prototype has any number of
     followers.*  One is a chain, not a lattice: the master may itself
     name a master, and so on.  That is Tinderbox's rule as well.


File: org-agents.info,  Node: Declaring masters,  Next: Naming a master,  Prev: What a prototype is,  Up: Prototypes

11.2 Declaring masters
======================

The registry file carries one reserved top-level heading, ‘Prototypes’,
whose every entry is a master.  A master is an *ordinary entry whose
drawer holds ordinary properties*: there is no declaration syntax, and
none is wanted, because what a master lends is exactly what it holds.

     * Prototypes
     ** Task
        :PROPERTIES:
        :STATUS: active
        :OWNER:  johnw
        :END:
     ** Blocked Task
        :PROPERTIES:
        :PROTOTYPE: Task
        :STATUS:    blocked
        :END:

   ‘Blocked Task’ names ‘Task’ in its own ‘:PROTOTYPE:’, so the two form
a chain: an entry reading through ‘Blocked Task’ gets ‘blocked’ for
‘STATUS’ and ‘johnw’ for ‘OWNER’.

   The section is skipped silently by the declaration reader, where any
other top-level entry with no ‘:ATTR_TYPE:’ would be named.  Tinderbox
keeps its own built-in prototypes in a top-level container of the same
name.


File: org-agents.info,  Node: Naming a master,  Next: The resolution order,  Prev: Declaring masters,  Up: Prototypes

11.3 Naming a master
====================

‘:PROTOTYPE:’ names a master in one of two ways:

   • *by name*, matched against the headings of the registry file's
     ‘Prototypes’ section: ‘:PROTOTYPE: Blocked Task’;
   • *by id*, as ‘:PROTOTYPE: id:e1000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000009’,
     which resolves to the entry carrying that ‘:ID:’ wherever
     ‘org-id-locations’ says it lives.  Any entry in the corpus may be a
     master this way, with no registry involved at all.

   ‘org-id-locations’ is the id form's one precondition.  The resolver
consults that table rather than searching for the id, so a reference the
table does not know about is a dangling reference and is reported as
one: see *note The two diagnostics::.

   ‘PROTOTYPE’ is the only property outside this package's own ‘AGENT_’
and ‘ATTR_’ vocabularies that org-agents reads by name.


File: org-agents.info,  Node: The resolution order,  Next: Why outline inheritance is not in that order,  Prev: Naming a master,  Up: Prototypes

11.4 The resolution order
=========================

Resolving one property at one entry consults four things, in this order,
and stops at the first that answers:

  1. *the entry's own drawer*, read with inheritance *off*;
  2. *the prototype chain*, nearest hop first;
  3. *the registry's* ‘:ATTR_DEFAULT:’ for that name;
  4. *nil*.

   This is Tinderbox's own checklist, item for item; see *note
Prototypes in Tinderbox:: for the passage it comes from.  The chain is
bounded at a thousand hops, which is a guard against a malformed corpus
and not a budget.

   Step 1 reads the drawer raw.  A present-but-empty ‘:NAME:’ answers
the empty string, which org-ql's own ‘property’ predicate matches, so
resolution must match it too.

   With the registry and masters above, and this corpus:

     * Projects
       :PROPERTIES:
       :OWNER: johnw
       :END:
     ** TODO Rewrite the exporter
        :PROPERTIES:
        :STATUS: active
        :END:
     ** TODO Audit the prefilter
        :PROPERTIES:
        :PROTOTYPE: Blocked Task
        :END:
     ** DONE Ship 0.1
        :PROPERTIES:
        :STATUS: done
        :END:

   resolution answers as follows.  The ‘own’ column is ‘org-entry-get’
with no inheritance, which is what a drawer, a grep and org-ql's
‘property’ all see; the other two are ‘org-agents-resolve-property’.

Entry                  own ‘STATUS’   resolved ‘STATUS’   resolved ‘OWNER’
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
Projects               nil            ‘active’            ‘johnw’
Rewrite the exporter   ‘active’       ‘active’            nil
Audit the prefilter    nil            ‘blocked’           ‘johnw’
Ship 0.1               ‘done’         ‘done’              nil

   Every step of the order is visible in that table.  ‘Projects’ spells
no ‘STATUS’ and reaches ‘active’ at step 3, from ‘:ATTR_DEFAULT:’.
‘Audit the prefilter’ spells no ‘STATUS’ and reaches ‘blocked’ at step
2, from the nearest hop of its chain, and ‘johnw’ from the hop beyond
it.  ‘Ship 0.1’ answers from its own drawer at step 1, which is why the
registry's default does not override it.


File: org-agents.info,  Node: Why outline inheritance is not in that order,  Next: Reads are virtual,  Prev: The resolution order,  Up: Prototypes

11.5 Why outline inheritance is not in that order
=================================================

Look again at ‘Rewrite the exporter’ in the table above.  Its parent
‘Projects’ sets ‘:OWNER: johnw’, and its resolved ‘OWNER’ is *nil*.

   That is not an oversight.  *Containment is not inheritance.*  A task
filed under a project is not a kind of project; a note filed under
‘Reading’ is not a kind of reading.  Where a corpus does mean the
outline, Org's own property inheritance already says so, and the query
layer spells that axis ‘$NAME*’ (see *note The two axes::).  ‘$NAME^’ is
this axis.  The two are orthogonal, and a query has to say which it
means because the two answer differently: over the corpus above,
‘$OWNER*’ finds the two entries under ‘Projects’ and ‘$OWNER^’ finds
‘Projects’ itself along with the entries whose chains reach a master
that sets it.  The two answers share no entries at all.

   Tinderbox is explicit about this: "inheritance has nothing to do with
the document hierarchy; a note's prototype is not necessarily its
container."  Of the five ideas org-agents borrows, this is the one where
the agreement is strongest.


File: org-agents.info,  Node: Reads are virtual,  Next: Names that never travel,  Prev: Why outline inheritance is not in that order,  Up: Prototypes

11.6 Reads are virtual
======================

Resolution is a *read*.  Nothing is ever written into the inheriting
entry: no property is copied down, no subtree is materialised, no cache
is persisted anywhere in your files.  So a master may be changed once
and every follower changes with it, which is the whole point of the
mechanism and is Tinderbox's own promise about it.

   The price is worth stating plainly, because a reader will otherwise
discover it at an inconvenient moment.

   *Grep does not see an inherited value.*  Nor does ‘org-entry-get’,
nor a column view, nor org-ql's own ‘property’ predicate.  Over the
corpus above, ‘grep blocked’ finds one line, in the registry file, and
finds nothing in ‘corpus.org’ at all, even though one entry there
resolves to ‘blocked’.

   What *does* see it is:

   • ‘property-resolved’, the org-ql predicate this package defines,
     which is what ‘$NAME^’ expands to.  So the agent whose query is
     ‘(property-resolved "STATUS" "blocked")’ matches ‘Audit the
     prefilter’, which spells no ‘:STATUS:’ anywhere.  See *note The
     property-resolved predicate::.
   • ‘org-agents-resolve-property’, for a caller in Lisp.
   • ‘org-agents-faces-mode’, which reads the resolved value and is the
     reason the mode is worth having at all.  See *note Faces from
     attribute values::.

   The rule to carry away is therefore: *an inherited value is real to a
query and invisible to a text search*.  Where you need the second, spell
the value in the drawer; where you need one authoritative place to
change it, use a master.


File: org-agents.info,  Node: Names that never travel,  Next: The two diagnostics,  Prev: Reads are virtual,  Up: Prototypes

11.7 Names that never travel
============================

Three kinds of name answer from the entry's own drawer and stop there,
whatever a master or a registry default says.

   • *Every* ‘AGENT_’ *name*, because behaviour does not travel.  A
     master lending its ‘:AGENT_QUERY:’ would make every follower an
     agent, and a registry default for such a name would make every
     entry in the corpus one.  The same reasoning is why an action is
     not inheritable; see *note The three refusals::.
   • ‘PROTOTYPE’ *itself*, because a declared default for it would hand
     every entry in the corpus a master.
   • *Org's special properties.*  A master's ‘CATEGORY’ is not this
     entry's.

   Measured, with a master that carries ‘:AGENT_QUERY:’, ‘:PROTOTYPE:’
and ‘:CATEGORY:’ along with a ‘:STATUS:’, at a follower that spells only
‘:PROTOTYPE: Task’:

     (org-agents-resolve-property "STATUS")       => "active"   ; nearest hop
     (org-agents-resolve-property "AGENT_QUERY")  => nil        ; never travels
     (org-agents-resolve-property "PROTOTYPE")    => "Task"     ; its own, not the master's
     (org-agents-resolve-property "CATEGORY")     => "follower" ; Org's answer, not the master's

   Tinderbox has a comparable exclusion, its *intrinsic* attributes,
chosen for a partly different reason and over a different list; see
*note What it changed::.


File: org-agents.info,  Node: The two diagnostics,  Next: Limitations of prototypes,  Prev: Names that never travel,  Up: Prototypes

11.8 The two diagnostics
========================

Two things can be wrong with a chain, and each is said *once per update*
rather than once per entry or once per redisplay.

   A *dangling reference*, a ‘:PROTOTYPE:’ naming a master that is not
there, is one message naming the entry that made the reference:

     org-agents: no prototype `Nonesuch' named by `Dangling' in proto.org:1

   Resolution then carries on at the next step of the order, so the
entry still gets the registry's default if there is one.  A dangling
reference costs you the chain and not the answer.

   A *cycle* is a ‘user-error’ naming the hops:

     org-agents: prototype cycle at `Looping' in cycle.org:1: A -> B -> A

   Two entry points into resolution differ in what they do with a cycle,
and the difference matters if you are writing Lisp against this.
‘org-agents-resolve-property’ signals, which is right for a command.
‘org-agents-resolve-property-quietly’ reports instead, and is what
residual Lisp and the ‘property-resolved’ predicate use, because a
‘user-error’ raised from inside org-ql's generated matcher would abort
the whole update over one malformed entry.


File: org-agents.info,  Node: Limitations of prototypes,  Prev: The two diagnostics,  Up: Prototypes

11.9 Limitations of prototypes
==============================

Virtual resolution costs the ripgrep prefilter some of its narrowing,
and it has to: an inheriting entry never spells the value, so the
ordinary pattern would drop its file with no error at all.  A
‘property-resolved’ conjunct therefore pushes a *widened* pattern, the
local ‘:NAME:’ line or a ‘:PROTOTYPE:’ line as one alternation; and
where the registry declares an ‘:ATTR_DEFAULT:’ the query could match,
an entry spelling *neither* line matches too, nothing can narrow, and
the conjunct stays residual.  Both cost candidate files and never an
answer.  See *note Inheritance and pushability:: for the full account.


File: org-agents.info,  Node: Appearance,  Next: Action code,  Prev: Prototypes,  Up: Top

12 Appearance
*************

In Tinderbox a note's colour is an attribute, and a rule or an agent
action assigns it: "an agent that contains 'urgent tasks' can
automatically change their color in order to draw your attention."  The
idea is that a document's appearance should follow from its data rather
than from a separate act of formatting.

   org-agents keeps the idea and changes the mechanism completely, for
one reason: in Tinderbox, setting ‘$Color’ is a *write* to a note, and
here it would be a write to a text file under version control.  So
appearance in org-agents is a *declaration* that is read, never an
action that is applied.

* Menu:

* Faces from attribute values::  org-agents-faces-mode.
* The ATTR_FACES syntax::        How an attribute's values are mapped to faces.
* From declaration to headline:: A worked appearance example.
* It changes no bytes::          One font-lock keyword, and nothing else.
* Faces in every Org buffer::    global-org-agents-faces-mode.
* Precedence, and following an edit: Precedence and following an edit.  Where the face sits in the list, and the one gap.
* The two refusals::             No geometry, and no writes.


File: org-agents.info,  Node: Faces from attribute values,  Next: The ATTR_FACES syntax,  Up: Appearance

12.1 Faces from attribute values
================================

‘org-agents-faces-mode’ is a buffer-local minor mode, lighter ‘"
Attrs"’, which draws a headline in the face that its *resolved* value
for a declared attribute names.  It needs an Org buffer and refuses
politely in anything else.

   Resolved is the point of the mode.  An entry that spells only
‘:PROTOTYPE: Task’, or that spells nothing at all and takes
‘:ATTR_DEFAULT:’, is faced exactly as one that spells the value in its
own drawer.  Grep does not see an inherited value and neither does the
drawer under your eye; the face does, and reading a corpus is what that
is for.

   Where several declared attributes name faces, every one of them is
consulted in *registry declaration order*, and the first that resolves
to a value it maps wins.


File: org-agents.info,  Node: The ATTR_FACES syntax,  Next: From declaration to headline,  Prev: Faces from attribute values,  Up: Appearance

12.2 The ATTR_FACES syntax
==========================

‘:ATTR_FACES:’ is pipe-separated two-word groups: a value, whitespace, a
face name.

     :ATTR_FACES: blocked error | paused shadow | done success

   Which reads: draw a headline whose resolved ‘STATUS’ is ‘blocked’ in
‘error’, ‘paused’ in ‘shadow’, and ‘done’ in ‘success’.  A value the
declaration does not mention maps to no face, and such a headline is
left exactly as Org drew it.

   Face names are read with ‘intern’ and never with ‘read’, because the
registry is data.  A declaration that does not split cleanly into
two-word groups yields no faces, warned once; it costs the faces and not
the rest of the declaration.


File: org-agents.info,  Node: From declaration to headline,  Next: It changes no bytes,  Prev: The ATTR_FACES syntax,  Up: Appearance

12.3 From declaration to headline
=================================

Take the registry's ‘STATUS’ declaration from *note The registry file::,
whose ‘:ATTR_FACES:’ is the line above and whose ‘:ATTR_DEFAULT:’ is
‘active’, and the corpus from *note The resolution order::.  Turn the
mode on and fontify.  Measured, headline by headline, with the resolved
value beside the ‘face’ text property actually on the line:

     Projects                 STATUS=active   face=org-level-1
     Rewrite the exporter     STATUS=active   face=(org-todo org-level-2)
     Audit the prefilter      STATUS=blocked  face=(error org-todo org-level-2)
     Ship 0.1                 STATUS=done     face=(success org-done org-level-2)
     Errands                  STATUS=active   face=org-level-1
     Renew the passport       STATUS=active   face=(org-todo org-level-2)
     Collect the print        STATUS=active   face=(org-todo org-level-2)
     Reading                  STATUS=active   face=org-level-1
     Tinderbox notes          STATUS=active   face=org-level-2

   Three things to read out of that run.

   ‘Audit the prefilter’ is drawn in ‘error’ although its drawer spells
no ‘:STATUS:’ at all: the value arrives through its prototype chain,
which is exactly why the mode reads the resolved value.

   ‘active’ maps to no face, so most of the corpus is left as Org drew
it.  The mode is a way of marking the exceptional, not a way of
colouring everything.

   The attribute's face is *prepended* to what Org already put there,
not substituted for it.  So ‘Ship 0.1’ reads ‘(success org-done
org-level-2)’: the colour follows the attribute while the height, the
family and Org's own done-state face all survive underneath.


File: org-agents.info,  Node: It changes no bytes,  Next: Faces in every Org buffer,  Prev: From declaration to headline,  Up: Appearance

12.4 It changes no bytes
========================

The whole of this mode is *one font-lock keyword*.  No text is inserted,
no property is written into the file, no overlay is created.  The ‘face’
text property that font-lock manages, and removes again, is the entire
mechanism.

   Measured on the run above: ‘(buffer-modified-p)’ afterwards is nil,
and turning the mode off leaves no ‘face’ property behind.  The third
headline of that corpus reads ‘(error org-todo org-level-2)’ with the
mode on and ‘(org-todo org-level-2)’ with it off, which is Org's own
answer and nothing more.

   That is what makes the mode safe to leave on in a file under version
control, and it is the difference between reading an attribute for
appearance and writing a ‘$Color’ into a note.


File: org-agents.info,  Node: Faces in every Org buffer,  Next: Precedence and following an edit,  Prev: It changes no bytes,  Up: Appearance

12.5 Faces in every Org buffer
==============================

‘global-org-agents-faces-mode’ turns ‘org-agents-faces-mode’ on in
*every* Org buffer, and deliberately not only in those whose text
mentions a declared property.

   That is a considered difference from ‘global-org-agents-mode’, which
does scan a buffer's text for ‘:AGENT_QUERY:’ before arming it.  The two
tests differ because the two features differ: an agent is spelled in the
file it lives in, so a text scan finds every buffer that has one, while
*a value arriving through a prototype or a registry default is spelled
nowhere*.  A text scan would miss precisely the entries this mode exists
for.

   It is affordable because the matcher answers nil before resolving
anything where nothing declares a face, and because it reads only the
entry at its match and honours the region font-lock hands it.  Measured
over a 400-entry buffer of which one entry is drawn, under a registry
declaring two face-bearing attributes, a twelve-line window costs 5
resolutions where the whole buffer costs 799; a headline outside the
fontified region carries no ‘face’ property at all.  Under a registry
declaring one face-bearing attribute the same figures are 3 against 400.
The cost scales with what is displayed and with how many attributes name
faces, not with the size of the corpus.


File: org-agents.info,  Node: Precedence and following an edit,  Next: The two refusals,  Prev: Faces in every Org buffer,  Up: Appearance

12.6 Precedence, and following an edit
======================================

The keyword is added at the *end* of ‘font-lock-keywords’ and applies
with ‘prepend’.  Both are load-bearing.  Org's own headline keyword
applies only where no face is set yet, so whichever keyword runs first
claims the headline outright: added at the front, this mode's face
destroys ‘org-level-N’ and takes the height and the family with it.
Added last and prepending, it wins attribute by attribute and Org's face
survives underneath, which is the ‘(error org-todo org-level-2)’ above.

   The face *follows an edit to the value it is drawn from*.  The face
is on the headline and the value is in the drawer below it, and an
ordinary edit refontifies only its own line, so the mode extends the
region font-lock was about to redraw to include the headline above the
drawer.

   *The one gap.*  An edit to the *registry*, or to a *master in another
buffer*, is not followed.  A buffer already fontified keeps the colour
it was drawn with until something refontifies it: ‘M-x’
‘font-lock-flush’, or scrolling away and back.  Nothing here watches
other files, and a mode that did would be watching the whole corpus.


File: org-agents.info,  Node: The two refusals,  Prev: Precedence and following an edit,  Up: Appearance

12.7 The two refusals
=====================

Two things a reader may look for here are refusals rather than
omissions, and both are worth stating in the same breath as the feature.

   *No geometry.*  Tinderbox's ‘$Width’, ‘$Height’, ‘$Xpos’ and ‘$Ypos’
describe a map view.  Org has no map, so there is nothing in an outline
for them to mean: they are not ported, and no spelling of them is
reserved.  See *note What was deliberately not ported and why::.

   *No writes of any kind from an appearance declaration.*  Setting a
tag or a TODO state from a resolved value would be action code arriving
through another door, and *inheritable* action code at that: a master's
declaration running in every follower's file makes per-file trust
meaningless.  Anything that edits your corpus goes through the
action-code trust model or not at all.  See *note The trust model::.


File: org-agents.info,  Node: Action code,  Next: Reference,  Prev: Appearance,  Up: Top

13 Action code
**************

An agent may carry an *action* as well as a query: ‘:AGENT_ACTION:’,
holding a declarative sentence over a fixed vocabulary of nine verbs,
each of which edits the entries the query matched.

   This is Tinderbox's ‘$AgentAction’, and it is the one part of
org-agents that writes outside the agent's own file.  Three refusals are
what make that affordable to reason about, and they come before the
syntax because they are the more important half.

