13.1 The three refusals
=======================

*An action never runs on save, on a timer, or from either minor mode.*
The only thing in this package that runs an action is
‘org-agents-apply-actions’, typed.  That is *structural* rather than a
promise kept by discipline: the action text is not part of the plist the
save path reads, so it does not exist as data anywhere along it.  The
one read of ‘:AGENT_ACTION:’ in the whole package is inside that
command.

   Tinderbox has ‘$Rule’, performed at frequent intervals, and ‘$Edict’,
performed at startup and roughly hourly.  Neither is ported.  A
Tinderbox document is one binary file the application owns while it is
open; an Org corpus is thousands of text files under version control
that other tools also write.  Code that edits those files while you are
not looking is a commit nobody authored.  See *note What was
deliberately not ported and why:: for the rest of that accounting, in
Tinderbox's own vocabulary.

   *An action is not inheritable.*  ‘:AGENT_ACTION:’ is read from the
entry's own drawer, with ‘org-entry-get’ and no INHERIT argument, and
never through ‘org-agents-resolve-property’.  So no prototype, no
outline ancestor, no ‘#+PROPERTY:’ line and no ‘org-global-properties’
setting can supply one.  The reason is per-file trust: if any of those
could, then the code that edits your corpus when you act on file A would
be written in file B, and reading a file before trusting it would mean
nothing.  This is a deliberate divergence from Tinderbox, whose ‘$Rule’
is normally inherited and whose own worked example is a rule inherited
from a ‘Task’ prototype.

   *Nothing in the property is ever evaluated.*  The parser is a regexp
lexer; a token becomes a function by name construction plus ‘fboundp’;
arguments reach a verb as strings.  There is no expression language, no
assignment, no conditional, no iteration, and nothing resembling
Tinderbox's ‘runCommand’.  The worst thing expressible in an
‘:AGENT_ACTION:’ is a bounded, greppable Org edit.


File: org-agents.info,  Node: Writing an action,  Next: The nine verbs,  Prev: The three refusals,  Up: Action code

13.2 Writing an action
======================

An action is a sentence of verbs, applied left to right at every matched
entry:

     * Stamp what I reviewed today
       :PROPERTIES:
       :AGENT_QUERY:  (and (todo "TODO") (property "NEXT_REVIEW"))
       :AGENT_ACTION: set-property!(REVIEWED, today) tag!(+reviewed)
       :END:

   Every verb's name ends in ‘!’, which is the convention this
vocabulary uses to mark that it writes.  Arguments go in parentheses,
separated by commas, and may be quoted or bare: a bare argument is
anything but a comma, a parenthesis or a quotation mark, and a quoted
argument admits backslash escapes.  A verb taking no argument,
‘archive!’, is written without parentheses.

   The parser reads no buffer, creates none and writes nothing.  Every
diagnostic it can raise fires before the query has run, so a typo does
not even open the corpus:

     org-agents: :AGENT_ACTION: `frobnicate!' is not an action verb
       (there is no `org-agents-action/frobnicate!')
     org-agents: :AGENT_ACTION: `archive!' must be the last verb

   Everything else is checked in the *plan* phase, at the match, before
anything is written.  That is where a verb learns which buffer it is in,
which is what a check against buffer-local TODO keywords needs.


File: org-agents.info,  Node: The nine verbs,  Next: Value keywords,  Prev: Writing an action,  Up: Action code

13.3 The nine verbs
===================

The vocabulary is closed at nine.  Their exact argument lists are in
*note Action verbs::; what follows is what each is for and what it will
not do.

   ‘set-property!(NAME, VALUE)’ sets a property.  It refuses several
names, and each refusal closes a hole rather than tidying the
vocabulary.  Org's *special properties* are refused with the verb that
does edit them named instead: ‘TODO’ sends you to ‘todo!’, ‘PRIORITY’ to
‘priority!’, ‘SCHEDULED’ to ‘scheduled!’, ‘DEADLINE’ to ‘deadline!’,
‘TAGS’ to ‘tag!’.  Any ‘AGENT_’ name and ‘PROTOTYPE’ are refused because
an action that could write them would *plant* an agent, a prototype link
or action text into a file you have never edited, undoing the per-file
trust the non-inheritance rule exists to keep.  ‘ARCHIVE’ is refused
because it steers where ‘archive!’ puts a subtree, and the dry run would
have named the destination it read before that.

   ‘delete-property!(NAME)’ removes a property.  It is destructive: see
*note Destructive verbs and batch::.

   ‘tag!(+added -removed)’ adds and removes tags, given
whitespace-separated *signed* terms applied left to right.  An unsigned
term is refused:

     org-agents: :AGENT_ACTION: `tag!' term `reviewed' must be `+TAG'
       to add it or `-TAG' to remove it

   This is *deliberately not* org-edna's ‘tag!’, and the divergence is
the point.  That one hands a whole tag specification to ‘org-set-tags’
and therefore *replaces* the entry's tags.  Over an agent's match set
that is a silent mass deletion, from a verb whose one-word form looks
additive.

   ‘todo!(STATE)’ sets the TODO keyword.  STATE is checked against
‘org-todo-keywords-1’ *read in the match's own buffer*, because those
keywords are buffer-local and a corpus may spell different ones file by
file.  A *repeating* entry is refused, and this one is a disappointment
taken deliberately: measured, ‘org-todo’ on a repeating entry does not
leave it in the state it was asked for at all, but shifts the stamp and
writes ‘:LAST_REPEAT:’.  A dry run cannot show any of that, and the line
you approved said ‘TODO -> DONE’.  Advancing a repeater is a per-entry
judgement, which is what ‘C-c C-t’ is for.

   ‘priority!(LETTER)’ takes one upper-case letter inside
‘org-priority-highest’ to ‘org-priority-lowest’, and names the range
when it refuses.

   ‘scheduled!(DATE)’ and ‘deadline!(DATE)’ take the same shapes and
make the same refusal.  The shape check exists to close a measured
hazard: ‘(org-read-date nil nil "junk")’ answers with *today's date,
silently*, so a naive version of ‘scheduled!(nextweek)’ would
mass-schedule a corpus to today and report nothing wrong.

     org-agents: :AGENT_ACTION: `scheduled!' takes a date `2026-08-27',
       a date and time `2026-08-27 14:00', an offset `+7d', `today' or
       `now' -- not `nextweek'

   ‘effort!(VALUE)’ writes an effort.  The argument stays a *string*,
because Org's effort values are text: ‘0:30’, ‘2d’.  Nothing in this
vocabulary calls ‘string-to-number’ at all.

   ‘archive!’ archives the entry's subtree.  It is destructive and it is
*terminal*: it must be the last verb and may appear once, since it
removes the subtree a later verb would edit.  The parser refuses the
ordering rather than letting an apply pass discover it.  Its destination
is computed without archiving, so the dry run can say where the subtree
is about to go, and computed *again* immediately before archiving, with
the subtree left alone where the two disagree.


File: org-agents.info,  Node: Value keywords,  Next: The dry run,  Prev: The nine verbs,  Up: Action code

13.4 Value keywords
===================

Three bare words are interpreted rather than stored:

‘today’
     An *inactive* date stamp, as ‘[2026-08-20 Thu]’.
‘now’
     An *inactive* date and time stamp.
‘empty’
     The empty string.

   Inactive deliberately: a value written into a property drawer must
not put the entry on the agenda.

   The keywords are interpreted *in the verbs and never in the parser*,
which is not an implementation detail you can ignore.  The parser cannot
know which argument is a value, because ‘set-property!’'s first argument
is a property *name* and must stay literal.  So a verb interprets its
own value arguments, and a verb that has no value argument interprets
nothing.

   *A quoted argument is never a keyword.*  ‘set-property!(REVIEWED,
today)’ stores today's date; ‘set-property!(REVIEWED, "today")’ stores
the five letters.  Quoting is the escape hatch, and it is the only one.

   The date-taking verbs accept ‘today’ and ‘now’ too, and expand them
themselves rather than handing them to ‘org-read-date’, so what is
scheduled is what the word says.  Their other shapes are an ISO date, an
ISO date and time, and an Org offset such as ‘+7d’ or ‘++2w’.


File: org-agents.info,  Node: The dry run,  Next: The blast radius,  Prev: Value keywords,  Up: Action code

13.5 The dry run
================

Put point in the agent and type ‘M-x’ ‘org-agents-apply-actions’.
Nothing is written yet.  What happens first is a *dry run*: every verb
is called in its ‘plan’ phase at every match, which writes nothing and
is caught if it does, and the result is a report in ‘*Org Agents
Actions*’, one line per intended edit.

   For the agent in *note Writing an action::, over the corpus of *note
A corpus to work with:::

     org-agents: 6 edits at 3 entries in 1 file (1 not open before this ran).
       Nothing written yet.
     …/corpus.org:5: set-property!(REVIEWED, today)  nil -> [2026-08-20 Thu]
     …/corpus.org:5: tag!(+reviewed)  "" -> :reviewed:
     …/corpus.org:14: set-property!(REVIEWED, today)  nil -> [2026-08-20 Thu]
     …/corpus.org:14: tag!(+reviewed)  "" -> :reviewed:
     …/corpus.org:28: set-property!(REVIEWED, today)  nil -> [2026-08-20 Thu]
     …/corpus.org:28: tag!(+reviewed)  "" -> :reviewed:

   Read the report before answering anything.  ‘FILE:LINE:’ comes first
on every line so ‘next-error’ walks them; the arrow shows old and new;
the header counts both edits and entries, and says how many of the files
were not already open.  A row that would change nothing says so and is
not counted as an edit, and where no row would change anything the
command says that and asks nothing, because "apply 0 edits?"  is not
informed consent.

   Then you are asked *once*, about the whole plan:

     org-agents: apply 6 edits at 3 entries in 1 file (1 not open before this ran)?

   Answer yes and the edits are applied in order, with the hooks and the
logging that would edit anything the report does not name bound off.  An
error, a ‘no’, a ‘C-g’, or an entry that did not end up as its line said
stops at that edit: that line says what happened, and every later line
says ‘not attempted’.  Finally the buffers that were modified are named:

     org-agents: applied 6, refused 0, failed 0, skipped 0, 0 not attempted;
       modified corpus.org; nothing was saved -- see `*Org Agents Actions*'

   *Nothing is saved.*  Measured on that run: the buffer is modified and
the file on disk is byte-for-byte what it was.  Not even ‘archive!’
saves; the archive file is left modified like any other buffer, and
named.


File: org-agents.info,  Node: The blast radius,  Next: How much one run may edit,  Prev: The dry run,  Up: Action code

13.6 The blast radius
=====================

*The blast radius of a run is the rows the report shows.*  That sentence
is the whole of the safety argument, and three properties of the command
are what make it true.

   The match set is computed exactly as an update computes it: gated,
prefiltered, with the agent itself dropped from its own match set and
every generated alias excluded by ‘org-agents-exclude’.  So an agent's
action never edits the agent, and never edits an alias some other agent
wrote.

   The plan pass writes nothing, and is checked rather than trusted:
every live buffer's modification tick is taken before the pass and
compared after, because a promise about one buffer is not a promise
about the corpus.

   The apply pass runs with hooks and logging bound off, so nothing
arrives in the files that the report did not name.


File: org-agents.info,  Node: How much one run may edit,  Next: Destructive verbs and batch,  Prev: The blast radius,  Up: Action code

13.7 How much one run may edit
==============================

‘org-agents-action-limit’ is how many *entries* one run will edit.  It
defaults to 100, and that default is chosen so that a mistake is
reviewable, not for performance.

   A plan over more entries than the limit is *refused* rather than
truncated, naming the count, the limit and the option:

     org-agents: 2 entries matched and `org-agents-action-limit' is 1, so
       nothing was planned; raise the option in init to act on more -- a
       truncated plan would apply a subset you could not predict

   Three details of that design are deliberate.  The gate is on
*entries* and not on edits, because entries are what you reason about:
an agent matches four hundred things, not four hundred times two verbs.
The *dry run is never capped*, whatever the limit is set to, because a
cap on planning would make the report understate what applying would do,
which is the one thing the report may not do.  And raising it is a
decision taken *in init*: the option is risky, so no file can set it for
you.

   There is deliberately no other bound.  An agent that legitimately
stamps three thousand entries must raise this option before it will run,
and that is the right cost: the three-thousand-entry case is exactly the
alarming one.  Since nothing is saved, the worst case of a bad run is N
modified buffers, reviewable and undoable one at a time, and this option
is the bound on N.


File: org-agents.info,  Node: Destructive verbs and batch,  Next: The same command over a selection,  Prev: How much one run may edit,  Up: Action code

13.8 Destructive verbs and batch
================================

Two verbs remove information rather than change it: ‘delete-property!’
and ‘archive!’.  They confirm *at every entry, on every run*.  There is
no remembered approval, no option that silences it, and nothing to set.

   In batch, or under ‘inhibit-interaction’, a confirmation is a
*refusal and not a default*:

     org-agents: apply 6 edits at 3 entries in 1 file
       (1 not open before this ran)?  -- refused, because there is no one to ask

   The check comes before ‘y-or-n-p’ is ever reached, so neither of
those conditions reads standard input and neither can hang.  That
matters more than it looks: measured, in ‘-batch’, ‘(y-or-n-p "ok? ")’
with a ‘y’ on standard input returns t, and with standard input closed
signals ‘end-of-file’ from inside whatever called it.  A script, a CI
job, or any invocation whose standard input happened to carry text would
otherwise have answered yes on your behalf.


File: org-agents.info,  Node: The same command over a selection,  Next: The trust model,  Prev: Destructive verbs and batch,  Up: Action code

13.9 The same command over a selection
======================================

With a *prefix argument*, ‘org-agents-apply-actions’ acts on the entries
the links in the *region* name rather than on the agent's whole match
set.  The action text still comes from the agent's own drawer.

   That is what makes a stamp fall out of this command: a stamp is this
command pointed at a selection, and the region is the selection.  Select
some of the agent's alias headings, or some rows of its table, or some
items of its list, and only those entries are planned.  The first
bracket link on each line is what is read, using the same reading that
recognizes an alias, so all three views are served by one mechanism, in
order and without repeats.

   The prefix argument *is* the request, so what has to be true is that
there is a mark and that it is somewhere other than point.  An empty
region, or none at all, is refused with a message saying what the prefix
argument means.  The two protections of *note The blast radius:: still
hold: the agent's own entry is skipped, and a generated alias is not a
target.


File: org-agents.info,  Node: The trust model,  Next: Writing a verb of your own,  Prev: The same command over a selection,  Up: Action code

13.10 The trust model
=====================

Stated once, in plain words, because the rest of this chapter is
consequences of it.

   *A property drawer is file content, and file content is not trusted.*
An Org file may have arrived from anywhere: a colleague, a repository, a
mail attachment, an export from some other tool.  Anything a file can
say to this package must therefore be something whose worst case you are
willing to accept from a stranger.

   So the vocabulary is closed and nothing in it is evaluated: the worst
sentence an ‘:AGENT_ACTION:’ can spell is a bounded Org edit that
ripgrep can find afterwards.  Nothing runs unless you type the command.
Nothing reaches disk unless you say yes to a report that named every
edit.  The destructive verbs ask again, every time.  The bound on how
much one run may do is raised only from init, which no file can write.

   The rule underneath all of it: *file content may only tighten*.  A
file can narrow what happens to your corpus; only your init and your
keystrokes can widen it.


File: org-agents.info,  Node: Writing a verb of your own,  Next: Limitations of action code,  Prev: The trust model,  Up: Action code

13.11 Writing a verb of your own
================================

A verb is resolved by *name construction plus* ‘fboundp’: the token
‘frobnicate!’ becomes the function ‘org-agents-action/frobnicate!’ if
such a function is bound, and an error naming both if it is not.  So a
tenth verb is a ‘defun’ in your init file with that name.

   The contract has three parts:

   • The function's first argument is a *phase*, either ‘plan’ or
     ‘apply’.  Its remaining arguments are the verb's own, and they
     arrive as *strings* in the order written.
   • In the ‘plan’ phase it must *write nothing* and answer a cons of
     the old value and the new, ‘(OLD . NEW)’, which is what the
     report's ‘old -> new’ is.  A plan pass that writes is caught.
   • In the ‘apply’ phase it makes the edit, at point, in the match's
     own buffer.

   Because the resolution is by name, defining such a function is what
grants the verb to every ‘:AGENT_ACTION:’ in your corpus.  That is the
intended design and it is also the thing to think about before writing
one: a verb is a capability you are adding to file content, so give it
the same narrowness the nine have.


File: org-agents.info,  Node: Limitations of action code,  Prev: Writing a verb of your own,  Up: Action code

13.12 Limitations of action code
================================

*Applying actions over a corpus scope leaves every file in scope
visited.*  The command reaches its match set through the same collection
an update uses, which opens each candidate file with
‘find-file-noselect’ and kills nothing afterwards.  That is the
footprint *note Limitations of the registry:: already describes, with
one difference that matters: some of those buffers are now *modified*.

   Nothing is saved, which is the point, so the corpus on disk is
exactly as it was.  But a run over an ‘all’ scope can leave a few
thousand buffers open and some tens of them modified, and *there is no
corpus-wide undo to put them back with*.  Undo is per buffer, and the
report in ‘*Org Agents Actions*’ is the list of which ones to look at.

   The remedies are the ordinary ones, and there are two, used together.
Keep ‘org-agents-action-limit’ low enough that a mistake is reviewable.
And work through the report before doing anything else: ‘next-error’
walks the edits, ‘undo’ in a buffer takes back the edits made in it, and
‘M-x’ ‘ibuffer’ with ‘* u’ finds what is still modified.  Run an action
over an unbounded scope deliberately rather than casually, and prefer an
explicit ‘:AGENT_SCOPE:’ list where you can name the files.

   For the features org-agents does not have here at all, and why, see
*note What was deliberately not ported and why::: no timer, no startup
pass, no action on an outline edit, and no inheritable action.


