14 Reference
************

What follows is the complete vocabulary, arranged for looking things up
rather than for reading through.  Each entry says what the thing is and
points at the chapter that explains why.

   The package establishes *no key bindings*.  The only keymap it
defines is local to the ‘*org-agents approvals*’ buffer.  Keys named in
this manual are Org's own, or ones you are invited to bind yourself.

* Menu:

* Commands::                     The interactive commands, by name.
* Minor modes::                  The four modes, by name.
* User options::                 All ten, with their defaults.
* Agent properties::             All ten, with their defaults and refusals.
* Registry properties::          The four ATTR_ properties, by name.
* Action verbs::                 The nine verbs of AGENT_ACTION, by name.
* Functions for Lisp callers::   The predicate and the resolver, for your own code.


File: org-agents.info,  Node: Commands,  Next: Minor modes,  Up: Reference

14.1 Commands
=============

 -- Command: org-agents-update
     Update the agent at point, or the ‘org-agents’ block point sits in.
     With point inside a block, that block alone is written; otherwise
     the whole agent is.  Writes ‘:AGENT_MATCHED:’, leaves the buffer
     modified and unsaved.  See *note By hand::.

 -- Command: org-agents-update-buffer
     Update every agent in the current buffer, continuing past failures
     and naming each one that failed.  A dynamic block supplying its own
     ‘:query’ is left alone.  See *note By hand::.

 -- Command: org-agents-update-all
     Update every agent in the files ‘org-agents-files’ names.  Files
     are left modified rather than saved.  See *note By hand::.

 -- Command: org-agents-preview
     Read a query from the minibuffer and show what an agent with that
     query would match, over ‘org-agenda-files’, writing nothing.  Gated
     exactly as an agent's query is.  See *note Looking before you
     write::.

 -- Command: org-agents-insert-dblock
     Insert an empty ‘org-agents’ dynamic block at point and leave it
     unwritten.  What ‘C-c C-x x’ offers.  See *note Inserting a
     block::.

 -- Command: org-agents-check-attributes
     Lint a scope against the attribute registry and report: a property
     name in use that nothing declares, a value that does not parse as
     its declared type, a value outside its declared vocabulary.
     Reports into ‘*org-agents attributes*’ in ‘compilation-mode’; edits
     nothing.  Reads its scope from the same vocabulary as
     ‘:AGENT_SCOPE:’.  See *note Checking a corpus::.

 -- Command: org-agents-attribute-columns
     Return a ‘COLUMNS’ format string over the named declared
     attributes, ‘%ITEM’ first and a ‘{+}’ summary cookie on an
     attribute declared ‘number’, as in ‘%ITEM %STATUS %REVIEWS{+}’.
     With a prefix argument, also set the result as the ‘:COLUMNS:’
     *property* of the entry at point, which is what descendants inherit
     and what ‘org-agenda-columns’ reads.  Refuses an undeclared name,
     and a declared name a ‘COLUMNS’ format cannot spell.  See *note A
     corpus-wide column view::.

 -- Command: org-agents-list-approvals
     Pop ‘*org-agents approvals*’, a listing of every remembered
     approval and refusal with the query text its hash covers.  See
     *note Reviewing what you approved::.

 -- Command: org-agents-apply-actions
     Print a dry run of what this agent's ‘:AGENT_ACTION:’ would do to
     the entries its query matches, one ‘FILE:LINE:’ line per intended
     edit, and write nothing until the whole plan is agreed to.  With a
     prefix argument, act on the entries the links in the region name
     instead.  The only thing in the package that runs an action.  See
     *note The dry run::.

 -- Function: org-agents-allowed-values
     Not a command.  Written for ‘org-property-allowed-value-functions’:
     one argument, a property name, answering a flat list of the values
     the registry declares for it, or nil for a property it is not
     responsible for.  It is not added to that hook for you.  See *note
     Completing declared values::.

   Three more commands work only in the approvals listing:

 -- Command: org-agents-approvals-revoke
     Forget the approval on this line, in the session and on disk.
     Bound to ‘d’ in ‘*org-agents approvals*’.  See *note Reviewing what
     you approved::.

 -- Command: org-agents-approvals-refuse
     Forget the approval on this line and record the query as refused,
     which the gate consults before anything else and which no later
     approval undoes.  Bound to ‘r’.  See *note Reviewing what you
     approved::.

 -- Command: org-agents-approvals-unrefuse
     Lift a refusal.  The query goes back to needing approval, not to
     having it.  Bound to ‘u’.  See *note Reviewing what you approved::.

   Three key sequences reach this package, and all three are Org's:

‘C-c C-c’
     Inside an ‘org-agents’ dynamic block, runs ‘org-update-dblock’ and
     so reaches this package's writer.

‘C-c C-x x’
     ‘org-dynamic-block-insert-dblock’, which offers ‘org-agents’ among
     the block types.

‘C-c C-x C-u’
     ‘org-dblock-update’: the dynamic block at point, or with a prefix
     argument every dynamic block in the buffer.


