14.3 User options
=================

Every one of these is marked risky, which means Emacs will not set it
from a file-local variables block without your explicit confirmation.
The reason is stated in each entry.

 -- User Option: org-agents-files
     Default ‘("~/org/agents.org")’.  A list of files and directories,
     or the symbol ‘agenda’ for ‘org-agenda-files’.  Says which files
     ‘org-agents-update-all’ opens *and writes*; a directory contributes
     the Org files below it, recursively.  Risky for that reason.  Not
     to be confused with ‘:AGENT_SCOPE:’, which says which files an
     agent reads.  See *note By hand::.

 -- User Option: org-agents-exclude
     Default ‘(not (property "AGENT_MATCH"))’.  Lisp conjoined into
     every agent query and every preview, appended last so cheap
     predicates short-circuit first.  It is what keeps agents from
     matching each other's generated aliases; set it to nil to match
     aliases like any other entry.  Applied on the Emacs side only,
     never in the prefilter.  This value is part of the form the gate
     approves, so editing it invalidates every remembered approval--and
     no refusal.  See *note The structural gate::.

 -- User Option: org-agents-prefilter
     Default ‘auto’.  Whether an unbounded scope's files may be narrowed
     with ripgrep before org-ql opens them.  Evaluation is always
     org-ql's, against live buffers; a prefilter only chooses which
     *files* org-ql opens, so it can never change an answer.  Only
     ‘active’, ‘all’ and a directory scope are ever prefiltered, because
     ‘agenda’ and an explicit file list already name their files.

     ‘auto’
          Use ripgrep when ‘org-agents-rg-executable’ is found and the
          query offers a conjunct to push.  Otherwise scan live, with
          one message naming the file count so that the slowness is
          explained rather than mysterious:

               org-agents: scope ‘.’ not narrowed (no pushable conjunct); scanning
               6 files live

          The reason in parentheses is one of ‘prefiltering off’, ‘no
          pushable conjunct’, ‘ripgrep not found’ or ‘ripgrep failed’.

     ‘require’
          Refuse an unbounded scope that cannot be narrowed, rather than
          scanning it live, naming the scope and the reason:

               org-agents: scope ‘.’ cannot be narrowed (no pushable conjunct) and
               ‘org-agents-prefilter’ is ‘require’; for live evaluation set it to
               ‘auto’, or use ‘agenda’ or an explicit file list

          For someone who would rather be told that an agent cannot be
          answered affordably than wait for a live walk of a whole
          corpus.  It refuses a _query_; ‘org-agents-check-attributes’
          is deliberately outside it and scans live instead, because a
          lint that declined to run would be failing its own contract.

     ‘nil’
          Never run ripgrep; read every scope live.

     What the prefilter buys is *buffers, not regexp work*, and the
     difference between the two paths over a corpus scope is a
     difference of kind rather than of degree; the measurements are in
     *note Which scopes get narrowed and why::.  Risky, because it
     chooses whether a subprocess is spawned at all.  See *note Bounding
     a run::.

 -- User Option: org-agents-rg-executable
     Default ‘"rg"’.  The program the prefilter runs, resolved with
     ‘executable-find’.  ripgrep 13 or later is wanted, because the
     prefilter passes ‘--crlf’; the flags were verified against 15.2.0
     and no other version.  With no ripgrep found, the answer is the
     same as a failed run: the scope is scanned live, with the message
     that says so.  Risky, because it names a program that will be run
     as a subprocess.

 -- User Option: org-agents-rg-timeout
     Default ‘30’.  Seconds to wait for one prefilter run, or nil for no
     bound.  A bound is needed because nothing else provides one: on an
     unresponsive filesystem a run can take arbitrarily long.  It is a
     deadline over an asynchronous process rather than a timer, because
     a timer set before a synchronous ‘call-process’ to a hanging child
     never fires--Emacs runs no timers while blocked there.  On expiry
     the answer is the same "unavailable" a missing ripgrep gives, so
     the scope is scanned live: a spurious expiry costs a slow correct
     answer and never a wrong one.  The new cost is reentrancy, since
     waiting on a subprocess runs timers; see *note Limitations of
     dynamic blocks:: and *note Bounding a run::.  Risky: a file-local
     nil takes the bound off, and a file-local 0 expires every run.

 -- User Option: org-agents-attributes-file
     Default ‘"~/org/attributes.org"’.  The attribute registry.
     Optional: a missing or unreadable file declares nothing and says
     nothing about it.  Pure data--nothing in it is ever evaluated.
     Risky because it says which file is read on every property
     completion in every Org buffer, and what vocabulary is then
     offered.  See *note The registry file::.

 -- User Option: org-agents-safe-queries
     Default nil.  The record of queries you have approved, managed the
     way ‘safe-local-variable-values’ is: each entry pairs the SHA-1 of
     a printed form with that form's text.  Persisted with
     ‘customize-save-variable’, which writes nothing unless
     ‘user-init-file’ is set, so where your init is a tangled Org file
     you must set ‘custom-file’ to a file of its own or every approval
     is session-only.  Risky, because it is the record the gate
     consults.  See *note Remembered approvals::.

 -- User Option: org-agents-refused-queries
     Default nil.  The record of queries you have refused, in the same
     shape.  A refusal is consulted before anything else and no later
     approval undoes it.  Risky for the same reason.  See *note Refused
     queries::.

 -- User Option: org-agents-refused-heads
     Default ‘(semantic)’.  Predicate heads the gate refuses before the
     safe list and before any remembered approval, and which
     ‘org-ql-ask-unsafe-queries’ does not override.  Matching is by
     literal symbol, so an alias of an offending predicate must be
     listed too.  Risky.  See *note Refused heads::.

 -- User Option: org-agents-action-limit
     Default ‘100’, or nil for no limit.  How many *entries* one
     ‘org-agents-apply-actions’ run will edit.  A plan over more is
     refused rather than truncated, naming the count, the limit and this
     option.  The dry run is never capped.  The gate is on entries
     rather than edits because entries are what you reason about.
     Risky.  See *note How much one run may edit::.


