14.5 Registry properties
========================

Written by you, in the file ‘org-agents-attributes-file’ names, in the
drawer immediately under an attribute's heading.  See *note The
attribute registry::.

 -- Registry Property: ATTR_TYPE
     The attribute's type: one of ‘string’, ‘number’, ‘date’, ‘boolean’,
     ‘set’ or ‘list’.  Required; this property is what makes an entry a
     declaration.  The value is compared as text before it is interned,
     so a misspelling leaves no symbol behind: the entry is named once
     and skipped, declaring nothing.  A bad type costs its entry.  See
     *note The six types::.

 -- Registry Property: ATTR_VALUES
     The values the attribute admits, separated by whitespace.  Absent,
     the attribute admits anything its type does.  A trailing ‘:ETC’
     leaves the vocabulary open, which is Org's own spelling of that,
     and reaches Org unfiltered.  For a ‘boolean’ the property has no
     meaning and is ignored with a warning, the reader synthesizing
     ‘true’ and ‘false’ instead.  See *note The declaration:: and *note
     Completing declared values::.

 -- Registry Property: ATTR_DEFAULT
     The attribute's default, written as text, and the last step of the
     prototype resolution order.  It stays the trimmed string the file
     holds: never a parsed number, never a decoded time.  A default that
     does not validate against the type and the vocabulary is warned
     about and discarded, which costs that field and nothing else.  See
     *note The resolution order::.

 -- Registry Property: ATTR_FACES
     Pipe-separated two-word groups, ‘VALUE FACE | VALUE FACE ...’: what
     ‘org-agents-faces-mode’ draws a headline with when the attribute
     resolves to VALUE at that entry.  Face names are read with ‘intern’
     and never with ‘read’.  A value that does not split cleanly into
     two-word groups yields no faces, warned about once, which costs the
     faces and not the declaration.  See *note The ATTR_FACES syntax::.


File: org-agents.info,  Node: Action verbs,  Next: Functions for Lisp callers,  Prev: Registry properties,  Up: Reference

14.6 Action verbs
=================

The whole vocabulary of ‘:AGENT_ACTION:’, applied only by
‘org-agents-apply-actions’.  Arguments reach a verb as strings and are
never evaluated.  A verb name ends in ‘!’; a value argument may be one
of the keywords in *note Value keywords:: unless it is quoted.  See
*note The nine verbs::.

 -- Action Verb: set-property! name value
     Set property NAME to VALUE at each matched entry.  Refuses any of
     Org's special properties, naming the verb that does edit it;
     refuses any ‘AGENT_’ name and ‘PROTOTYPE’, because an action may
     not plant an agent, a prototype link or action text in another
     file; refuses ‘ARCHIVE’, because it steers where ‘archive!’ puts a
     subtree and the dry run would name the destination it read before
     that.

 -- Action Verb: delete-property! name
     Delete property NAME at each matched entry.  Destructive: it
     confirms at every entry on every run, no remembered approval and no
     option silences it, and in batch it is refused rather than assumed.
     See *note Destructive verbs and batch::.

 -- Action Verb: tag! spec
     Add and remove tags, following SPEC: whitespace-separated *signed*
     terms applied left to right, so ‘tag!(+a -b)’ adds ‘a’ and removes
     ‘b’.  An unsigned term is refused.  Deliberately not org-edna's
     ‘tag!’, which replaces an entry's tags.

 -- Action Verb: todo! state
     Set the entry's TODO keyword to STATE.  STATE is checked against
     ‘org-todo-keywords-1’ read in the match's own buffer, since those
     keywords are buffer-local.  A repeating entry is refused:
     ‘org-todo’ would shift its stamp and write ‘:LAST_REPEAT:’, and the
     dry run cannot show either.

 -- Action Verb: priority! letter
     Set the entry's priority to LETTER: one upper-case letter, inside
     ‘org-priority-highest’ to ‘org-priority-lowest’.  Anything else is
     refused, and the refusal names the range.

 -- Action Verb: scheduled! date
     Schedule the entry for DATE.  Accepts an ISO date ‘2026-08-27’, an
     ISO date and time ‘2026-08-27 14:00’, an Org offset ‘+7d’ or
     ‘++2w’, or the keywords ‘today’ and ‘now’, which this verb expands
     itself.  Anything else is refused, because ‘org-read-date’ answers
     unparsable text with today's date silently.

 -- Action Verb: deadline! date
     Give the entry a deadline of DATE.  The same shapes ‘scheduled!’
     takes, and the same refusal.

 -- Action Verb: effort! value
     Set the entry's effort to VALUE, written to ‘org-effort-property’.
     The argument stays a string, because Org's effort values are text:
     ‘0:30’, ‘2d’.

 -- Action Verb: archive!
     Archive the entry's subtree.  Takes no argument, and is written
     without parentheses.  Destructive, so it confirms at every entry on
     every run; and *terminal*, so it must be the last verb and may
     appear once, which the parser enforces.  The destination is
     computed without archiving so the dry run can name it, and computed
     again immediately before archiving, with the subtree left alone
     where the two disagree.  Nothing is saved: the archive file is left
     modified like any other buffer, and named.


File: org-agents.info,  Node: Functions for Lisp callers,  Prev: Action verbs,  Up: Reference

14.7 Functions for Lisp callers
===============================

 -- Function: property-resolved name &optional value
     An org-ql predicate: the prototype-aware ‘property’.  Matches an
     entry whose *resolved* value for NAME is non-nil, or equal to VALUE
     where one is given.  Resolution is the entry's own drawer, then its
     ‘:PROTOTYPE:’ chain nearest hop first, then the registry's
     ‘:ATTR_DEFAULT:’, so a value the entry does not spell is a match
     here where ‘property’ has none.  VALUE is compared
     case-sensitively.  This is what ‘$NAME^’ expands to.  See *note The
     property-resolved predicate:: and *note The resolution order::.

 -- Function: org-agents-resolve-property name &optional pom
     The resolved value of NAME at POM (point by default) as *text*, or
     nil.  Signals a ‘user-error’ naming the hops where the prototype
     chain has a cycle.

 -- Function: org-agents-resolve-property-quietly name &optional pom
     The same, with a cycle *reported* rather than signalled.  This is
     what residual Lisp and predicates use, because a ‘user-error’
     raised inside org-ql's generated matcher would abort the whole
     update.

 -- Function: org-agents-attribute name
     The registry's declaration of NAME as a plist, or nil.  Keys are
     ‘:name’, ‘:type’, ‘:values’, ‘:default’, ‘:faces’, ‘:doc’, ‘:file’
     and ‘:line’.  See *note The declaration::.

 -- Function: org-agents-attributes
     The declared attribute names, in registry file order.

 -- Function: org-agents-attribute-valid-p type value &optional values
     Whether VALUE is admissible for TYPE, and for VALUES where a
     vocabulary is given.  A predicate, not a diagnosis: it answers yes
     or no and says nothing about why.  See *note The six types::.


