15 History and Acknowledgments
******************************

The model this package implements is not its own.  Agents, prototypes,
action code and user-defined attributes are Eastgate Systems' Tinderbox,
where they have been in continuous use since 2002 and where its
designer, Mark Bernstein, worked out what they should mean: that a query
can be a note, that inheritance should relate two notes rather than
describe where either sits, and that a document should be able to act on
itself.  What follows is an Emacs implementation of those ideas over Org
outlines, arrived at by reading the Tinderbox manual and asking what
each idea would have to become to be honest in a plain-text corpus under
version control.  It is not affiliated with or endorsed by Eastgate
Systems, and the divergences catalogued in this chapter are this
package's responsibility and not Tinderbox's.

* Menu:

* Tinderbox::                    What it is, who makes it, and why its model is worth borrowing.
* What org-agents took::         The five concepts, in Tinderbox's terms, and where each one lives.
* What it changed::              The five deliberate divergences, with the reason for each.
* What was deliberately not ported, and why: What was deliberately not ported and why.  The absences, each with its reason.
* Org and org-ql::               What this package does not reimplement, and who wrote it.
* Trademarks and non-affiliation:: Whose trademark Tinderbox is, and what this package is not.


