15.1 Tinderbox
==============

Tinderbox is a Macintosh application for notes, published by Eastgate
Systems, Inc.  of Watertown, Massachusetts, and designed by Mark
Bernstein.  Its own manual describes _The Tinderbox Way_ as "a book
about the design and philosophy of Tinderbox written by Tinderbox
designer Mark Bernstein" (‘tbxman920:1171’).  The application has been
shipping since 2002; its copyright notice reads "Tinderbox is ©
Copyright 2002-2021 by Eastgate Systems, Inc."  (‘tbxman920:5626’).

   A Tinderbox document holds notes, each with a large space of named,
typed attributes; those attributes drive everything else, including what
a note looks like, what inherits from what, which notes an agent
gathers, and what an action does.  Every claim this manual makes about
Tinderbox is supported by a quotation from the *v9.2* manual, cited by
line number in its plain-text edition as ‘tbxman920:LINE’.  The version
matters: later releases may differ, and nothing here should be read as a
description of any version but that one.

   What makes the model worth borrowing into Emacs is not any one of its
features but the shape of the whole.  Tinderbox treats a query as a
thing you can keep, treats describing an object and filing it as
different acts, and treats a document as something that can be asked to
maintain itself.  Org has the raw material for all three -- a query
language, a property drawer, dynamic blocks -- and had assembled none of
them that way.

   The debt is one of ideas only.  No Tinderbox code, data format or
asset is used, referenced or reproduced here, and nothing in this
package was derived from any of them; the correspondence was worked out
from the published manual.


