15.2 What org-agents took
=========================

Five concepts were taken, and they are named here as Tinderbox names
them.  *note Agents in the Tinderbox tradition:: introduces each one
with the quotations that describe it; this section is the summary and
the map.

Agents
     "Agents are notes that search your Tinderbox document continuously
     for notes that meet the agent's criteria.  If an agent finds a note
     that satisfies its criteria, it automatically places an alias of
     that note inside the agent" (‘tbxman920:1015--1016’).  Here: an
     ordinary Org entry carrying ‘:AGENT_QUERY:’, which is what makes it
     an agent, exactly as ‘$AgentQuery’ is what makes a Tinderbox note
     one.  Its matches become child aliases, a list, or a table.  See
     *note Views:: and *note Updating::.
User Attributes
     "You can add your own user attributes to any Tinderbox document"
     (‘tbxman920:1531’), each with a type, a default, a suggested
     vocabulary and a description (‘tbxman920:1534--1536’,
     ‘1472--1475’).  Here: the registry file, one top-level entry per
     attribute.  See *note The attribute registry::.
Prototypes
     "It shares all the properties of the prototype, except where we
     specify otherwise" (‘tbxman920:1683’), by a checklist that consults
     the note, then its chain, then the default
     (‘tbxman920:1739--1746’), and independently of the outline:
     "inheritance has nothing to do with the document hierarchy"
     (‘tbxman920:1751’).  Here: ‘:PROTOTYPE:’, resolved in that order,
     virtually.  See *note Prototypes:: and *note The resolution
     order::.
Action Code
     "An action describes a change made on one or more notes"
     (‘tbxman920:2795’), and an agent may carry one
     (‘tbxman920:2543--2545’).  Here: ‘:AGENT_ACTION:’, over nine verbs.
     See *note Action code::.
Appearance from attribute values
     Tinderbox's own example is an agent holding urgent tasks, which
     "can automatically change their color in order to draw your
     attention" (‘tbxman920:2792’).  Here: ‘:ATTR_FACES:’ and
     ‘org-agents-faces-mode’.  See *note Appearance::.

   Two smaller borrowings deserve their own mention.  The ‘set’ and
‘list’ attribute types are Tinderbox's, unordered-unique and ordered
respectively: "Lists are strings separated by semicolons"
(‘tbxman920:1428’), and "Sets are best though of as a special form of
List.  Sets only hold one instance of any given value"
(‘tbxman920:1439’).  And the relative date words ‘today’ and ‘now’ that
the action verbs accept are Tinderbox's too: for a date value "you can
also use the terms yesterday, today, and tomorrow, and add or subtract
units of time" (‘tbxman920:1398--1399’).

   *note The vocabulary map:: gives the whole correspondence as one
table, with a pointer for each row.


File: org-agents.info,  Node: What it changed,  Next: What was deliberately not ported and why,  Prev: What org-agents took,  Up: History and Acknowledgments

15.3 What it changed
====================

Five things were changed rather than dropped.  In each case the idea
survives and the mechanism does not, and in each case the reason is the
difference in medium: a Tinderbox document is one binary file that one
application owns while it is open, and an Org corpus is thousands of
plain-text files under version control that other tools also write.

The query language is org-ql's, not Tinderbox's
     Tinderbox agent queries are written in Tinderbox's own expression
     language.  Reimplementing that over Org would have produced a
     second query engine to sit beside the one Org users already have.
     So a query here is an org-ql S-expression, with a ‘$PROP’ reference
     layer on top of it and no engine of its own.  See *note The query
     language::.
An update happens when you ask for one
   Tinderbox agents "scan your document constantly" (‘tbxman920:2528’)
on a priority-driven timer.  org-agents' run on ‘org-agents-update’,
‘org-agents-update-buffer’, ‘org-agents-update-all’, or a save under
‘org-agents-mode’.  A timer that rewrote a corpus would put edits into
files nobody was looking at, and stamps into commits nobody made.  One
consequence is a property Tinderbox has no use for: ‘:AGENT_MATCHED:’
records *when the answer was last computed*, which a continuously
updating agent never needs to say.  Another is that an update which
changes nothing restores the old stamps, so that a save reaches disk
byte-identical.  See *note Updating::.
An alias is a heading, not a proxy
     In Tinderbox, "the attribute values of an alias are almost always
     identical to the values of the original note … If you make the
     original note red, the alias will be red; if you make the alias
     green, the original note will also be green"
     (‘tbxman920:1555--1556’), and "if the original note is deleted, all
     its aliases are also deleted" (‘tbxman920:1557’).  An org-agents
     alias is a heading holding a link: ordinary Org text, written by
     one render and reaped by the next.  Editing it edits nothing but
     itself.  Org has no facility for a bidirectional proxy, and
     pretending otherwise would be worse than admitting it.  In exchange
     there is a contract Tinderbox does not need, under which an alias
     you have written under stops being the package's to delete.  See
     *note The alias contract::.
Attributes are declared in a file, not in a document inspector
     Tinderbox's user attributes are declared through a dialog and
     stored in the document.  Here they are declared in an ordinary Org
     file, so that the declarations are greppable, diffable, and
     reviewable in a pull request like anything else -- and so that a
     corpus with no declarations at all still works.  The cost is that a
     corpus can drift from its declarations, which is why there is a
     lint.  See *note Checking a corpus::.
The action language became a vocabulary
     Tinderbox action code is a full expression language, with
     assignment, conditionals, iteration, user-defined functions, and
     ‘runCommand’ to reach outside the document altogether
     (‘tbxman920:2879--2882’).  org-agents' is nine verbs, parsed by a
     regexp lexer, resolved by name construction plus ‘fboundp’,
     receiving their arguments as strings, and never evaluated.  The
     reason is where the text comes from: a Tinderbox document is one
     file you opened deliberately, while an Org corpus is thousands of
     files, any of which may have arrived from somewhere else.  A
     language in a property is code from a file.  See *note Action
     code::.

   Three smaller changes, for completeness.  ‘set’ and ‘list’ members
are separated by whitespace rather than semicolons, because whitespace
is what Org's own ‘NAME_ALL’ convention uses and therefore what
‘org-property-get-allowed-values’ already reads; the semantics are
Tinderbox's exactly.  Tinderbox's boolean coercions -- "the number 0,
the empty string, and the date ‘never’ are considered to be false"
(‘tbxman920:1391--1392’) -- are not ported: here those are values that
fail to be booleans, and the lint says so, rather than values silently
read as false.  And a number may not carry an exponent, though Tinderbox
allows "either an integer or a floating point (decimal) number, positive
or negative" (‘tbxman920:1426’), because ‘1e3’ in an Org drawer is far
more likely to be a product code than a thousand.


