15.4 What was deliberately not ported, and why
==============================================

Some of Tinderbox's mechanisms were left out on purpose, and an honest
account says which, and why, rather than leaving a reader to conclude
they were merely missed.  Nothing in this section is a plan; each entry
is a decision.  The line number after each name is where the Tinderbox
v9.2 manual describes the thing that is not being ported.

‘$Rule’ (frequent timer), ‘tbxman920:2800’
     Code that edits files while you are not looking, in a corpus under
     version control, is a commit nobody authored.  Structurally
     impossible here: the action text is not on the save path at all.
‘$Edict’ (startup, then roughly hourly), ‘tbxman920:2801’, ‘2907’
     The same objection, on a slower clock.
‘$OnAdd’, ‘tbxman920:2798’
     It fires on an edit to the outline, which is the same objection
     again.
inheritable actions -- a ‘$Rule’ from a prototype, ‘tbxman920:2897’
     Per-file trust.  If an action could arrive from a prototype, an
     ancestor, a ‘#+PROPERTY:’ line or ‘org-global-properties’, then the
     code that edits your corpus when you act on file A is written in
     file B, and reading a file before trusting it means nothing.
‘$Width’, ‘$Height’, ‘$Xpos’, ‘$Ypos’, ‘tbxman920:1529’, ‘1562’
     They describe the map view.  Org has no map, so there is nothing in
     an outline for them to mean.  No spelling is even reserved.
the alias/original live proxy, and the ‘original’ designator, ‘tbxman920:1555--1557’, ‘1566--1567’
     An Org alias is a heading holding a link.  Org has no facility for
     a bidirectional proxy, and an alias has no attributes of its own to
     distinguish from the match's.
‘$PrototypeBequeathsChildren’ -- materialised child inheritance, ‘tbxman920:1730--1733’
     A copy made at assignment time silently stops tracking its source,
     which is the opposite of what virtual resolution is for -- and it
     would mean writing subtrees into files on the strength of one
     property.
‘$AgentPriority’, ‘tbxman920:2549’
     It exists to schedule a timer, and there is no timer.
the ‘Action’ attribute type, ‘tbxman920:1455’
     Its values are evaluated when used.  The registry is pure data; a
     type like this is precisely what could not be ported without giving
     that up.
the ‘color’, ‘file’, ‘interval’, ‘URL’ and ‘dictionary’ types, ‘tbxman920:1375--1456’
     Not implemented.  ‘URL’ in particular would be an Org link, which
     Org already has.

   Three of those deserve their reasoning spelled out, because they are
the ones a Tinderbox reader will miss most.

   *The timer.*  Tinderbox's ‘$Rule’ runs "at frequent intervals"
(‘tbxman920:2800’) and its ‘$Edict’ "at startup and then at intervals of
approximately one hour" (‘tbxman920:2907’).  Both are sound in a
document one application owns.  In a corpus of plain files under version
control they are not: a rule is a piece of code that edits your files
while you are not looking, and the result is a diff nobody wrote.  Here
the only thing that runs an action is ‘org-agents-apply-actions’, typed,
and the refusal is structural rather than disciplined -- the action text
is not part of the data the save path reads, so there is no hook it
could be reached from.  See *note The three refusals::, and *note
Updating::, which states the same absence from the other direction: as a
limitation of the updating feature rather than as an act of attribution.

   *Inheritable actions.*  A Tinderbox rule is normally inherited; the
manual's own worked example is a rule "inherited from the Task
prototype" (‘tbxman920:2897’).  Here ‘:AGENT_ACTION:’ is read from the
entry's own drawer, with no inheritance of any kind and never through
the prototype resolver.  Inheritance of behaviour and per-file trust
cannot both hold: if a master in one file could lend an action to a
follower in another, then reading a file before trusting it would tell
you nothing about what acting on it will do.

   *Materialised inheritance.*  Tinderbox can copy a prototype's
children into a note that adopts it, and says of the copies:

     Note that these "inherited" notes are created at the time the
     prototype is assigned; adding or removing children to the prototype
     at some later time will not affect notes that already use the
     prototype.

     -- _Children of Prototypes_, ‘tbxman920:1730--1731’

   A copy made at assignment time is a copy that has stopped tracking
its source, which is the opposite of what makes virtual resolution worth
having: that one change at the master reaches every follower.  It would
also mean this package writing whole subtrees into files on the strength
of a single property.

   Two absences are not divergences at all but agreements, and are
recorded here so that they are not mistaken for gaps.  Tinderbox's
"inheritance has nothing to do with the document hierarchy"
(‘tbxman920:1751’) is exactly this package's position, and ‘$NAME*’ and
‘$NAME^’ exist as two spellings because of it.  And Tinderbox's own
non-inherited set -- its intrinsic attributes, which "are never
inherited from prototypes" (‘tbxman920:1560’) -- has a recognisably
similar motive to this package's, which excludes ‘AGENT_*’ because
behaviour does not travel, ‘PROTOTYPE’ itself because a declared default
for it would hand the whole corpus one master, and Org's special
properties because a master's ‘CATEGORY’ is not yours.  The lists
differ; the instinct does not.


