15.5 Org and org-ql
===================

org-agents is a thin layer, and it is thin because of what it stands on.

   Org mode is Carsten Dominik's, maintained since by Bastien Guerry,
Ihor Radchenko and many others.  Everything this package treats as given
-- the outline, the property drawer, links, the agenda, dynamic blocks,
column view, tags, TODO keywords, planning lines, and the plain-text
format that makes all of it inspectable by other tools -- is Org's.  See
*note What Org already had:: for the parts of Tinderbox's model that
were already present, and therefore not written again here.

   org-ql is Adam Porter's.  It is the evaluation engine: every match
this package reports was found by org-ql in a live buffer, and the query
language documented in *note The query language:: is org-ql's with a
reference layer on top.  The one predicate org-agents defines,
‘property-resolved’, is an ‘org-ql-defpred’ and behaves like any other
org-ql predicate.  ‘org-agents-preview’ hands a query to
‘org-ql-search’.  Where this manual is thin about what a predicate
means, org-ql's own manual is the place to look.

   Two single-file modules from the author's Emacs configuration,
‘org-ql-ext’ and ‘org-ext’, are load-time dependencies; the
‘property-ts’ predicate that several examples here rely on comes from
the first of them.


